2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT Samuel Arnold
2 CONT
2 CONT Born	10 August 1740
2 CONT London, England
2 CONT Died	22 October 1802 (aged 62)
2 CONT Resting place	Westminster Abbey
2 CONT Occupation(s)	Composer, organist
2 CONT Notable work	Writing the earliest version of Humpty Dumpty
2 CONT Abimelech
2 CONT The Baron Kinkvervankotsdorsprakingatchdern
2 CONT Inkle and Yarico
2 CONT Father	Thomas Arnold
2 CONT Samuel Arnold (10 August 1740 – 22 October 1802) was an English composer and organist.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arnold was born in London (his mother is said to have been Princess Amelia; his father was Thomas Arnold).[1][2] He began writing music for the theatre in about the year 1764. A few years later, he became the director of music at 
2 CONC Marylebone Gardens, for which he wrote much of his popular music. In 1777, he worked for George Colman the Elder at the Little Theatre, Haymarket. In 1783, he became organist at the Chapel Royal and in 1793 he became the organist 
2 CONC at Westminster Abbey, where he was eventually buried. He also wrote the earliest version of Humpty Dumpty. He was a close friend and associate of Haydn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Works
2 CONT Arnold's best-known works include:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT "O Give Thanks" in the hand of Thomas Barrow
2 CONT The Maid of the Mill (1765)
2 CONT Abimelech (1768)
2 CONT The Prodigal Son (1773)
2 CONT Incidental music for Macbeth (1778)
2 CONT The Baron Kinkvervankotsdorsprakingatchdern (1781)
2 CONT The Castle of Andalusia (1782)
2 CONT Two to One (1784), libretto George Colman. Includes the song "Pensive I Mourn".[3]
2 CONT Turk and No Turk (1785)
2 CONT Inkle and Yarico (1787)
2 CONT Juvenile Amusements (1797)
2 CONT He is also known for producing the first collected edition of the works of George Frideric Handel between 1787 and 1797, published in 180 parts. This was the most comprehensive collection of Handel's music prior to the appearance 
2 CONC of the Händel-Gesellschaft edition in the next century.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT https://hymnary.org/person/Arnold_Samuel
2 CONT https://www.westminster-abbey.org/abbey-commemorations/commemorations/samuel-arnold
2 CONT https://www.classiccat.net/arnold_s/biography.php
