1 OCCU Landgreve i Hessen-Kassel, Kurfurste i Hessen 1803-06, 1813-21, German Electorial Duke of Hesse; famous "Soldier Trader"
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *Wilhelm von Hessen-Kassel.
2 CONT *Landgraf von Hessen-Kassel in 1785. As Wilhelm IX
2 CONT *Kurfürst Wilhelm IX von Hessen-Kassel in 1785. As Wilhelm IX 
2 CONT *Kurfürst Wilhelm I von Hessen on 15 May 1803. As Wilhelm I.
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_I,_Elector_of_Hesse
2 CONT
2 CONT William I, Elector of Hesse (German: Wilhelm I., Kurfürst von Hessen; 3 June 1743 – 27 February 1821) was the eldest surviving son of Frederick II, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel (or Hesse-Cassel) and Princess Mary of Great Britain, th
2 CONC e daughter of George II.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince William as a boy
2 CONT Prince William was born on 3 June 1743 in Kassel, capital of the Landgraviate of Hesse-Kassel in the Holy Roman Empire. Born into the House of Hesse, he was the second but eldest surviving son of Prince Frederick of Hesse-Kassel (
2 CONC the future Landgrave Frederick II) and his wife, Princess Mary of Great Britain. A former heir to the landgraviate, also named William, had died in infancy in 1742; therefore, hopes were high for the future of the new heir apparen
2 CONC t. He had two younger brothers: Prince Charles and Prince Frederick.
2 CONT
2 CONT His father's marriage with the British princess was not a happy one, and Frederick abandoned the family in 1747 and converted to Catholicism in 1749. In 1755 he formally annulled his marriage. William's grandfather, Landgrave Will
2 CONC iam, granted the newly acquired county of Hanau to his daughter-in-law and grandsons. Technically, young William became the reigning prince of Hanau, while under his mother's regency. The young prince William, together with his tw
2 CONC o younger brothers, lived with their mother, the landgravine Mary. From 1747 they were supported by Protestant relatives and moved to Denmark. There they lived with Mary's sister, Louise of Great Britain, and her family; Louise di
2 CONC ed in 1751.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT On 1 September 1764, William married his first cousin, Wilhelmina Caroline of Denmark and Norway (1747–1820), who was the second surviving daughter of Frederick V of Denmark and Norway. They married at Christiansborg Palace and re
2 CONC sided for two decades mostly in Denmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1785 they moved to Kassel when William succeeded to the landgraviate. During the lifetime of his father, William had already received the Principality of Hanau, south of the Hessian territories near Frankfurt, as successor of i
2 CONC ts newly extinct princes. The Hanau people did not want to have a Catholic ruler.
2 CONT
2 CONT William's younger brother Charles in 1766 married another of their Danish first cousins, Princess Louise of Denmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign
2 CONT Upon the death of his father on 31 October 1785, he became William IX, Landgrave of Hesse-Kassel. He was said to have inherited one of the largest fortunes in Europe at the time.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT William looked for help in managing his estate. He hired Mayer Amschel Rothschild as "Hoffaktor" in 1769,[1] to supervise the operation of his properties and tax-gathering. The wealth of William's estate provided a good living for
2 CONC  Rothschild and the men had a strong relationship; he founded the Rothschild family dynasty, which became important in financing and banking in Europe. Although they had been acquainted since 1775, William IX did not formally desi
2 CONC gnate Rothschild as his overseer until 1801.
2 CONT
2 CONT The early fortunes of the Rothschild family were made through a conjunction of financial intelligence and the wealth of Prince William. During the Napoleonic Wars, William used the Frankfurt Rothschilds to hide his fortune from Na
2 CONC poleon. This money then saw its way through to Nathan Mayer, (N.M.) in London, where it helped fund the British movements through Portugal and Spain. The interest made from this venture was reaped by the budding banker barons, who
2 CONC  used it to swiftly develop their fortune and prestige in Europe and Britain. It was not long before their riches outweighed those of their benefactor, William of Hesse-Kassel.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1803, Landgrave William was created His Royal and Serene Highness The Prince-Elector of Hesse.[2] In 1807 his electorate was annexed by the Kingdom of Westphalia, ruled by Jérôme Bonaparte, Napoleon's brother. William escaped t
2 CONC o Denmark with his family and lived there in exile until the French were expelled from Germany. Following the defeat of the Napoleonic armies in the Battle of Leipzig, William was restored in 1813.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the restoration of the Electorate, he started with building a monumental palace in Kassel, the Chattenburg. Construction was halted after his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a member of the Tugendbund, a quasi-Masonic secret society founded after the Battle of Jena–Auerstedt in June 1808 at Koningsberg.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Several other prince-electors of the Holy Roman Empire had been recognized as kings at the Congress of Vienna (1815), and William attempted to join them by declaring himself King of the Chatti. However, the European powers refused
2 CONC  to recognize this title at the Congress of Aix-la-Chapelle (1818) and instead granted him the grand ducal style of "Royal Highness."[4] Deeming the title of Prince-Elector to be superior in dignity to that of Grand Duke, William 
2 CONC chose to remain an Elector, even though there was no longer a Holy Roman Emperor to elect. Hesse-Kassel would remain an Electorate until it was annexed by Prussia in 1866.
2 CONT
2 CONT He ruled until his death in Kassel in 1821. He was succeeded by his son William.
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Wilhelm I, his wife, Wilhelmine Caroline and their surviving children: Wilhelm, Marie Friederike and Karoline Amalie
2 CONT With his wife Wilhelmina Caroline of Denmark and Norway, William had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie Friederike (14 September 1768 – 17 April 1839), married Alexius Frederick Christian, Duke of Anhalt-Bernburg on 29 November 1794, divorced 1817
2 CONT Karoline Amalie (11 July 1771 – 22 February 1848), married Augustus, Duke of Saxe-Gotha-Altenburg on 24 April 1802. No issue
2 CONT Friedrich (8 August 1772 – 20 July 1784), died in childhood
2 CONT Wilhelm (28 July 1777 – 20 November 1847), his successor
2 CONT William had several mistresses and fathered over twenty recognized illegitimate children, providing some financial means to each of them.
2 CONT
2 CONT With his first official mistress, Charlotte Christine Buissine (1749-?), William had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Wilhelm von Heimrod (16 July 1775 – 6 January 1811)
2 CONT Karl von Heimrod (19 July 1776 – 13 May 1827), married Charlotte von Stockhausen (1781–1855) in 1803; they had seven children.
2 CONT Friedrich von Heimrod (9 August - 30 October 1777), died in infancy
2 CONT Friedrich von Heimrod (1778 – 3 September 1813); he had at least one son.
2 CONT With his second official mistress, Rosa Dorothea Ritter (1759–1833), William had eight children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Wilhelm Karl von Haynau (24 December 1779 – 21 January 1856), married firstly Karoline von Schack (died 1807) in 1803; they had two children. Married secondly Luise Sophie Buderus von Carlshausen (1787–1813) in 1808; they had two 
2 CONC children. Married thirdly Sophie Friederike von Lengerke (1798–1820) in 1818; no known children survived to adulthood. Married fourthly Elisabeth Freiin von Trott zu Solz (1793–1844) in 1822; they had three daughters.
2 CONT Georg Wilhelm von Haynau (27 February 1781 – February 1813), married Charlotte Sophie von Wildungen (1782–1858) in 1808; they had 3 children.
2 CONT Philipp Ludwig von Haynau (18 May 1782 – 5 June 1843), married Wilhelmine von Zeppelin (1791–1872) in 1821; they had two children.
2 CONT Wilhelmine von Haynau (20 July 1783 – 27 May 1866), married Karl Frhr von Hanstein (1771–1861) in 1801; they had nine children.
2 CONT Moritz von Haynau (4 July 1784 – 9 September 1812), married Anna Auguste von Wurmb (1789–1872) in 1809; they had two daughters.
2 CONT Marie Sophie Agnes Philippine Auguste von Haynau (11 September 1785 – 21 April 1865), married Wilhelm Freiherr von Wintzingerode (1782–1819) in 1805; they had one son.
2 CONT Julius Jacob von Haynau (14 October 1786 – 14 March 1853), married Theresia Weber von Treuenfels (1787–1851) in 1808; they had one daughter.
2 CONT Otto of Hanau (12 June 1788 – before 24 May 1792), died in childhood
2 CONT With his third and final official mistress, Karoline von Schlotheim (1766–1847), William had 13 children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Wilhelm Friedrich von Hessenstein (23 June 1789 – 26 April 1790), died in infancy
2 CONT Wilhelm Karl von Hessenstein (19 May 1790 – 22 March 1867), married Angelika von Osten-Sacken (1802–1852) in 1820; they had one daughter.
2 CONT Ferdinand von Hessenstein (19 May 1791 – 15 December 1794), died in childhood
2 CONT Karoline Frederike Auguste von Hessenstein (9 June 1792 – 21 August 1797), died in childhood
2 CONT Auguste Wilhelmine von Hessenstein (22 August 1793 – 1 June 1795), died in childhood
2 CONT Ludwig Karl von Hessenstein (11 August 1794 – 17 November 1857), married Auguste von Pückler (21 September 1794 - 8 November 1861).
2 CONT Friederike von Hessenstein (16 October 1795 – 13 September 1845), married Wilhelm von Steuber (Kassel, 29 December 1790 - Kassel, 6 July 1845) in 1824; they had three children.
2 CONT Wilhelm Ludwig Georg von Hessenstein (28 July 1800 – 16 January 1836), married Luise von dem Bussche-Hünnefeld (27 March 1804 - 21 May 1829) in 1827; they had one son. Married secondly Karoline Wolff von Gudenburg (11 February 181
2 CONC 2 – 20 August 1836) in 1831; they had two sons.
2 CONT Friedrich Ludwig von Hessenstein (8 February 1803 – 8 September 1805), died in childhood
2 CONT Karoline von Hessenstein (16 February 1804 – 18 March 1891), married Karl von Stenglin (12 August 1791 - 15 March 1871) in 1822; they had six children.
2 CONT stillborn child (1805)
2 CONT stillborn child (1806)
2 CONT stillborn son (1807 in Itzehoe)
2 CONT From a relationship with Marianne Wulffen, Johann Heinrich Resse was born (1760, Kassel, Hessen), father of the renowned Portuguese-German engineer, Colonel João Guilherme Resse.[5]
2 CONT
