2 TITL Gustav III of Sweden - Wikidata
1 NOTE When Gustav succeeded his father, the power of the monarchy had been 
2 CONT greatly curtailed, and the national assembly, the Riksdag, was torn by 
2 CONT intense party strife. After failing to reconcile the divided factions, 
2 CONT Gustav decided to regain complete control of the government for the 
2 CONT monarchy. With the help of the army he staged a mock revolt in 1772 and 
2 CONT forced the Riksdag to accept a new constitution that gave him absolute 
2 CONT power. He then instituted a series of financial and judicial reforms to 
2 CONT correct corruption in high office, grant freedom of the press and complete 
2 CONT religious toleration, and enlarge the navy, making it one of the strongest 
2 CONT in Europe. In 1788 he undertook an inconclusive war with Russia that was 
2 CONT marked by treason among the nobility at home and mutiny among his troops. 
2 CONT A Swedish naval victory in 1790, however, destroyed a third of the Russian 
2 CONT fleet and enabled him to end the war on terms favorable to Sweden. While 
2 CONT preparing to intervene in the French Revolution in 1792, Gustav was 
2 CONT assassinated in a plot conceived by hostile nobles. A patron of 
2 CONT literature, art, and science, he founded the Swedish Academy in 1786.
1 NOTE "OF SWEDEN"
