1 NOTE Frederick William II, son of Augustus Hohenzollern, came to rule Prussia following the death of Frederick II the Great in 1786. Frederick William II ruled until 1797.  He was succeeded by his son Frederick William III.
1 NOTE Frederick William II (1744-97), king of Prussia (1786-97), grandson of 
2 CONT Frederick William I and nephew of Frederick II, born in Berlin. He 
2 CONT succeeded to the throne in 1786 upon the death of his uncle. In 1792 he 
2 CONT made an alliance with Leopold II, Holy Roman emperor, to support Louis XVI 
2 CONT of France in the French Revolution. As a result of Frederick's 
2 CONT participation in the ensuing wars, he was forced in 1795 by the Treaty of 
2 CONT Basel to cede to France Prussian territories west of the Rhine River. He 
2 CONT secured territory from Poland, however, by participating in the Polish 
2 CONT partitions of 1793 and 1795. Influenced during his reign by the 
2 CONT Rosicrucian order, of which he was a member, he suppressed the ideas of 
2 CONT the Enlightenment, imposing censorship on religion, education, and the 
2 CONT press. He lacked interest in military affairs and allowed a supreme 
2 CONT college of war to supervise the army, which declined markedly during his 
2 CONT reign. Through his own ineptitude and that of the favorites he appointed 
2 CONT to administrative positions, the treasury of Prussia was bankrupted and 
2 CONT the reputation of the country diminished by the end of his reign.
1 NOTE KING OF PRUSSIA
