1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julius_Jacob_von_Haynau
2 CONT
2 CONT Julius Jacob von Haynau (October 14, 1786 – March 14, 1853) was an Austrian general who was prominent in suppressing insurrectionary movements in Italy and Hungary in 1848 and later. His soldiers called him the "Habsburg Tiger"; t
2 CONC hose opponents who suffered from his brutality called him the "Hyena of Brescia" and the "Hangman of Arad."
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life and education
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Kassel, Julius Jacob von Haynau was the illegitimate son of Rosa Dorothea Ritter, and William I (1743-1821), the landgrave (later elector) of Hesse-Kassel. He was born after his father's return with his wife and family to 
2 CONC Hesse-Kassel after 20 years in Denmark. His father acknowledged this natural son, providing for his education and entry into the military officer corps as a cadet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and family
2 CONT
2 CONT After several years of service in the Army, at nearly 22 years of age, on October 11, 1808 Haynau married Thérèse von Weber. She was the daughter of Field Marshal Lieutenant Weber. Her father was killed in action the next year at 
2 CONC the Battle of Aspern-Essling during the Napoleonic Wars.
2 CONT
2 CONT William and Thérèse had more than four decades of marriage together before her death in 1850. She was survived by her husband and one daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Military career
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1801, Haynau entered the Austrian army as an infantry officer cadet at the age of 15. He gained extensive experience as a soldier during the Napoleonic Wars. He was wounded at Wagram in 1809, which the French won in one of thei
2 CONC r most important battles of the wars.
2 CONT
2 CONT Haynau later distinguished himself during the Austrian operations in Italy in 1813 and 1814. Between 1815 and 1847, Haynau was promoted several times, reaching the rank of field marshal lieutenant.
2 CONT
2 CONT Role in the revolutions
2 CONT
2 CONT Haynau was said to have a violent temper, which led him into trouble with his superiors. His support for the monarchy led him to fiercely oppose the revolutionary movements of the mid-nineteenth century.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the revolutionary insurrections of 1848 broke out in Italy, Haynau was selected to command troops to suppress them. He fought with success in Italy. He became known in this period for the severity with which he suppressed an 
2 CONC uprising in Brescia and punished participants. A mob in Brescia had massacred invalid Austrian soldiers in the hospital, and von Haynau ordered reprisals. Numerous attackers were executed.
2 CONT
2 CONT In June 1849, Haynau was called to Vienna to command a reserve army; he was ordered into the field against the Hungarians during their revolution. The entry of the Russians against the Hungarians contributed to the end of the revo
2 CONC lution, and they turned over the army to the Austrians.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Hungary as in Italy, Haynau was accused of brutality. For instance, he was said to have ordered women whipped who were suspected of sympathizing with the insurgents. He also ordered the execution by hanging of the 13 Hungarian 
2 CONC rebel generals at Arad on October 6, 1849.
2 CONT
2 CONT Opponents called him the "Hyena of Brescia" and "Hangman of Arad." In admiration, Austrian soldiers referred to him as the "Habsburg Tiger."
2 CONT
2 CONT Later years
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon the restoration of peace, Haynau was appointed to high command in Hungary. His temper quickly led him into quarrels with the minister of war, and he resigned his command in 1850. In later years, he travelled abroad, including
2 CONC  to western Europe and England.
2 CONT
2 CONT His reputation for brutality had spread throughout western Europe. In Brussels, Haynau narrowly escaped mob violence. In London, he was attacked and beaten by two draymen during a visit to what was then the Barclay & Perkins brewe
2 CONC ry. When the Italian revolutionary, Giuseppe Garibaldi, visited England in 1864, he insisted on visiting the brewery to thank "the men who flogged Haynau."
2 CONT
2 CONT References in popular culture
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1862, during the American Civil War, the Union General Benjamin Butler commanded federal forces occupying New Orleans, Louisiana. They struggled with daily insults from the residents. He ordered that women showing disrespect to
2 CONC ward the Union officers were to be treated as common prostitutes. The Confederate General P. G. T. Beauregard referred to Butler as "the Haynau of the North" for his order. Beauregard did not explain his allusion, believing that h
2 CONC is officers were familiar with Haynau's reputation.
2 CONT
2 CONT G. K. Chesterton, the noted English author, later described the event in London in his book, The Crimes of England (1916), published during World War I, when Great Britain was at war with Germany:
2 CONT
2 CONT "When an Austrian general who had flogged women in the conquered provinces appeared in the London streets, some common draymen off a cart behaved with the direct quixotry of Sir Lancelot or Sir Galahad. He had beaten women and the
2 CONC y beat him. They regarded themselves simply as avengers of ladies in distress, breaking the bloody whip of a German bully."
2 CONT -------------------------
