1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Princess Elisa Radziwill (28 October 1803 – 27 September 1834) was the first love of Kaiser Wilhelm I. 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Biography
2 CONT Eliza was the fifth child in the family of Prince Anton Radziwiłłłł and his wife Louise Friederike of Prussia. 
2 CONT Eliza received a good education, is gifted musically and artistically. 
2 CONT Élise and Prince Wilhelm, who was five years her senior, knew each other from early childhood, as Elise's parents often visited the Palace of the Crown Princes, the residence of the Prussian royal couple Frederick William III and 
2 CONC Louise. 12-year-old Eliza and 18-year-old Wilhelm danced at a ball in 1815, and then feelings flared up. On January 27, 1821, they performed together at a performance of the Palace Theater, where Elise, who played the title role, 
2 CONC aroused everyone's admiration.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was described as the most beautiful lady of the Prussian court, called "the angel" and the "white rose".
2 CONT The dramatic history of Wilhelm and Eliza's matrimonial plans remained a topic of conversation at the courts of Europe from 1820 to 1826. Prince Wilhelm was second in line to the Prussian throne after his brother Frederick William
2 CONC , and when choosing a wife, he was bound by the obligation to marry an equal.
2 CONT
2 CONT In this regard, Eliza's parents and Frederick William III stocked up on numerous documents testifying to the Radziwills' kinship with several ruling houses. But according to German (and only) rules, this was not enough. The Radziw
2 CONC ills, who were elevated to the rank of imperial princes in the 14th century, were never members of the imperial estates, that is, they were not represented in the council of imperial princes and, thus, were not included in the Rei
2 CONC chstag. Therefore, they were not mediatized and, accordingly, were not recognized as sovereigns.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1824, King Frederick William III asked the childless Russian Emperor Alexander I to adopt Eliza, but he refused. A second attempt to arrange the adoption of Eliza by her uncle, Prince Augustus of Prussia, also failed, as the re
2 CONC sponsible commission decided that the adoption would "not change the blood" (The position is very strange, rather politically motivated, since Barbara Radziwił was also married to the King of Poland and the Grand Duke of Lithuania
2 CONC , Russia and Žamoitia, Sigismund II Augustus). According to some other sources, this marriage had other opponents, namely the Mecklenburg relatives of the late Queen Louise of Mecklenburg-Strelitz, who had great influence on the c
2 CONC ourts of Berlin and St. Petersburg and did not like Elise's father. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Finally, on June 22, 1826, the king was forced to demand that his son renounce the marriage. Wilhelm obeyed. The last time he saw Eliza was in 1829. Elisa later became engaged to Prince Frederick of Schwarzenberg, but this arrange
2 CONC ment was also dissolved.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1822 to 1830, the Radziwills rarely visited Berlin and lived mainly in Poznań, Antonín and the Krkonoše Mountains. In 1831, Élise contracted tuberculosis and died while being treated in Bad Freienwald in 1834. In 1838, her re
2 CONC mains were transferred to Antonín to the new mausoleum of the Radziwills.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kaiser Wilhelm remembered Elisa for the rest of his life. On his desk in Berlin's palace on Unter den Linden there was always a miniature portrait of Elise.
2 CONT The love story of Wilhelm and Elise was adapted into a film in 1938, the role of Eliza Radziwił in Prussian Love Story was played by Lida Baarova. After the outbreak of World War II and the German invasion of Poland, the film was 
2 CONC banned from screening, and the love affair between a Prussian prince and a Polish princess seemed to Nazi propaganda to be out of step with the political situation. After the war, the film was shown in West German cinemas under th
2 CONC e title ''Love Story''. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Links:==
2 CONT *[http://www.agnessecretprincess.com/ Private website concerning Eliza and her decedants]
