2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lord_Frederick_FitzClarence Wikipedia: Lord Frederick FitzClarence]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Lieutenant-General Lord Frederick FitzClarence, GCH (Monday, 9 December 1799 – Monday, 30 October 1854) was a British Army officer and the third illegitimate son of King William IV by his mistress Dorothea Jordan.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Military career''
2 CONT
2 CONT FitzClarence was commissioned as an officer in the British Army in 1814. While a captain in the Coldstream Guards, FitzClarence commanded a small detachment of Guards to act in support of the police with the arrest of the Cato Str
2 CONC eet conspirators in 1820. The arrest was not straightforward, and a scuffle ensued.
2 CONT
2 CONT Frederick FitzClarence gained the rank of Colonel in the service of the 36th (Herefordshire) Regiment of Foot. On Tuesday, 24 May 1831, he was granted the rank of a marquess' younger son by his father, William IV, upon the latter'
2 CONC s ascension to the throne. Having been invested as a Knight Grand Cross of the Royal Guelphic Order (G.C.H.) that same year, he became Lieutenant-Governor of Portsmouth and General Officer Commanding South-West District in 1847, a
2 CONC nd then Commander-in-Chief of the Bombay Army in 1852. He died in office in October 1854.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Coat of arms''
2 CONT
2 CONT The coat of arms of Lord Frederick FitzClarence were the royal arms of King William IV (without the escutcheon of the Arch Treasurer of the Holy Roman Empire and without the Crown of Hanover) debruised by a baton sinister (azure(?
2 CONC )) charged with two anchors (or(?)).
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Family''
2 CONT
2 CONT On Saturday, 19 May 1821, he married Lady Augusta Boyle (died Friday, 28 July 1876), the eldest daughter of the 4th Earl of Glasgow. They had two children:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Augusta Georgiana Frederica FitzClarence (December 1823 – Tuesday, 18 September 1855)
2 CONT * William FitzClarence (born & died 1827)
