1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''From [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elizabeth_Hay,_Countess_of_Erroll Wikipedia: Elizabeth Hay, Countess of Erroll]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Hay, Countess of Erroll (née FitzClarence; Saturday, 17 January 1801 – Wednesday, 16 January 1856) was an illegitimate daughter of King William IV of the United Kingdom and Dorothea Jordan. She married William Hay, 18th 
2 CONC Earl of Erroll, and became Countess of Erroll on Monday, 4 December 1820 at age 19. Due to Hay's parentage, William Hay became Lord Steward of the Household. Elizabeth and William Hay married at St George's, Hanover Square. Hay is
2 CONC  pictured in a FitzClarence family portrait in House of Dun, and kept a stone thrown at her father William IV and the gloves he wore on opening his first Parliament as mementos.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1856, while ill herself, she was summoned from Scotland to visit her dying brother Adolphus. Her illness worsened and she died on the journey in Edinburgh, Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Children and descendants''
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth and William Hay together had four children.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Lady Ida Harriet Augusta Hay (Thursday, 18 October 1821 – Tuesday, 22 October 1867), was one of Queen Victoria's bridesmaids, was the Hays' firstborn child and daughter. She married Charles Noel, 2nd Earl of Gainsborough and had
2 CONC  five children. Her descendants include the Earls of Gainsborough, the Marquesses of Bute and the Baronets of Bellingham.
2 CONT * William Hay, 19th Earl of Erroll (Saturday, 3 May 1823 – Thursday, 3 December 1891), wed to Eliza Amelia Gore on Wednesday, 20 September 1848, was the second child and firstborn son.
2 CONT * Lady Agnes Georgiana Elizabeth Hay (Tuesday, 12 May 1829 – Saturday, 18 December 1869), wed to James Duff on Monday, 16 March 1846, was the third child and second daughter. Lady Agnes Hay's son, Alexander Duff, married Princess 
2 CONC Louise, daughter of King Edward VII.
2 CONT * Lady Alice Mary Emily Hay (Tuesday, 7 July 1835 – Tuesday, 7 June 1881), wed to Charles Edward Louis Casimir Stuart (1824–1882; known also popularly as Count died'Albanie) nephew of fraud John Sobieski Stuart, was the final chil
2 CONC d and daughter of the Hays.
2 CONT
2 CONT Former British Prime Minister David Cameron is a fourth great-grandson of Lady Erroll, thus making him the fifth cousin twice removed to Queen Elizabeth II according to Debrett's.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000082/18010122/007/0003 British Newspaper Archive: Morning Chronicle Thursday, 22 January 1801 Page 3 Birth]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT On Saturday last, 17 January 1801, at [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bushy_House Bushy House, Bushy Road, Teddington, London TW11 0EQ 51.42028, -0.33917], Mrs. Jordan, of a Daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000950/18010203/015/0003 British Newspaper Archive: Cumberland Pacquet and Ware's Whitehaven Advertiser Tuesday, 3 February 1801 Page 3 Births]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT At Bushy Park, Mrs. Jordan of a Daughter
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000060/18560121/015/0005 British Newspaper Archive: Glasgow Herald Monday, 21 January 1856 Page 5 Deaths]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Deaths: At [The British Hotel, now Yotel Edinburgh, 68-73 Queen Street, Edinburgh EH2 4NA 55.9534547, -3.2059383], on Wednesday, 16 January 1856 [instant], Elizabeth, Dowager Countess of Erroll.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000296/18560122/010/0002 British Newspaper Archive: Dundee, Perth, and Cupar Advertiser Tuesday, 22 January 1856 Page 2 The Late Countess Dowager of Errol]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The Late Countess Dowager of Errol. — The deceased Countess was third daughter of the late King William IV. and Mrs Jordan, and was born [ERROR 18 CORRECTION 17] January 1801. Her Ladyship married, Monday, 4 December 1820, William
2 CONC  George [Hay], sixteenth Earl of Errol, who died in April 1846, and by whom her Ladyship leaves issue, the present Earl of Errol, Lady Campden, Lady Agnes Duff, and Lady Alice Hay, unmarried. She had been spending the Christmas Ho
2 CONC lidays joyously at Skene House [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Provost_Skene%27s_House Provost Skene's House, Marischal Square, Guestrow, Aberdeen AB10 1AS 57.1483, -2.0975], in the company of her daughter, Lady Agnes Duff, and oth
2 CONC er members of her family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Towards the end of her visit, she became unwell, but not seriously as to prevent her undertaking journey southward, to attend the sick bed of her brother, Lord Adolphus Fitzclarence. She proceeded to [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki
2 CONC /Wemyss_Castle Wemyss Castle, East Wemyss, Fife KY1 4TE 56.1442, -3.0808] on Friday, 11 January 1856, and arrived in Edinburgh on Saturday last, 12 January 1856. Her Ladyship was there attended by Professor Simpson, who apprehende
2 CONC d no danger till Sunday, 13 January 1856.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fearing then that all might not go well, he telegraphed immediately to Lord Errol, Lady Agnes Duff, Lady Campden, and Lady Mary Fox, who all arrived in time to see the Countess alive, and perfectly conscious of their presence. At 
2 CONC half-past ten on Wednesday, 16 January 1856, suffering from congestion of the brain, she sunk into gentle sleep, and in rather less than half-an-hour, and without effort or struggle, she breathed her last, surrounded all her child
2 CONC ren, and Mr Duff her son-in-law. She would have completed her fifty-fifth year on Thursday, 17 January 1856.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Aberdeen Herald says:— "To all who had the privilege or honour of meeting her in society, she exhibited the highest degree of affability and unaffected courtesy; to her neighbours in the country she was endeared kind and homel
2 CONC y intercourse she kept up with them; and to the poor she was liberal, open-handed benefactress. It will be long ere in this part the country we forget the pious, amiable, and benevolent Countess of Errol.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://thepeerage.com/p10508.htm#i105079 The Peerage: Lady Elizabeth Fitz-Clarence]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Elizabeth Fitz-Clarence was born illegitimately on Saturday, 17 January 1801 at [ERROR Busby House, Busby, Lanarkshire, Scotland CORRECTION Bushy House, Teddington, London]. She was the daughter of William IV Hanover, King of
2 CONC  the United Kingdom and Dorothea Bland. She married William George Hay, 18th Earl of Erroll, son of William Hay, 17th Earl of Erroll and Alicia Eliot, on Monday, 4 December 1820 at London, England. She died on Wednesday, 16 Januar
2 CONC y 1856 at age 54 at Edinburgh, Midlothian, Scotland. From Monday, 4 December 1820, her married name became Hay. After her marriage, Lady Elizabeth Fitz-Clarence was styled as Countess of Erroll on Monday, 4 December 1820. In 1831 
2 CONC she was granted the rank of a daughter of a Marquess.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Lady Elizabeth Fitz-Clarence and William George Hay, 18th Earl of Erroll
2 CONT * Lady Alice Mary Emily Hay died Tuesday, 7 June 1881
2 CONT * Lady Ida Harriet Augusta Hay born 1821, died Tuesday, 22 October 1867
2 CONT * Major William Harry Hay, 19th Earl of Erroll born Saturday, 3 May 1823, died Thursday, 3 December 1891
2 CONT * Lady Agnes Georgiana Elizabeth Hay born Tuesday, 12 May 1829, died Saturday, 18 December 1869
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
