1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lord_Augustus_FitzClarence
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord Augustus FitzClarence (1 March 1805 – 14 June 1854), was the youngest illegitimate son of William IV of the United Kingdom and his long-time mistress Dorothea Jordan. Like his siblings, he had little contact with his mother a
2 CONC fter his parents separated in 1811.
2 CONT
2 CONT Career
2 CONT In 1829 Augustus was appointed a Chaplain of his father (then Duke of Clarence and St Andrews)[1] and later that year he was presented with the vicarage of Mapledurham in Oxfordshire, succeeding John Sumner (later Bishop of Cheste
2 CONC r and Archbishop of Canterbury).[2] King William IV was a lavish benefactor of the church and the parish and, among his gifts was the clock in the tower which bears his initials, ″W R.″; he also made generous contributions to exte
2 CONC nd the vicarage and to enclose its adjacent grounds with substantial provisions for the foundation of a new school in the village. The marvellous collection of silver gilt communion plate presented to the church by Lord Augustus s
2 CONC hortly after his appointment was probably in turn a gift from his father.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1830 he was appointed Chaplain in ordinary to his father (now King William IV),[1] and on 24 May 1831 was granted the rank of a marquess' younger son, being appointed Chaplain to Queen Adelaide after his father's accession to t
2 CONC he throne in 1832. Following his studies at Trinity College, Cambridge, he received the degrees of LL.B. (2 June 1832) and LL.D. (6 July 1835).[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and issue
2 CONT On 2 January 1845 he married Sarah Elizabeth Catharine Gordon, a daughter of Maj. Lord Henry Gordon (a younger brother of Charles Gordon, 10th Marquess of Huntly and a relative of the husband of his sister Lady Augusta Gordon) by 
2 CONC his wife Louisa Payne. By his wife he had six children:[4][better source needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT Dorothea FitzClarence (1845–1870), who married Thomas William Goff, MP for Roscommon, and had issue.[5][6]
2 CONT Eva FitzClarence (1847–1918).
2 CONT Beatrice FitzClarence (1847–1909), a twin with Eva.
2 CONT Augustus FitzClarence (1849–1861), who died young.
2 CONT Henry Edward FitzClarence (1853–1930), who married Mary Isabel Templer Parsons and had issue.
2 CONT Mary FitzClarence (1854–1858), who was born posthumously and who died in infancy.
2 CONT Death
2 CONT FitzClarence died on 14 June 1854. He was buried at St Margaret's Church, Mapledurham.[7] His widow lived until 23 March 1901.
2 CONT
