2 TITL Willem Frederik George Lodewijk  der Nederlanden | WieWasWie | BS Huwelijk | 24651544
1 NOTE (in Dutch, Willem Frederik George Lodewijk)  He was also the grand duke of 
2 CONT Luxembourg (1840-49).  He was educated at the University of Oxford. When 
2 CONT the French overran the Netherlands in 1795, William and his family were 
2 CONT exiled for 18 years. As William, prince of Orange, he entered the British 
2 CONT army in 1811 during the Napoleonic Wars and served as aide-de-camp to 
2 CONT General Arthur Wellesley, later 1st duke of Wellington; William commanded 
2 CONT the Dutch and Belgian forces at the Battle of Waterloo in 1815. That year, 
2 CONT his father became king of the Netherlands and Belgium. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When the Belgians rebelled against Dutch rule in 1830, William advocated 
2 CONT greater autonomy for them, but the king rejected his proposed concessions. 
2 CONT The following year, Dutch forces under the younger William's command 
2 CONT subdued the Belgians. In 1832, however, France intervened on behalf of the 
2 CONT Belgians, forcing William to withdraw, and in 1839 Belgium was granted 
2 CONT independence. William succeeded to the throne upon the abdication of his 
2 CONT father in 1840. The chief event of his reign was the grant in 1848 of a 
2 CONT liberalized constitution that greatly abridged royal power. He was 
2 CONT succeeded by his son William III.
