1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ernst_von_Wildenbruch
2 CONT
2 CONT Ernst von Wildenbruch (February 3, 1845, Beirut – January 15, 1909, Berlin) was a German poet and dramatist.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Wildenbruch was born at Beirut in Lebanon, the son of the Prussian consul-general, Ludwig von Wildenbruch. Having passed his early years at Athens and Constantinople, where his father was attached to the Prussian legation, he came
2 CONC  in 1857 to the Kingdom of Prussia, received his early schooling at the Padagogium at Halle and the Französische Gymnasium in Berlin, and, after passing through the cadet school, became, in 1863, an officer in the Prussian Army. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Two years later Wildenbruch abandoned his military career, but was recalled to the colors in 1866 for the Austro-Prussian War. He next studied law at the University of Berlin, and again served in the army during the Franco-Prussia
2 CONC n War (1870–71). 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1876 Wildenbruch was attached to the foreign office, which he finally quit in 1900 with the title of counsellor of legation. He achieved his first literary successes with the epics Vionville (1874) and Sedan (1875). After publi
2 CONC shing a volume of poems, Lieder und Balladen (1877), he produced, in 1882, the tragedy Die Karolinger. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Wildenbruch married Maria Karoline von Weber, daughter of the engineer Max Maria von Weber and niece to Carl Maria von Weber, in 1885. The couple had no children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wildenbruch's wife, Maria Karoline von Wildenbruch, née Baroness von Weber, 1875
2 CONT
2 CONT Among Wildenbruch's chief dramas may be mentioned the tragedy Harold (1882); Die Quitzows (1888); Der Generalfeldoberst (1889); Die Haubenlerche (1891); Heinrich und Heinrichs Geschlecht (1895); Die Tochter des Erasmus (1900); and
2 CONC  König Laurin (1902). He also wrote several volumes of short stories (Novellen, 1883; Neue Novellen, 1885; Tiefe Wasser, 1897, and others).
2 CONT
2 CONT Wildenbruch was twice (in 1884 and 1896) awarded the Schiller Prize, and was, in 1892, created a doctor of philosophy honoris causa by the university of Jena. 
