2 CONT * burial: Cruden, Aberdeenshire, Scotland
2 CONT * source: [https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/6MWH-QJ6 FamilySearch]
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT William Henry Hay,19th Earl of Erroll, was in Canada for a time ,circa 1844-1846 and was stationed in Annapolis Royal, Nova Scotia . In 1848 he was an A.D.C. on the staff of the Earl of Elgin, Governor General of Canada. In 1854 h
2 CONC e was wounded at Alma in the Crimea. He gained the rank of Major in the Rifle Brigade. His father William Hay, 18th Earl of Erroll established the fishing community of Port Erroll in the 1840's & 1850's on the sea coast in Aberdee
2 CONC nshire. A functional harbour at the mouth of the Water of Cruden village was added in the 1870's, possibly by his son the 19th Earl of Erroll as he was credited with being the founder of Port Erroll. The Presbyterian Church dedica
2 CONC ted to St. Olaf or commonly called the Old Kirk built in 1776 with distinctive conical towers added in 1833 and St James' Episcopal Church, notably the first structure designed by architect William Hay, 18th Earl of Erroll ?? were
2 CONC  on top of Chapel Hill in 1842.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Hay-1074
