2 CONT https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Therese_von_Sachsen-Altenburg_(1823–1915)
2 CONT
2 CONT Henriette Friederike Therese Elisabeth of Saxe-Altenburg (9 October 1823 – †3 April 1915) was a princess of Saxe-Altenburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT Therese was the second of the four surviving daughters of Duke Joseph of Saxe-Altenburg (1789–1868) from his marriage to Amalie (1799–1848), daughter of Duke Ludwig of Württemberg. She thus belonged to the House of Saxe-Hildburgha
2 CONC usen, which was renamed the House of Saxe-Altenburg in 1826. Her godparents were, in addition to her grandfather Ludwig, Prince Alexander and Princess Elisabeth of Württemberg. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Together with her sisters Marie, Elisabeth and Alexandra, she was trained by Carl Ludwig Nietzsche. [2] Later, Therese supported his son Friedrich Nietzsche and his family financially. [3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Therese, although described as the most attractive of the sisters, never married. Among her candidates was the French Emperor Napoleon III. Therese first took care of her seriously ill mother and then cared for her father until hi
2 CONC s death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Until her death, the princess lived in Wolfersdorf Castle over the summer, which could be kept in good condition through constant use and regained importance for the Altenburg court. In 1894, Kaiser Wilhelm II was a guest in the c
2 CONC astle during a hunt. Therese was a Dame of the Bavarian Order of Theresa.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Princess_Therese_of_Saxe-Altenburg_
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/219441338/therese-von_sachsen-altenburg
