2 CONT Princess Marie of the Netherlands was the fourth child and younger daughter of Prince Frederick of the Netherlands and wife of William, 5th Prince of Wied. She was the mother of William, Prince of Albania. She was the last survivi
2 CONC ng grandchild of William I of the Netherlands.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie was born at Wassenaar, Netherlands the fourth child and younger daughter of Prince Frederick of the Netherlands (1797–1881) second son of William I of the Netherlands, and his wife, Princess Louise of Prussia (1808–1870), da
2 CONC ughter of Frederick William III of Prussia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Princess Marie was diagnosed with profound hearing problems at an early age. Like her sister Louise, she was considered intelligent - and very regal - but not attractive. Her marital considerations were also affected by the consid
2 CONC erable fortune (enormous even by contemporary royal/imperial standards) that she would bring to any match. Her parents hoped to marry her to Albert Edward, Prince of Wales (later King Edward VII) but the Prince's mother, Queen Vic
2 CONC toria. opposed the match.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie married on 18 July 1871 in Wassenaar, William, Prince of Wied (1845–1907), elder son of Hermann, Prince of Wied and Princess Marie of Nassau.
2 CONT
2 CONT They had six children: William Frederick, Prince of Wied (27 June 1872 – 18 June 1945) married Princess Pauline of Württemberg, had issue. Prince Alexander of Wied (28 May 1874 – 15 January 1877) William, Prince of Albania (26 Mar
2 CONC ch 1876 – 18 April 1945) married Princess Sophie of Schönburg-Waldenburg (1885–1936), had issue. Prince Victor of Wied (7 December 1877 – 1 March 1946) married Countess Gisela of Solms-Widenfels (1891–1976), had issue. Princess Lo
2 CONC uise of Wied (24 October 1880 – 29 August 1965) Princess Elisabeth of Wied (28 January 1883 – 14 November 1938)
