2 CONT ''''''HANKÓ ILDIKÓ - KISZELY ISTVÁN
2 CONT
2 CONT ''''''A NÁDORI KRIPTA''''''
2 CONT
2 CONT ''''''Paulina (or Paula)''''''
2 CONT
2 CONT Early in the morning of 8 March 1801, Alexandra Pavlovna gave birth to a daughter. According to medical reports, the child was “very weak” and died a few hours after birth. After her death she was baptized with the name Paula (Pau
2 CONC lina).
2 CONT
2 CONT After her death the girl was placed in an oak coffin lined with fabric embroidered with silver thread; her head rested on a pillow decorated with metallic embroidery. She was dressed in a long burial shirt made of pure white silk.
2 CONC  The front of the garment was adorned with a ribbon of fine satin silk tied at the neck, the two loose ends reaching the full length of the clothing.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the outside of the wooden coffin a cross made of brocade was attached, after which the entire coffin was placed in a copper sarcophagus that was locked with a double key. On it the following inscription in Latin was written:
2 CONT
2 CONT ''''''“Paulina, child of Archduke Joseph of Austria, Palatine of Hungary, and Alexandra Pavlovna, daughter of the Emperor of Russia, spent the first and last day of her life in Buda on 8 March 1801.”''''''
2 CONT
2 CONT The little daughter of the Grand Duchess rested in the Capuchin church in Buda until 1838. On 24 May her coffin, together with her mother’s separate urn and the silver casket containing the heart, was quietly transferred to the Pa
2 CONC latine Crypt beneath the Chapel of Saint Sigismund in Buda.
2 CONT
2 CONT The tomb of Paulina was opened in December 1978. The copper pillow and the princely crown that had originally belonged to the top of the tomb monument were found in the collections of the Kiscell Museum of the Budapest History Mus
2 CONC eum, where they had somehow arrived several years earlier.
2 CONT
2 CONT The sculptural tomb monument was briefly laid horizontally while the grave itself was lifted with a mechanical device. The coffin was opened in the presence of a commission at our residence. The body of little Paulina had not been
2 CONC  embalmed, therefore only metric and chemical examination of the bones could be carried out. The dimensions of the bones corresponded to those of a full-term, well-developed newborn, and there is no indication that the child had b
2 CONC een “very weak.”
2 CONT
2 CONT No pathological changes were found on the bones, except that the sphenoid bone of the skull was relatively small in comparison with the other bones of the body. The chemical composition of the bones corresponds to Paulina’s age, a
2 CONC nd no signs of disturbed ossification were detected. The size of the eye sockets was normal, and the grooved surface at the junction of the ends and shafts of the long bones was considered normal.
2 CONT
2 CONT “As regards Paulina’s condition at birth, we were unable to find any medical documents,” the forensic report states. “During our investigations we found no signs of congenital diseases or abnormalities in the development of the bo
2 CONC nes. The body length calculated from the long bones (51.49 cm) corresponds to that of a mature full-term newborn. Based on the examination, the exact cause of death cannot be determined; however, the previously described illness o
2 CONC f the mother could have caused intrauterine hypoxia (lack of oxygen), which may explain the death of the newborn shortly after birth.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Considering contemporary newspaper reports that the labor had been very difficult and prolonged, it is possible that the child’s death was caused by complications during childbirth. Consequently, the death of the newborn must be r
2 CONC egarded as the result of natural causes, and the theory of poisoning can be dismissed, a conclusion also supported by toxicological examinations.
2 CONT
2 CONT Magyar Elektronikus Könyvtár
2 CONT https://mek.oszk.hu
