1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Princess Victoria of the United Kingdom ==
2 CONT
2 CONT The Princess Victoria (Victoria Alexandra Olga Mary; 6 July 1868 – 3 December 1935), also called "Toria".  Princess Victoria Alexandra Olga Mary of Wales was baptised with the name of Victoria Alexandra Olga Mary Saxe-Coburg and G
2 CONC otha. On 1917 her name was legally changed to Victoria Alexandra Olga Mary Windsor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents: Edward VII of the United Kingdom (1841-1910) & Alexandra of Denmark (1844-1925).
2 CONT
2 CONT Princess Victoria was a member of the British Royal Family, the fourth child and second daughter of Edward VII; the younger sister of George V.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Early Life ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Princess Victoria was born on the 6 July 1868 at Marlborough House, London.  Her father was Prince Albert Edward, Prince of Wales (later King Edward VII), the eldest son of Queen Victoria and Prince Albert of Saxe-Coburg-Gotha. He
2 CONC r mother was Alexandra, Princess of Wales (née Princess Alexandra of Denmark), the daughter of King Christian IX of Denmark and Princess Louise of Hesse-Kassel (or Hesse-Cassel). As the granddaughter of the British monarch, in the
2 CONC  male-line, she was styled Her Royal Highness Princess Victoria of Wales. She was known to her family as Toria.
2 CONT She was christened at Marlborough House on 6 August 1868 by Archibald Campbell Tait, Bishop of London and her godparents were: her paternal grandmother Queen Victoria (for whom The Duchess of Cambridge stood proxy), The Emperor of
2 CONC  Russia (for whom the Russian ambassador Philipp, Graf de Brunnow stood proxy), The Tsarevitch of Russia, The Prince Arthur (her paternal uncle), Prince Ludwig of Hesse and by Rhine (her father's brother-in-law), Prince George of 
2 CONC Hesse-Cassel (her mother's great-uncle), her mother's sister-in-law The Queen of Greece (for whom The Grand Duchess of Mecklenburg-Strelitz stood proxy), The Dowager Queen of Denmark, The Dowager Grand Duchess of Mecklenburg-Strel
2 CONC itz, the Queen's cousin Princess Francis of Teck and Princess Frederick Augustus of Anhalt-Dessau.
2 CONT Princess Victoria of Wales was educated by tutors and spent her childhood at Marlborough House and Sandringham. The Princess was particularly close to her brother, the future King George V.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Royal life ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Although she had a number of suitors, Princess Victoria never married and as such bore no children. Her mother, Alexandra, is believed to have actively discouraged her from marrying. Instead she remained a companion to her parents
2 CONC , particularly her mother, with whom she lived until Queen Alexandra's death in 1925. The Princess then set up her own home at Coppins, Iver, in Buckinghamshire. She took a particular interest in the village life, becoming honorar
2 CONC y president of the Iver Horticultural Society.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Later life ==
2 CONT
2 CONT *Princess Victoria died at home in December 1935. Her funeral took place at St George's Chapel, Windsor Castle and she was buried at Frogmore Royal Burial Ground, Windsor Great Park. Her death greatly affected George V, who died o
2 CONC ne month later.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Titles, styles, honours and arms ==
2 CONT
2 CONT === Titles and styles ===
2 CONT
2 CONT *6 July 1868 – 22 January 1901: Her Royal Highness Princess Victoria of Wales
2 CONT *22 January 1901 – 3 December 1935: Her Royal Highness The Princess Victoria
2 CONT
2 CONT === Honors ===
2 CONT
2 CONT *Imperial Order of the Crown of India, 6 August 1887
2 CONT *Dame Grand Cross of the Order of the Hospital of St. John of Jerusalem
2 CONT *Member First Class of the Royal Order of Victoria and Albert
2 CONT *Member First Class of the Royal Family Order of King Edward VII
2 CONT *Member First Class of the Royal Family Order of King George V
2 CONT
2 CONT === Arms ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon her younger sister's marriage in 1896, Princess Victoria was awarded a personal coat of arms, being the arms of the kingdom, bearing an inescutcheon of the shield of Saxony and differenced with a label argent of five points, 
2 CONC the first, third and fifth bearing roses gules, and the second and fourth crosses gules.[2]  The inescutcheon was dropped by royal warrant in 1917.
2 CONT
