1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Rubert Cambridge, Viscount Trematon
2 CONT
2 CONT formerly...
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Rubert Cambridge
2 CONT
2 CONT HSH Prince Rupert of Teck
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rupert_Cambridge,_Viscount_Trematon
2 CONT Rupert Alexander George Cambridge, Viscount Trematon (24 April 1907 – 15 April 1928) was an English-born great-grandson of Queen Victoria. Originally Prince Rupert of Teck, he and his family relinquished their German titles in 191
2 CONC 7. As heir apparent to the Earldom of Athlone, he was known as Lord Trematon, but he died before he could inherit the title.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Rupert with his mother and sister, c. 1909
2 CONT Prince Rupert was born on 24 April 1907 at Claremont House, England. His parents were Prince Alexander of Teck and the former Princess Alice of Albany, great-grandson and granddaughter of George III and Queen Victoria respectively
2 CONC . Prince Rupert was a hemophiliac, a condition he shared with many descendants of Queen Victoria, including his maternal grandfather, Prince Leopold, Duke of Albany, who died because of it. [1] Rupert attended Ludgrove School[2] a
2 CONC nd Eton as a boy.[3] At the time of his death, he was an undergraduate at Trinity College, Cambridge.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Viscount Trematon
2 CONT During the First World War, anti-German feeling in the United Kingdom led Rupert's uncle King George V to renounce all Germanic titles for himself and his family. In response to this, Prince Alexander, Rupert's father, on 14 July 
2 CONC 1917[5] renounced his title of a prince of Teck in the Kingdom of Württemberg and the style His Serene Highness. Alexander, along with his brother, Prince Adolphus of Teck, adopted the name Cambridge, after their maternal grandfat
2 CONC her, Prince Adolphus, Duke of Cambridge. On 7 November,[6] the King created his brother-in-law Earl of Athlone and Viscount Trematon. Alexander was now styled The Right Honorable the Earl of Athlone.[7] Rupert adopted the courtesy
2 CONC  title of Viscount Trematon. His mother retained her title of Princess of Great Britain and Ireland with the style Her Royal Highness and became known as Princess Alice, Countess of Athlone.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death
2 CONT Viscount Trematon died on 15 April 1928 from an intracerebral hemorrhage as a result of a car crash in France.[8] On 1 April 1928, Lord Trematon was driving with two friends on the road from Paris to Lyon. While overtaking another
2 CONC  vehicle, Trematon's car hit a tree and overturned.[4] One of his friends died from injuries and Trematon was taken to a nearby hospital at Belleville-sur-Saône with a slight fracture of the skull.[9] He never recovered consciousn
2 CONC ess and died in hospital two weeks later in the early hours of 15 April.[8] His funeral took place in St George's Chapel, Windsor Castle, and was attended by King George V and Queen Mary.[10] He was interred in the Royal Vault at 
2 CONC St George's Chapel on 20 April before being buried in the Royal Burial Ground, Frogmore, on 23 October 1928.[11] His death without issue meant that the title of Earl of Athlone became extinct in 1957 when his father died without f
2 CONC urther issue, his infant brother Maurice having died in 1910.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Teck-5
2 CONT * https://royalmusingsblogspotcom.blogspot.com/2010/04/viscount-trematon-is-dead-at-age-of-20.html
2 CONT * https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/6137145/rupert_viscount-trematon
2 CONT
2 CONT British Royalty. Viscount Trematon. Born Rupert Alexander George Augustus von Teck, at Claremont House, Esher, Surrey, the eldest son of Alice Mary Victoria Augusta Pauline, Princess of Albany and Sir Alexander Augustus Frederick 
2 CONC William Alfred George von Teck. From his mother, Rupert inherited the blood disorder hemophilia, which had been passed to her from her grandmother, Queen Victoria. In 1917 during the anti-Germanic climate of the First World War, h
2 CONC is name was legally changed to Rupert Alexander George Augustus Cambridge, and he was styled Viscount Trematon. He attended Eton and Trinity College, Cambridge University. While traveling in Bellevue-sur-Saone, France, Rupert died
2 CONC  as a result of injuries sustained in a car accident. Funeral services were held for him at St George's Chapel, Windsor Castle, four days prior to what would have been his twenty-first birthday.
