1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gustav_Sabac_el_Cher
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/255617021/gustav-sabac_el_cher
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.blackpast.org/global-african-history/sabac-el-cher-gustav-albrecht-1868-1934/
2 CONT
2 CONT Afro-German band conductor and restaurateur Gustav Albrecht Sabac el Cher, born March 10, 1868 in a palace in Berlin, was the son of August Sabac el Cher (1836?-1885), a Sudanese man who as an orphaned boy in Egypt was presented a
2 CONC s a gift to Prussian Prince Friedrich Heinrich Albrecht and accompanied him back to Germany to serve the prince as valet, butler, and decorated soldier. Gustav’s mother, Anna Maria Jung, was the daughter of a prosperous textile me
2 CONC rchant.
2 CONT
2 CONT Proficient with the violin since childhood, at age 17 Gustav entered military service as a musician and eventually received training at the Royal Academy of Music in Charlottenburg Palace in Berlin. During his tenure as Band Meist
2 CONC er of the First Prussian Regiment of Grenadiers in Konigsberg he became somewhat of a celebrity known for his arrangement of military marches and Mozart overtures. Leaving the German Army in 1909, Gustav found freelance work direc
2 CONC ting orchestras in several cities and in the early 1920s was a pioneering radio orchestra conductor. He later owned a garden restaurant popular with tourists in Königs Wusterhausen in the state of Brandenburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gustav lived a comfortable life with his wife, Gertrud Perlig, and sons Herbert and Horst. A staunch monarchist and nationalist, he welcomed the rise of fascism in Germany. However, his fortunes soon changed upon the election Adol
2 CONC ph Hitler as Chancellor in 1933. When Nazi authorities declared his business “Negerlokal” (meaning Negro-owned), the restaurant was boycotted and forced to close. Plans to launch another restaurant in central Berlin ended when Gus
2 CONC tav died in 1934. Recognizing his loyalty and contributions to German culture, Kaiser Wilhelm II, exiled in the Netherlands, sent a telegram to Gertrud expressing his condolences. Ironically, followers of the reactionary military 
2 CONC veterans' group Stahl helm (Steel Helmet), of which Gustav was a member, placed a wreath decorated with swastikas on his coffin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Both of Gustav’s sons fought for Nazi Germany in World War II. Horst was a medic in the Wehrmacht who went missing in action in 1943 during Germany’s invasion of Russia. Herbert was enlisted in the Volkssturm (national militia) bu
2 CONC t survived the war. Like his father, he enjoyed a career in music and was a violinist at the Mannheim National Theatre in post-war West Germany.
2 CONT
2 CONT Neither Herbert’s daughter, Angela, a business executive, nor his son, Axel, a retired textile engineer and diving instructor living in Stuttgart, were fully aware of their African ancestry or of the achievements of their great gr
2 CONC andfather August or Grandfather Gustav until 1999 when they were contacted by a military historian researching the background of subjects in a painting purchased by the German Historical Museum. The work, executed in 1890, titled 
2 CONC “Preussisches Liebesglück” (“Prussian Happiness in Love”) by noted artist Emil Donerstling, featured a young Gustav in uniform (skin color much darker than actual) affectionately embracing Gertrud.  Rediscovery of the painting and
2 CONC  nearly a decade of research led to a television documentary and book on the surprising history of the Sabac el Cher family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gustav Albrecht Sabac el Cher (10 March 1868 in Berlin[1] – † 4 October 1934 in Berlin) was a military musician in the Kingdom of Prussia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gustav's parents were August Sabac el Cher, a native of Egypt (now Sudan), and his wife Anna, née Jung. Until the age of 14 he attended the secondary school. At the age of eight, he began taking violin lessons. At the age of 17, h
2 CONC e joined the Prussian Army in Brandenburg an der Havel as a military musician with the band of the Brandenburg Fusilier Regiment No. 35. In 1893 he entered the Royal Academy of Music in Charlottenburg. In 1895 he was appointed con
2 CONC ductor of the (1st East Prussian) Grenadier Regiment "King Frederick III" No. 1 (renamed in 1900 to Grenadier Regiment "Kronprinz" No. 1) in Königsberg. There he soon became a well-known personality in the city, which is reflected
2 CONC  in numerous newspaper articles. Sabac el Cher composed his own pieces of music and arranged various Mozart overtures for military music.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1901 he married Gertrude Perling (1883–1935), the daughter of a teacher. Eight years later, he resigned from the army with the rank of senior music master (deputy officer). With his wife and two sons, Horst and Herbert, he move
2 CONC d back to Berlin. He worked as a civilian bandmaster and accepted engagements in various German cities. During the Weimar Republic, he repeatedly appeared in the new medium of radio as a conductor of major orchestras.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the end of the 1920s, the family opened a garden restaurant in Senzig near Königs Wusterhausen. In 1933, after the National Socialists seized power, the guests stayed away, so that the El Chers had to close their restaurant, wh
2 CONC ich had been doing well for years. They moved back to Berlin and opened a coffee house at Oranienburger Strasse 39. However, they also had to give up this a few months later due to pressure from the authorities.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Sabac el Cher died in Berlin, his widow received a telegram of condolence from the exiled Emperor Wilhelm and Crown Prince Wilhelm, in whose Königsberg regiment her husband had once served. When she died six months later, she
2 CONC  was buried next to her husband in Berlin. Later, both were transferred to Senzig by their sons, who had settled in Königs Wusterhausen, where they found their final resting place.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their son Horst (1908–1943) died in the Second World War on the Eastern Front as a medic for the Wehrmacht in the Caucasus, while their son Herbert (1903–1963) survived the war. A third son had died shortly after birth in 1906.
