1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sir Henry Bellingham, 4th Baronet From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT   (Redirected from Sir Alan Henry Bellingham, 4th Baronet) Jump to navigationJump to search For other people named Henry Bellingham, see Henry Bellingham (disambiguation). Sir Alan Henry Bellingham, 4th Baronet, (23 August 1846 – 
2 CONC 9 June 1921) was an Anglo-Irish Conservative Member of Parliament. He was Justice of the Peace, High Sheriff of Louth and Lord Lieutenant of Louth. He was Senator of the Royal University of Ireland and Private Chamberlain to popes
2 CONC  Pius IX, Leo XIII and Pius X. He was the father of the diplomat Sir Edward Bellingham, 5th Bt. and the uncle of Sir Evelyn Wrench, editor of The Spectator.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents 1	Background 2	Career 3	Family 4	References 5	External links Background[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Castle Bellingham, County Louth; seat of the Bellinghams since 1660. Born at Dunany House, Castlebellingham, County Louth, he was the eldest son of Sir Alan Bellingham (1800-1889), 3rd Bt., and his wife Elizabeth, only daughter of
2 CONC  Henry Clarke, of West Skirbeck House, Lincolnshire.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was educated at Harrow School and Exeter College, Oxford, where he graduated with a Bachelor of Arts in 1869 and a Master of Arts three years later.[2] He succeeded his father as baronet in 1889.[3] In 1900, he inherited the Ca
2 CONC stlebellingham estate from his uncle, Sydney Robert Bellingham. In 1909, he received a Honorary Doctorate of Law from the Royal University of Ireland and became one of its senators.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Career[edit] In 1875, he was called to the bar by Lincoln's Inn.[2] Bellingham served in the British Army and captain in the 6th Battalion, Royal Irish Rifles.[2] He entered the British House of Commons in 1880, representing Count
2 CONC y Louth as Member of Parliament (MP) until 1885.[5] He was High Sheriff of Louth in 1897, Justice of the Peace for this county[4] and, having been previously a Deputy Lieutenant was appointed Lord Lieutenant of Louth in 1911, an o
2 CONC ffice he held until his death in 1921.[6] Bellingham was Commissioner of National Education for Ireland and was successively Private Chamberlain to the three popes, Pius IX, Leo XIII and Pius X.[7] He was also High Sheriff of Lout
2 CONC h for 1897.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family[edit] On 13 January 1874, he married firstly Lady Constance Julia Eleanor Georgiana Noel, daughter of Charles Noel, 2nd Earl of Gainsborough at St Thomas of Canterbury Church, Exton.[8] She died in 1891, and Bellingham marr
2 CONC ied secondly Hon. Lelgarde Harry Florence Clifton, younger daughter of Augustus Wykeham Clifton and his wife Bertha Clifton, 22nd Baroness Grey de Ruthyn at the Church of Our Lady, St John's Wood on 11 June 1895.[8] Bellingham had
2 CONC  two sons and two daughters by his first wife.[3] He died aged 74 and was succeeded in the baronetcy by his older son Edward.[1] His second daughter Augusta married John Crichton-Stuart, 4th Marquess of Bute.[8] He was the uncle o
2 CONC f Sir Evelyn Wrench, editor of The Spectator
2 CONT
