1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT She married John Crichton-Stuart, 4th Marquess of the County of Bute, son of John Patrick Crichton-Stuart, 3rd Marquess of the County of Bute and Hon. Gwendolen Mary Anne Fitzalan-Howard, on 6 July 1905 at Castle Bellingham, Kilsa
2 CONC ran, County Louth, Ireland. Both her father and her father-in-law were noted converts to Roman Catholicism.
2 CONT
2 CONT After her marriage Augusta Bellingham was styled as Marchioness of Bute and her married name became Crichton-Stuart. She and her husband had seven children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT The Marchioness of Bute with her children in 1912 Mary Crichton-Stuart (8 May 1906-1980) married Edward Walker and had issue Sir John Crichton-Stuart, 5th Marquess of the County of Bute (b. 4 August 1907-d. 14 August 1956) Jean Cr
2 CONC ichton-Stuart (b. 28 October 1908-d. 23 October 1995), married Lt.-Cmdr. Hon. James Bertie and had issue (two sons); her elder son was Fra' Andrew Willoughby Ninian Bertie, Prince and Grand Master of the Sovereign Military Order o
2 CONC f Malta from 1988 until his death in 2008. Robert Crichton-Stuart (b. 12 December 1909-d. 1976), married Lady Janet Egida Montgomerie (1911–1999), daughter of Archibald Montgomerie, 16th Earl of Eglinton and had issue. David Crich
2 CONC ton- Stuart (b. 8 February 1911-d. 1970)), married Ursula Packe and had issue. Patrick Crichton-Stuart (b. 1 February 1913-d. 5 February 1956), married Jane von Bahr and had issue. Rhidian Crichton-Stuart (b. 4 June 1917-d. 25 Jun
2 CONC e 1969) Honours[edit] She was invested as a Dame Commander of the Order of the British Empire (DBE) in 1918. She was also invested as a Dame of Grace, Order of St. John of Jerusalem, and decorated with the Médaille de la Reine Eli
2 CONC sabeth of Belgium.
2 CONT
2 CONT She died in 1947, aged 66.<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT [https://livesofthefirstworldwar.org/lifestory/7665737 LOTFWW]<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT '''MOUNT STUART ROYAL NAVAL HOSPITAL 1914 - 1918'''<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT With the outbreak of war, Augusta Crichton-Stuart and her husband John, the Marchioness and Marquess of the County of Bute, offered their home to the nation. The red sandstone Victorian Gothic Mansion became known as Mount Stuart 
2 CONC Royal Naval Hospital, the transformation being carried out under the direction of the Marchioness of Bute. Lady Bute also received instruction in nursing, and administered the hospital throughout the war. She was invested as a Dam
2 CONC e Commander of the Order of the British Empire (DBE) in 1918. She was also invested as a Dame of Grace, Order of St. John of Jerusalem, and decorated with the Médaille de la Reine Elisabeth of Belgium. She and her husband had seve
2 CONC n children, the last born during the war, in June of 1917.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Isle of Bute, Scotland, United Kingdom
2 CONT
2 CONT Shared on LOTFWW by Kristen den Hartog
2 CONT
2 CONT from Scotland's War - University of Edinburgh<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
