1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''From [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Princess_Alexandra,_2nd_Duchess_of_Fife Wikipedia: Princess Alexandra, 2nd Duchess of Fife]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Princess Alexandra, 2nd Duchess of Fife, RRC GCStJ (Alexandra Victoria Alberta Edwina Louise Duff; Sunday, 17 May 1891 – Thursday, 26 February 1959), born Lady Alexandra Duff and known as Princess Arthur of Connaught after her mar
2 CONC riage, was the eldest surviving grandchild of King Edward VII and also the first cousin of George VI. Alexandra and her younger sister, Maud, had the distinction of being the only female-line descendants of a British sovereign off
2 CONC icially granted both the title of Princess and the style of Highness.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Lineage and early life''
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexandra's father was Alexander Duff, 1st Duke of Fife. Having succeeded his father as the 6th Earl Fife, he was elevated to Duke of Fife and Marquess of Macduff in the Peerage of the United Kingdom on his marriage in 1889 to Pri
2 CONC ncess Louise of Wales, the eldest daughter of the future Edward VII. Princess Louise accordingly became the Duchess of Fife, and succeeded as the head of many Scottish Feudal Baronies, including MacDuff, named for James Duff, 2nd 
2 CONC Earl Fife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexandra was born at [https://maps.nls.uk/geo/explore/#zoom=16.1&lat=51.46046&lon=-0.26532&layers=6&b=1&marker=51.460000,-0.269166 East Sheen Lodge, now near 67 York Avenue, East Sheen, London SW14 7LQ 51.460000, -0.269166], Rich
2 CONC mond on Sunday, 17 May 1891. After ten years of marriage and the birth in 1893 of Alexandra's younger sister Maud, no more children would be born to Alexandra's parents and the dukedom and marquessate of Fife were headed toward ex
2 CONC tinction since only a male heir could inherit those titles. On Tuesday, 24 April 1900, Queen Victoria granted Alexander Duff a second dukedom of Fife, along with the earldom of Macduff, stipulating by special remainder that these 
2 CONC two titles would jointly devolve, in default of sons born to him and the Queen's granddaughter, upon their daughters in order of seniority of birth, and upon their respective agnatic male descendants in the same order. After her f
2 CONC ather's death in 1912, therefore, she inherited the Dukedom of Fife in her own right.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a female-line granddaughter of the British monarch, Alexandra was not entitled to the title of "Princess", nor to the style of Her Royal Highness. Instead she was styled Lady Alexandra Duff, as the daughter of a duke, even thou
2 CONC gh she was born fifth in the line of succession to the British throne. Alexandra and her sister were unique among British princesses in that they were descended from both King William IV (through his mistress, Dorothea Jordan), an
2 CONC d William IV's niece, Queen Victoria, who succeeded him because he left no legitimate issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was baptised at the Chapel Royal, St James's Palace on Monday, 29 June 1891 by the Archbishop of Canterbury, Edward White Benson. Her godparents were Queen Victoria and the Prince and Princess of Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Princess Alexandra''
2 CONT
2 CONT On Thursday, 9 November 1905, King Edward VII declared his eldest daughter Princess Royal. He further ordered Garter King of Arms to gazette Lady Alexandra Duff and her sister Lady Maud Duff with the style and attribute of Highnes
2 CONC s and the style of Princess prefixed to their respective Christian names, with precedence immediately after all members of the British royal family bearing the style of Royal Highness. From that point, Her Highness Princess Alexan
2 CONC dra held her title and rank, not from her ducal father, but from the decree issued by will of the sovereign (her maternal grandfather).
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1910, Alexandra became secretly engaged to Prince Christopher of Greece and Denmark, a son of King George I of the Hellenes. The engagement was terminated when their disapproving parents learned of the liaison.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Marriage and issue''
2 CONT
2 CONT On Wednesday, 15 October 1913, Princess Alexandra married her first cousin, once removed, Prince Arthur of Connaught at the Chapel Royal, St. James's Palace, London.
2 CONT
2 CONT The bride's attendants were:
2 CONT * Princess Maud, the bride's sister.
2 CONT * Princess Mary of the United Kingdom, the bride's maternal first cousin and daughter of King George V.
2 CONT * Princess Mary of Teck and Princess Helena of Teck, daughters of Prince Adolphus, Duke of Teck (brother of Queen Mary).
2 CONT * Princess May of Teck, the bride's maternal second cousin and daughter of Prince Alexander of Teck (brother of Queen Mary) and Princess Alice of Albany.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Arthur of Connaught was the only son of the Duke of Connaught and Strathearn, third son of Queen Victoria and thus a younger brother of her maternal grandfather, King Edward VII. As such, Arthur and Alexandra were first cou
2 CONC sins once removed.
2 CONT
2 CONT After their marriage, Alexandra was referred to as HRH Princess Arthur of Connaught, in accordance with the tradition that a wife normally shares the title and style of her husband.
2 CONT
2 CONT With her husband, Alexandra also carried out royal engagements on behalf of her uncle, King George V, and later for her cousin, King George VI. She also served as a Counsellor of State between 1937 and 1944.
2 CONT
2 CONT The couple's only child, their son Alastair Windsor, 2nd Duke of Connaught and Strathearn, was born on Sunday, 9 August 1914.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Nursing career''
2 CONT
2 CONT World War I gave to Princess Arthur an opportunity to embrace her vocation of nursing in which she subsequently made a successful career. In 1915 she joined the staff of St. Mary's Hospital, Paddington, as a full-time nurse and wo
2 CONC rked in this capacity until the armistice. After the war she continued her training at St. Mary's, becoming a state registered nurse in 1919. She was awarded a first prize for a paper on eclampsia. Princess Arthur also practised a
2 CONC t Queen Charlotte's Hospital, specialising in gynaecology, where she received a certificate of merit. Throughout these years Princess Arthur increasingly impressed her superiors by her technical skill and practical efficiency.
2 CONT
2 CONT When her husband was appointed governor-general of the Union of South Africa, Princess Arthur ably seconded him and shared his popularity. Her tact and friendliness made her many friends among the South Africans, who also greatly 
2 CONC admired the interest which she displayed in hospitals, child welfare, and maternity work throughout the Union. To these subjects she brought her personal knowledge and experience, which enabled her to make effective and valuable s
2 CONC uggestions.
2 CONT
2 CONT On her return to London in 1923, Princess Arthur resumed her nursing career at University College Hospital, where she was known as Nurse Marjorie, and subsequently at Charing Cross Hospital. At this time she specialised in surgery
2 CONC , proving herself a competent theatre sister capable of performing minor operations herself and of instructing juniors. Her services to the nursing profession were recognized in July 1925, when she was awarded the badge of the Roy
2 CONC al Red Cross by George V.
2 CONT
2 CONT The outbreak of World War II in 1939 afforded Princess Arthur further scope for her nursing abilities. She refused the offer of a post as matron of a hospital in the country, preferring to become sister-in-charge of the casualty c
2 CONC learing station of the Second British General Hospital set up to treat the troops retreating from Dunkirk. Shortly thereafter, she opened the Fife Nursing Home in Bentinck Street which she personally equipped, financed, and admini
2 CONC stered as matron for ten years.
2 CONT
2 CONT On Monday, 26 April 1943 her only child, Alastair, Duke of Connaught and Strathearn, died unexpectedly (and in ill-defined circumstances) whilst staying at Rideau Hall in Ottawa with his relative the Governor-General of Canada, th
2 CONC e Earl of Athlone.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Later life and death''
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1949 the rheumatoid arthritis, from which Princess Arthur had suffered for many years, rendered her bedbound and so necessitated the closing of her nursing home. She retired to her London home at 64 Avenue Road, St John's Wood,
2 CONC  London where she wrote for private circulation two autobiographical fragments in a vivid and entertaining style: A Nurse's Story (1955) and Egypt and Khartoum (1956), in which she gave a graphic account of the shipwreck of SS Del
2 CONC hi which ran aground in fog and heavy seas in 1911 – Princess Arthur, her sister and mother nearly died and her father, Alexander Duff, 1st Duke of Fife, subsequently died as a result of his injuries a few weeks later. She was eng
2 CONC aged on a further volume on big-game hunting in South Africa when she died at home on Thursday, 26 February 1959.
2 CONT
2 CONT At her request she was cremated, and her ashes laid in St Ninian's Chapel, Braemar, on the March Lodge estate alongside her parents' and son's. Her will was sealed in London after her death in 1959. Her estate was valued at £86,21
2 CONC 7 (or £1.4 million in 2022 when adjusted for inflation).
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Titles, styles, honours and arms''
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Titles and styles''
2 CONT * Sunday, 9 November 1905: Lady Alexandra Duff
2 CONT * Thursday, 9 November 1905 – Monday, 29 January 1912: Her Highness Princess Alexandra
2 CONT * Monday, 29 January 1912 – Wednesday, 15 October 1913: Her Highness Princess Alexandra, Duchess of Fife
2 CONT * Wednesday, 15 October 1913 – Thursday, 26 February 1959: Her Royal Highness Princess Arthur of Connaught, Duchess of Fife
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite the fact that Alexandra and her sister were not daughters of a royal duke, they were sometimes unofficially referred to with the territorial designation of Fife but in official documents, until their marriages, they were a
2 CONC lways styled Her Highness Princess Alexandra or Maud, without the territorial designation "of Fife".
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Honours''
2 CONT * Royal Red Cross
2 CONT * Dame Grand Cross of the Most Venerable Order of St John of Jerusalem (elevated from Dame of Justice)
2 CONT * Royal Family Order of King Edward VII
2 CONT * Royal Family Order of King George V
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Honorary military appointments''
2 CONT * Colonel-in-chief, Royal Army Pay Corps
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000540/19590227/128/0006 British Newspaper Archive: The Scotsman Friday, 27 February 1959 Page 6 The Court]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Buckingham Palace, Thursday, 26 February 1959
2 CONT
2 CONT The Queen has received with great sorrow the news of the death of Her Royal Highness Princess Arthur of Connaught, Duchess of Fife, Her Majesty's cousin.
2 CONT
2 CONT The cremation of Her Royal Highness Princess Arthur of Connaught, will take place privately in London. The interment of the ashes will be at March Lodge, Braemar, on Tuesday, 3 March 1959, at 12.30 pm. A memorial service will be h
2 CONC eld within the Royal Chapel, St James's Palace, London, at 3 p.m.
2 CONT
