2 CONT '''From [https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000560/19091115/056/0007 British Newspaper Archive: Daily Mirror Monday, 15 November 1909 Page 7 Lady Dalmeny's sister to be married in London today]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Miss Millicent Constance Grosvenor, elder daughter of Lord Henry Grosvenor and a sister of Lady Dalmeny, and Mr. W. Molyneux Clarke, who are to be married at [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Paul%27s_Church,_Knightsbridge St Paul
2 CONC 's Church, 32a Wilton Place, Knightsbridge, London SW1X 8SH 51.5015, -0.1557] today, Monday, 15 November 1909. The wedding will be a very pretty affair, for the bride is to be attended by no fewer than ten bridesmaids, who will al
2 CONC l be attired alike in grey and pink chiffon and large violet and pale blue hats. Both bride and bridegroom have been the recipients of many costly gifts.—(Langfier.)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000619/19190213/010/0010 British Newspaper Archive: Birmingham Daily Post Thursday, 13 February 1919 Page 10 Officer's divorce suit: wife who returned home for the chil
2 CONC d's birthday]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Justice Coleridge, in the Divorce Division yesterday, Wednesday, 12 February 1919, heard the undefended suit of Captain William Molyneux Clarke, on the ground of the misconduct of his wife, Millicent Constance Clarke, with the
2 CONC  co-respondent, Captain Frank Billinge. Mr. Bayford stated that a claim damages had been withdrawn.
2 CONT
2 CONT The petitioner's case was that he married respondent in November 1909, and lived happily with her at Piddinghoe, Sussex. In 1917 petitioner obtained a commission in the army, and made the acquaintance of the co-respondent, who bec
2 CONC ame a visitor to the house. When the co-respondent was ordered to London, the respondent, although her husband objected, went to do "war work."
2 CONT
2 CONT On Thursday, 3 January 1918, he received this letter from his wife: — 
2 CONT
2 CONT Molly, dear, I feel that this letter will be an awful shock to you.... I cannot live with you, so I feel it is best to go.... I have gone to Frank Billinge. Well, he loves me and I love him.... Any evidence you may want you can ge
2 CONC t at Old Burlington Street, where we had a flat and stayed together as Captain and Mrs. Billinge.
2 CONT
2 CONT The petitioner said he immediately obtained leave and followed his wife to London. After a time he found her, and finally persuaded her to return home for the child's birthday. Then she went back to the co-respondent in London. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After hearing the evidence his Lordship pronounced a decree nisi with costs.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/information.pl?cite=vzzJG%2FQXxtdVLboC3b9auQ&scan=1 FreeBMD: Registration of marriage of Millicent C. Grosvenor in 1919] and [https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/information.pl?cite=dtnIeVGCFx5z1
2 CONC MKtolLn0Q&scan=1 FreeBMD: Registration of marriage of Frank Billinge in 1919]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * July to September 1919: Registration of marriage of Frank Billinge and Grosvenor; in Farnham (Volume 2a, Page 450)
2 CONT * July to September 1919: Registration of marriage of Millicent C. Grosvenor and Billinge; in Farnham (Volume 2a, Page 450)
2 CONT
