1 OCCU Princess of Romania,  Queen of Greece 1922-1924.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Queen Elisabeth of Greece born Princess Elisabeta of Romania (12 October 1894 - 14 November 1956) was the Queen Consort of King George II of Greece. She was the daughter of King Ferdinand I of Romania and his wife,
2 CONC  Queen Marie. Her full birth name was Elisabetha Charlotte Josephine Alexandra Victoria von Hohenzollern-Sigmaringen, Princess of Romania.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was born in the Peleş Castle. On 27 February 1921, she married the future King George, then Crown Prince, in Bucharest. She was queen consort from 1922 to 1923, and afterwards the couple lived in exile. The marriage was unsucc
2 CONC essful, and they were divorced on 6 July 1935. George was restored to his throne on 3 November, 1935. Elisabeth then returned to live in Romania until its monarchy was abolished after World War II. She had numerous affairs, includ
2 CONC ing one before her marriage with Frank Rattigan, father of playwright Terence Rattigan.[1] which resulted in his being expelled from the diplomatic service.
2 CONT
2 CONT She died in Cannes, France in 1956.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was once quoted as saying, "I've committed every vice in my life except murder, and I don't want to die without doing that either."
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabetha Charlotte Josephine Alexandra Victoria von Hohenzollern-Sigmaringen,  Prinsesse af Rumænien, Dronning af Grækenland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wikipedia: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elisabeth_of_Romania (engelsk)
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT ..Because 19-year-old [https://www.geni.com/people/Olwen-Gucci/6000000186297459301 Olwen] had been working for a Romanian princess named '''Elisabeth''', and when '''Elisabeth''' discovered that [https://www.geni.com/people/Aldo-G
2 CONC ucci/6000000186280278877 Aldo] had got her unmarried employee pregnant, she stormed into the Gucci shop in Florence to complain to Aldo's father [https://www.geni.com/people/Guccio-Gucci/6000000089271616821 Guccio], who had founde
2 CONC d the firm.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The princess''' was satisfied when [https://www.geni.com/people/Aldo-Gucci/6000000186280278877 Aldo] offered to marry [https://www.geni.com/people/Olwen-Gucci/6000000186297459301 Olwen], who was three months pregnant when the w
2 CONC edding took place at the Roman Catholic Church of Our Lady and St Oswald, Oswestry, on August 22, 1927.
2 CONT
2 CONT *https://www.shropshirestar.com/news/nostalgia/2017/11/04/shropshire-wedding-for-global-style-guru-gucci/
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT Princess Elisabeth of Romania (full name Elisabeth Charlotte Josephine Alexandra Victoria: was a princess of Romania and member of the House of Hohenzollern-Sigmaringen and by marriage Queen consort of Greece during 1922–1924.
2 CONT
2 CONT Raised by her grand-uncle King Carol I of Romania and his wife Queen Elisabeth, she was an introvert and socially isolated. Married to Prince George, the heir to the Greek throne in 1921, she felt no passion for him and underwent 
2 CONC the political turmoil in her adopted country after World War I. When her husband became King of Greece in 1922, Elisabeth was involved in assisting refugees who arrived to Athens after the disaster of the Greco-Turkish War. The ri
2 CONC se of the revolutionary climate, however, affected her health and with great relief she left the Kingdom of Greece with her husband in December 1923. The royal couple then settled in Bucharest, and King George II was deposed on 25
2 CONC  March 1924.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Romania, Elisabeth and George II's relationship deteriorated and the couple divorced in 1935. Very close to her brother, King Carol II of Romania, the princess amassed an important fortune, partly due to financial advice of her
2 CONC  lover, the banker Alexandru Scavani. After the death of her mother, Queen Marie, in 1938 and the dethronement of Carol II in 1940, Elisabeth took up the role of First Lady of Romania. At the end of World War II, she established c
2 CONC lose links with the Romanian Communist Party and openly conspired against her nephew, the young King Michael I, earning the nickname of "Red Aunt" of the sovereign. However, her communist links did not prevent her from being expel
2 CONC led from the country when the Socialist Republic of Romania was proclaimed in 1947. Exiled, the princess moved to Switzerland and then to Cannes, in southern France. She had a romantic relationship with Marc Favrat, a man almost t
2 CONC hirty years younger, whom she finally adopted just before her death in 1956.
2 CONT
2 CONT Second child and first daughter of Crown Prince Ferdinand and Crown Princess Marie of Romania (a member of the British royal family and later Princess of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha), Elisabeth (nicknamed Lisabetha or Lizzy by her famil
2 CONC y) was born on 12 October 1894 at Peleş Castle, Sinaia. Named after her paternal great-aunt, Queen Elisabeth of Wied, shortly after birth she was removed from her parents. With her older brother Prince Carol, she was raised by Kin
2 CONC g Carol I and his wife. Over the years, Elisabeth developed a cold character and a volatile temperament which socially isolated her. Considered "vulgar" by her mother, however, she had the reputation of being a classical beauty.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1911, Prince George of Greece, then second-in-line to the throne and his future wife's second cousin, met Elisabeth for the first time. After the Balkan Wars, during which Greece and Romania were allied, the Greek prince asked 
2 CONC for the hand of Elisabeth, but, advised by her great-aunt, she declined the offer, saying that her suitor was too small and too English in his manners. Disdainful, the princess even said on the occasion, that "God began the prince
2 CONC  but forgot to finish him" (1914).
2 CONT
2 CONT During World War I, Elizabeth was involved in helping wounded soldiers. She made daily visits to the hospitals and distributed cigarettes and comforting words to the victims of the fighting.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1919, Elisabeth and her sisters Maria and Ileana accompanied their mother, now Queen Marie, to Paris at the Peace Conference. The sovereign hoped that during her stay there she could find suitable husbands for her daughters, es
2 CONC pecially Elisabeth, already aged twenty-five. After a few months in France, the Queen and her daughters decided to return to Romania in early 1920. On the way back, they made a brief stop in Switzerland, where they found the Greek
2 CONC  royal family, who lived in exile since the deposition of King Constantine I during the Great War. Elisabeth then met again Prince George (now Diadochos and heir of the throne), who asked again her hand. Now more aware of her own 
2 CONC imperfections (her mother described her as fat and of very limited intelligence), Elisabeth decided to accept the marriage. However, at that time the future of the Diadochos was far from certain: displaced from the throne with his
2 CONC  father and replaced by his younger brother, now King Alexander I, George was forbidden to stay in his country, penniless and without any prospects.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nevertheless, the engagement satisfied both Elisabeth and George's parents. Delighted to have finally found a husband for her eldest daughter, the Queen of Romania soon invited the prince to travel to Bucharest in order to publicl
2 CONC y announce the engagement. George agreed but soon after his arrival in the country of his fiancée, he learned of the accidental death of Alexander I and the ensuing political turmoil that erupted in Greece.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 5 December 1920 a referendum of disputed results[a] called the Greek royal family to return home. King Constantine I, Queen Sophia and Diadochos George therefore returned to Athens on 19 December. Their return was accompanied b
2 CONC y a significant jubilation. A huge crowd surrounded the sovereign and the heir to the throne through the streets of the capital. Once at the palace, they appeared repeatedly on the balcony to greet the people who cheered them.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, a few weeks later George returned to Romania to marry Elisabeth. The wedding took place with great pomp in Bucharest on 27 February 1921. Shortly after, Crown Prince Carol of Romania, Elisabeth's elder brother, married Ge
2 CONC orge's younger sister, Princess Helen of Greece.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Greece, Elisabeth had great difficulty integrating into the royal family, and her relationship with Queen Sophia was particularly awkward. From an introverted temperament that could be mistaken as arrogance, Elisabeth felt disp
2 CONC laced by her in-laws, who regularly spoke in Greek in her presence, because she had not yet mastered the language. Only King Constantine I and his sister, the Grand Duchess Maria Georgievna of Russia, found favor in her eyes. Inde
2 CONC ed, even the shy Diadochos disappointed his wife, who wanted to share with him a more passionate relationship.
2 CONT
2 CONT Regretting not having her own home and being forced to constantly live with her in-laws, Elisabeth spent the already little revenues of her husband into redecorating their apartments. In addition, her family delayed in paying her 
2 CONC dowry and the savings that she left in Romania were soon lost because of the poor investments made by the manager of her fortune.
2 CONT
2 CONT Facing a very difficult political situation, due to the Greco-Turkish War, Elisabeth quickly understood that her space to maneuver was limited in her new country. However, she integrated the Red Cross, which was overwhelmed by the
2 CONC  arrival of wounded coming from Anatolia. The Crown Princess also occupied her free time practicing gardening, painting and drawing. She illustrated a book of poems written by the Belgian author Emile Verhaeren. She also liked wri
2 CONC ting and producing some new books of low value. Finally, she spent long hours studying the Modern Greek, a language that was extremely hard for her to learn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Disappointed by the mediocrity of her daily routine, Elisabeth began to nourish jealousy for her sister Maria, married to King Alexander I of Yugoslavia, and her sister-in-law Helen of Greece, wife of her brother Crown Prince Caro
2 CONC l of Romania. With the war and the revolution, the everyday life of the Greek royal family was indeed increasingly difficult, and the pension received by the Diadochos George didn't allow her to buy the clothes and jewelry that sh
2 CONC e wanted.
2 CONT
2 CONT Already strained by the war, the relations of the Diadochos and his wife were clouded by their inability to give an heir to the Kingdom of Greece. Elisabeth became pregnant a few months after her marriage, but she suffered a misca
2 CONC rriage during an official trip to Smyrna. Deeply affected by her miscarriage, the crown princess became sick with typhoid soon followed by pleurisy and worsened by depression. She found refuge with her family in Bucharest, but des
2 CONC pite the efforts of her mother and husband, neither Elisabeth's health nor her marriage fully recovered from the loss of her child.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile, the disaster of the Greco-Turkish War forced King Constantine I to abdicate, which pushed George on to the throne (27 September 1922). The new king, however, had no power, and he and his queen were unable to resolve the
2 CONC  repression organized by revolutionaries who took power against the representatives of the old regime. The new royal couple saw with anguish the near execution of Prince Andrew (the king's uncle) at the Trial of the Six.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite this difficult context, Elisabeth tried to make herself useful to her adopted country. To respond to the influx of refugees originating from Anatolia, the Queen had built shacks on the outskirts of Athens. To carry out her
2 CONC  projects, she mobilized her family and asked her mother, Queen Marie, to send wood and other materials.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, Elisabeth found it increasingly difficult to cope with Greece and its revolutionary climate. Her love for George II was over, and her letters to her mother show how much she worried for her future. Her correspondence also
2 CONC  revealed that she had no desire to have children.
2 CONT
2 CONT After an attempted monarchist coup d'état in October 1923, the situation of the royal couple became even more precarious. On 19 December 1923 King George II and his wife were forced into exile by the revolutionary government. With
2 CONC  Prince Paul (the king's brother and heir-presumptive to the throne), they then departed for Romania, where they learned of the proclamation of the Second Hellenic Republic on 25 March 1924.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Romania, George II and Elizabeth moved to Bucharest, where King Ferdinand I and Queen Marie gave to them a wing of Cotroceni Palace. After a few weeks, the couple moved to a modest villa in the Calea Victoriei. Regular guests o
2 CONC f the Romanian sovereigns, the exiled Greek royal couple participated in court ceremonies. But despite the kindness with shown by his mother-in-law, the exiled King of Greece in Bucharest felt aimless and barely conceal the boredo
2 CONC m that he felt at the Romanian court.
2 CONT
2 CONT Unlike her husband, Elisabeth was delighted with her return to Romania. Her relationship with her mother was sometimes stormy, even if their literary collaborations were successful. In the mid 1920s, Elisabeth illustrated the late
2 CONC st work of her mother, The Country That I Love (1925). The links with Crown Princess Helen of Romania (wife of Crown Prince Carol of Romania and sister of King George II of Greece) remained complicated due to the jealousy that the
2 CONC  exiled Queen of the Hellenes still continued to feel against her sister-in-law.
2 CONT
2 CONT Exarcebated by the humiliations of exile, financial difficulties and the lack of offspring, the relations between George II and Elisabeth deteriorate. After initially alleviating her weariness with too much rich food and gambling,
2 CONC  the former Queen of the Hellenes began a series of extramarital relationships with several married men. She even flirted with her brother-in-law King Alexander I of Yugoslavia when she visit her sister Queen Maria during an illne
2 CONC ss in Belgrade. Later, she entered into an affair with the banker of her husband, a Greek-Romanian named Alexandru Scavani, who was appointed her chamberlain to cover up the scandal. However, Elisabeth was not the only one respons
2 CONC ible for the failure of her marriage: over the years, George II spent less time with his wife and gradually settled his residence in the United Kingdom, where he also entered into an adulterous relationship.
2 CONT
2 CONT In May 1935, Elisabeth heard from a Greek diplomat that the Second Hellenic Republic was on the verge of collapse and that the restoration of the monarchy was imminent. Frightened by this news, the exiled Queen of the Hellenes the
2 CONC n launched divorce proceedings without inorming her husband. Charged with "desertion from the family home", George II saw his marriage dissolved by a Bucharest court without being really invited to speak on the matter (6 July 1935
2 CONC ).
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of King Ferdinand I in 1927, Romania began a period of great instability. After Crown Prince Carol resigned his rights to be able to live with his mistress Magda Lupescu, his son ascended to the throne as King Mich
2 CONC ael I under the direction of a Council of Regency. Nevertheless, a significant part of the population supports the rights of Carol, who finally manages to take the crown in 1930. Very close to her brother, she actively supports hi
2 CONC s return to Romania, Elisabeth kept him daily informed of the country's political life during his years of exile.
2 CONT
2 CONT Once on the throne, Carol II maintained stormy relations with the members of his family but retained his confidence in Elisabeth, who was the only member of the royal family who accepted his mistress. Thanks to the inheritance rec
2 CONC eived from her father, the financial advice of her lover, the banker Alexandru Scavani, and her good relations with her brother, the princess managed to live in great style in Romania. In March 1935, she acquired the large domain 
2 CONC of Banloc, near the border with Yugoslavia, a mansion in Sinaia and an elegant villa of Italian style, the called Elisabeta Palace, located in the Șoseaua Kiseleff in Bucharest.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of the Queen Mother Marie in 1938 and the deposition of Carol II in 1940, Elisabeth played the role of First Lady of Romania. Ambitiously, the princess has indeed no remorse to follow her brother's policy, even whe
2 CONC n she showed herself tyrannical with other members of the royal family.[58] After the return to the throne of Michael I and the establishment of the dictatorship of Marshal Ion Antonescu, Elisabeth stayed out from politics. Howeve
2 CONC r, from 1944, she forged links with the Romanian Communist Party and openly conspired against her nephew, who now considered her a spy. In early 1947, she received in her domain of Banloc the Marshal Tito, who deposed another of h
2 CONC er nephews, the young King Peter II of Yugoslavia. Finally, through Alexandru Scavani, the Princess participated in the financing of the guerrilla who fought against her former brother-in-law, the now King Paul I, in Greece.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, Elisabeth wasn't the only member of the Romanian royal family who had friendly relations with the communists: her sister Ileana did the same in the hope of putting her eldest son, Archduke Stefan of Austria, on the throne
2 CONC . For these reasons, the two princesses then receive the nickname of "Red Aunt" of King Michael I.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite her links with the Romanian Communist Party, Elisabeth was forced to leave the country after the proclamation of the Romanian People's Republic, on 30 December 1947. The new regime gave her three days to pack her belonging
2 CONC s and the Elisabeta Palace was ransacked. However, before she went into exile, the princess had time to burn her archives in the domain of Banloc. On 12 January 1948 she left Romania with her sister Ileana aboard a special train p
2 CONC rovided by the Communists. The Scavani family accompanied them, but both princesses lost much of their property after being expelled from the country.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth settled firstly in Zurich and then in Cannes, at the Villa Rose Alba. In France, she met a handsome young seducer named Marc Favrat. Having fallen in love with the young man, the princess asked her cousin, Frederick, Pri
2 CONC nce of Hohenzollern, to bestow a title on him, but Frederick refused. The princess then decided to adopt her lover; which she did three months before her death. She died at her home on 14 November 1956.
2 CONT
2 CONT The body of the princess was transferred to the Hohenzollern-Sigmaringen crypt, the Hedinger Kirche of Sigmaringen.
