1 NAME Johann Leopold William Albert Ferdinand Victor /of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha/
2 GIVN Johann Leopold William Albert Ferdinand Victor
2 SURN of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha
2 _MARNM of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha
1 NAME Johann Leopold, Hereditary Prince of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 2 AUG 1906
2 PLAC Schloss Callenberg
2 ADDR
3 CITY Coburg
3 STAE Bavaria
3 CTRY Germany
1 DEAT
2 DATE 4 MAY 1972
2 PLAC Grein
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Austria
1 BURI
2 DATE 11 MAY 1972
2 PLAC Roman Catholic Church of St Zeno
2 ADDR
3 CITY Bad Reichenhall
3 STAE Bavaria
3 CTRY Germany
1 OCCU
1 TITL Hereditary Prince
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} "Johann Leopold, Hereditary Prince of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha (Johann Leopold William Albert Ferdinand Victor; 2 August 1906 – 4 May 1972) was the eldest son of Charles Edward, Duke of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha and Victo
2 CONC ria Adelaide of Schleswig-Holstein. From his birth until his father's abdication, he was known as Hereditary Prince Johann Leopold.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT His paternal grandparents were Prince Leopold, Duke of Albany, the fourth and last son of Victoria of the United Kingdom and Albert of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha, and his wife, Princess Helena, Duchess of Albany
2 CONT
2 CONT John Leopold had four younger siblings: Sibylla, Hubertus, Caroline Mathilde and Friedrich Josias.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was heir-apparent, from his birth, until the forced abdication of his father on 18 November 1918, to Saxe-Coburg and Gotha. The abdication was a result of the German Revolution.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT In Dresden on 9 March 1932, John married "Feodora Freiherrin von der Horst" - as this was considered a morganatic marriage and the Prince was forced to cede his own succession rights. The couple had three children, and were divorc
2 CONC ed on 27 February 1962 after almost thirty years of marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Bad Reichenhall on 3 May, 1963, John married secondly (and again morganatic) with "Maria Theresia Reindl". They had no children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Titles, styles, honours and arms
2 CONT
2 CONT Titles and styles
2 CONT
2 CONT *2 August 1906 "His Highness" The Hereditary Prince of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Lost his titles due to Deprivation Act 1917. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "By the King's Order in Council of 28 March 1919, the following people were deprived of their titles[15] (names listed in the form given in the Order in Council):
2 CONT His Royal Highness Charles Edward, Duke of Albany." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Titles_Deprivation_Act_1917
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT The Prince had three children by his first wife, all barred from the succession of the duchy of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha"
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en-academic.com/dic.nsf/enwiki/2294224
2 CONT
2 CONT "On April 1, 1932, Johann Leopold joined the Nazi Party but his loyalty to the party was repeatedly questioned. During World War II, he served as a sergeant in anti-aircraft regiment but was released in 1943 because of political u
2 CONC nreliability. After World War II, proceedings against him in a  denazification appeals court were discontinued on February 20, 1947."
2 CONT
2 CONT "In 1948, Johann Leopold made headlines when he was sentenced to two years in prison by a criminal court in Amberg, Bavaria, Germany on charges of indecency with children. After this, he broke off all connections with his family."
2 CONT
2 CONT "On May 4, 1972, Johann Leopold died at the age of 65 from cancer in Grein, Austria. He was buried in his second wife's family grave at the Roman Catholic Church of St. Zeno in Bad Reichenhall, Bavaria, Germany"
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.unofficialroyalty.com/johann-leopold-hereditary-prince-of-saxe-coburg-and-gotha-8-20-19/
2 CONT
2 CONT " The Saxe-Coburg Double Suicide that Rocked the Gotha
2 CONT Ernst-Leopold and Sabine.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 27 June 1996, Ernst-Leopold Prinz von Sachsen-Coburg und Gotha and his wife Sabine died by suicide at Bad Wiessee, a resort town in Bavaria. Ernst-Leopold was sixty-one; Sabine was fifty-five, having celebrated her birthday onl
2 CONC y two days before her untimely death. The bodies of the couple were discovered by a local farmer who came across their white Mercedes Benz outside of a pub; the car had been parked when the farmer entered the pub and was still the
2 CONC re when he left the pub. Peering inside, the farmer discovered the horrific sight of the deceased couple, who each had hunting rifles in their laps. One of the detectives involved in the case stated: "It couldn't have been a murde
2 CONC r then suicide. All the circumstances and all the evidence from the discovery of the bodies to the post mortem confirm the joint suicide thesis." Upon learning of the death of the couple, an unnamed relative of Ernst-Leopold told 
2 CONC The Guardian, "Oh God, I bet the British royal family is getting anxious. It's those Coburgs again." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Born on 14 January 1935, Ernst-Leopold Eduard Wilhelm Josias Prinz von Sachsen-Coburg und Gotha was the eldest son and second child of Hereditary Prince Johann Leopold of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha and his wife Baroness Feodore von der
2 CONC  Horst, who wed in 1932. Johann Leopold and Feodore's marriage was morganatic; the result of this was that Johann Leopold gave up his rights of succession to the Duchy of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha as well as the ability to pass on his
2 CONC  princely titles to his children. Ernst-Leopold had one older sister, Marianne, and one younger brother, Peter.
2 CONT Ernst-Leopold.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1961, Ernst-Leopold Prinz von Sachsen-Coburg und Gotha married Ingeborg Henig. Ernst-Leopold and Ingeborg had one son, Hubertus, before divorcing in 1963. In 1963, Ernst-Leopold married Gertraude Monika Pfeiffer. Ernst-Leopold 
2 CONC and Gertraude had five children: Viktoria, Ernst-Josias, Carl-Eduard, Friedrich, and Alice. Ernst-Leopold and Gertraude divorced in 1985. Finally, in 1986, Ernst-Leopold married Sabine-Margarethe Henning. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ernst-Leopold found it difficult to accept his position as a morganatic member of the extended family of the Ducal House of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha. When he visited the United Kingdom, Ernst-Leopold would introduce himself as the Du
2 CONC ke of Albany, a title that was stripped from his grandfather Duke Carl Eduard of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha in 1919. After the unification of West Germany and East Germany, Ernst-Leopold relocated to Limbach-Oberfrohna; there he establ
2 CONC ished a property development and consulting company. A German baron, who was a friend of the family, recalled: "He tried to build up that business in Saxony, got taken for a ride by the sharks out there and got trapped in a hopele
2 CONC ss financial situation. He was living beyond his means." Ernst-Leopold was hopeful that he could gain restitution of properties that had been seized by the Soviets. However, as the elderly baron remembered, "He got nothing, becaus
2 CONC e the government has recognised the Soviet robbery and, anyway, he is well out of the line of succession. That's because under the law he and his children are allowed to call themselves princes and princess but they have no inheri
2 CONC tance rights where the dukedom is concerned because his father [Johann Leopold] married wrong. She [Feodore] was an excellent woman but she wasn't a princess. Ernst Leopold made a mess of most things. His suicide was the only way 
2 CONC out of desperation, out of a hopeless financial, economic and personal situation." 
2 CONT
2 CONT After learning of the tragic death of his first cousin, Prince Andreas of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha stated: "He had a complex about this for years. He always had problems. His last known residence was near Chemnitz. He bought up some 
2 CONC property that wasn't very successful. So I imagine he had some problems. Some people say he got into a lot of debt. It's hard to say. He had a lot of ups and downs in his life." "...
2 CONT
