1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ====Golden Fleece - Grand Masters ====
2 CONT ====He was the 1,165th Knight of the Order of the Golden Fleece in Spain====
2 CONT Links:
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://www.thepeerage.com/p10108.htm#i101079 The Peerage:]
2 CONT * [http://www.geneall.net/H/per_page.php?id=6785 Geneall]
2 CONT * [http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_de_Borb%C3%B3nhttp://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infante_Juan,_Count_of_Barcelona Wikipedia: Spanish]
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infante_Juan,_Count_of_Barcelona Wikipedia - English] Infante Juan, Count of Barcelona
2 CONT * From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT The Infante Don Juan of Spain, Count of Barcelona (Juan Carlos Teresa Silvestre Alfonso de Borbón y Battenberg) (La Granja, Segovia, June 20, 1913 – Pamplona, April 1, 1993), was the fourth son and designated heir of King Alfonso 
2 CONC XIII of Spain and Victoria Eugenie of Battenberg, the monarch replaced by the Second Spanish Republic, and father of King Juan Carlos, under whom a constitutional monarchy was restored. As King, he would have been Juan III of Spai
2 CONC n.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Early life and marriage===
2 CONT
2 CONT Juan was born at the Palace of San Ildefonso. His father was forced into exile when the Second Spanish Republic was proclaimed on April 14, 1931. Due to the renunciations of his brothers Alfonso of Spain, Prince of Asturias and In
2 CONC fante Jaime, Duke of Segovia Infante Juan was thus next in line to the Spanish throne. He thus received the title Prince of Asturias when he was serving with the British Royal Navy in Bombay.
2 CONT
2 CONT In March 1935 he passed his naval exams in gunnery and navigation, which would have entitled him to become a lieutenant in the Royal Navy if he gave up his Spanish nationality. This, however, he refused to do.
2 CONT
2 CONT He met his future wife at a party hosted by Victor Emmanuel III of Italy on the day before his sister (Infanta Beatriz) was to be married. He married HRH Princess Maria Mercedes of Bourbon-Two Sicilies (1910-2000), known in Spain 
2 CONC as Doña María de las Mercedes de Borbón Dos-Sicilias y Orleans, in Rome on October 12, 1935. On her marriage, she gained the title of Countess of Barcelona.
2 CONT
2 CONT Just before the birth of the Infante Juan Carlos, the Count of Barcelona decided to go hunting, with the doctor telling him and his wife that the future king would not be born for weeks. When he was told of the birth he drove to t
2 CONC he hospital so quickly that he broke an axle spring.
2 CONT
2 CONT They had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT HRH Infanta Doña Pilar, Duchess of Badajoz (born 1936)
2 CONT
2 CONT HM Don Juan Carlos I, King of Spain (born 1938)
2 CONT
2 CONT HRH Infanta Doña Margarita, Duchess of Soria, 2nd Duchess of Hernani (born 1939)
2 CONT
2 CONT HRH Infante Don Alfonso of Spain (Alfonso Cristino Teresa Angelo Francisco de Asis y Todos los Santos) (1941–1956)
2 CONT
2 CONT They lived in Cannes and Rome, and, with the outbreak of World War II, they moved to Lausanne to live with his mother, Queen Ena. Afterwards, they resided at Estoril, in Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Don Juan became heir to the Spanish throne after the renunciations of his two older brothers, Alfonso and Jaime, both in 1933. To assert his claim to the throne, he used the title of Count of Barcelona, a sovereign title associate
2 CONC d with the Spanish crown.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1936, his father sent him to enter Spain and participate in the uprising but, near the French border, General Mola arrested him and sent him back.
2 CONT
2 CONT When General Francisco Franco declared Spain to be a monarchy in 1947, he characterised it as a reinstitution. However, Franco was afraid that Don Juan would turn out to be too liberal and roll back the Falangist state. As a resul
2 CONC t, in 1969 Franco passed over Don Juan, who would have been king if the monarchy had continued uninterrupted, in favour of his son Juan Carlos, who Franco believed would be more likely to continue the Francoist state after his dea
2 CONC th. Juan Carlos surprised many by his support of democratising Spain. However, Franco and the Count of Barcelona did not have a good relationship, with the count constantly pressing Franco to restore the monarchy. Relations soured
2 CONC  further when Don Juan called Franco an "illegitimate usurper", while Franco claimed he had a stronger claim to rule Spain than did Don Juan.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Count of Barcelona formally renounced his claim to the throne in 1977, forty-six years after Spain had been declared a republic, eight years after being deposed by Franco, and two years after his son had become King Juan Carlo
2 CONC s. In return, his son officially granted him the title of Count of Barcelona, which he had claimed for so long.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is buried as Juan III (John III of Spain), with royal honours, in the Royal Crypt of the monastery of San Lorenzo del Escorial, near Madrid. His wife survived him for seven years.
2 CONT
2 CONT His mother was a granddaughter of Queen Victoria and he was therefore a second cousin to Edward VIII and George VI.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was fond of the sea, and joined the Naval School at San Fernando, Cádiz, and had tattoos of a marine theme from his time in the British Royal Navy.
2 CONT
2 CONT PVD's 27th cousin thrice removed
2 CONT Claim to the Spanish throne
2 CONT
2 CONT Juan became heir-apparent to the Spanish throne after the renunciations of his two older brothers, Alfonso and Jaime, both in 1933. To assert his claim to the throne, after his father's death he used the title of Count of Barcelon
2 CONC a, a sovereign title associated with the Spanish crown.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1936, his father sent him to enter Spain and participate in the uprising but, near the French border, General Mola arrested him and sent him back.
2 CONT
2 CONT When General Francisco Franco declared Spain to be a kingdom in 1947, he characterised it as a reinstitution. However, Franco was afraid that Don Juan would turn out to be too liberal and roll back the Falangist state. As a result
2 CONC , in 1969 Franco passed over Juan, who would have been King if the monarchy had continued uninterrupted, in favour of his son Juan Carlos, who Franco believed would be more likely to continue the Francoist State after his death. J
2 CONC uan Carlos later surprised many by his support of democratising Spain. Franco and the Count of Barcelona did not have a good relationship, with the Count constantly pressing Franco to restore the monarchy. Relations soured further
2 CONC  when Juan called Franco an "illegitimate usurper", while Franco claimed he had a stronger claim to rule Spain than did Juan.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Count of Barcelona formally renounced his rights to the Crown eight years after being displaced as recognized heir to the throne by Franco, and two years after his son Juan Carlos had become King. In return, his son officially
2 CONC  granted him the title of Count of Barcelona, which he had claimed for so long.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was buried as Juan III (John III of Spain), with honours due a King, in the Royal Crypt of the monastery of San Lorenzo del Escorial, near Madrid. His wife survived him for seven years.
2 CONT
2 CONT His mother was a granddaughter of Queen Victoria and he was therefore a second cousin to Edward VIII and George VI.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was fond of the sea, and joined the Naval School at San Fernando, Cádiz, and had tattoos of a marine theme from his time in the British Royal Navy.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''He was the 1,165th Knight of the Order of the Golden Fleece in Spain.''' 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Kuningas Juan carlos I isä ja Barcelonan kreivi vuodesta 1977
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT conde de Barcelona
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Infante Juan of Spain, Count of Barcelona, was the third son and designated heir of King Alfonso XIII of Spain and Victoria Eugenie of Battenberg. His father was replaced by the Second Spanish Republic, and under his son, Juan Car
2 CONC los I, a constitutional monarchy was restored.
2 CONT
2 CONT Juan was born at the Palace of San Ildefonso. His father was forced into exile when the Second Spanish Republic was proclaimed on 14 April 1931. Owing to the renunciations of his brothers Alfonso of Spain, Prince of Asturias, and 
2 CONC Infante Jaime, Duke of Segovia, Infante Juan was thus next in line to the defunct Spanish throne. He thus received the title Prince of Asturias when he was serving with the British Royal Navy in Bombay.
2 CONT
2 CONT In March 1935, he passed his naval exams in gunnery and navigation, which would have entitled him to become a lieutenant in the Royal Navy if he gave up his Spanish nationality. This, however, he refused to do.
2 CONT
2 CONT He met his future wife at a party hosted by Victor Emmanuel III of Italy on the day before his sister (Infanta Beatriz) was to be married. He married Princess María Mercedes of Bourbon-Two Sicilies (1910–2000), known in Spain as D
2 CONC oña María de las Mercedes de Borbón Dos-Sicilias y Orleans, in Rome on 12 October 1935.
2 CONT
2 CONT Just before the birth of the Infante Juan Carlos, the Count of Barcelona decided to go hunting, with the doctor telling him and his wife that the future king would not be born for weeks. When he was told of the birth, he drove to 
2 CONC the hospital so quickly that he broke an axle spring.
2 CONT
2 CONT They had four children, ten grandchildren and seventeen great-grandchildren:
2 CONT
2 CONT Infanta Pilar, Duchess of Badajoz (born 30 July 1936) she married Luis Gomez-Acebo y de Estrada, Vizconde de la Torre on 6 May 1967. They have five children and nine grandchildren. Juan Carlos I of Spain (born 5 January 1938) he m
2 CONC arried Princess Sophia of Greece and Denmark on 14 May 1962. They have three children and eight grandchildren. Infanta Margarita, Duchess of Soria (born 6 March 1939) she married Don Carlos Zurita y Delgado on 12 October 1972. The
2 CONC y have two children. Infante Alfonso of Spain (3 October 1941 – 29 March 1956) They lived in Cannes and Rome, and, with the outbreak of World War II, they moved to Lausanne to live with his mother, Queen Victoria Eugenie. Afterwar
2 CONC ds, they resided at Estoril, in Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Don Juan became heir-apparent to the defunct Spanish throne after the renunciations of his two older brothers, Alfonso and Jaime, both in 1933. To assert his claim to the throne, after his father's death he used the title of Count
2 CONC  of Barcelona, a sovereign title associated with the Spanish crown.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1936, his father sent him to enter Spain and participate in the uprising but, near the French border, General Mola arrested him and sent him back.
2 CONT
2 CONT When General Francisco Franco declared Spain a monarchy in 1947, he characterised it as a restoration. However, Franco was afraid that Don Juan would turn out to be too liberal and roll back the Falangist state. As a result, in 19
2 CONC 69, Franco passed over Don Juan, who would have been king if the monarchy had continued uninterrupted, in favour of his son Juan Carlos, who Franco believed would be more likely to continue the Francoist State after his death. Don
2 CONC  Juan Carlos later surprised many by his support of democratising Spain. Franco and Don Juan did not have a good relationship, with the Count constantly pressing Franco to restore the monarchy. Relations soured further when Juan c
2 CONC alled Franco an "illegitimate usurper", while Franco claimed he had a stronger claim to rule Spain than did Don Juan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Don Juan formally renounced his rights to the Crown eight years after being displaced as recognised heir to the throne by Franco, and two years after his son Don Juan Carlos had become king. In return, his son officially granted h
2 CONC im the title of Count of Barcelona, which he had claimed for so long.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was buried with honours due a King, in the Royal Crypt of the monastery of San Lorenzo del Escorial, near Madrid. His wife survived him by seven years.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was fond of the sea, and joined the Naval School at San Fernando, Cádiz, and had tattoos of a marine theme from his time in the British Royal Navy.
