1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://sussexhistoryforum.co.uk/index.php?topic=12908.0 Sheffield Daily Telegraph - Thursday 21 June 1928
2 CONT
2 CONT FUNERAL OF CAPTAIN VICKERS.
2 CONT
2 CONT Daring Exploits in War as Airman Recalled. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The funeral of Captain Oliver Henry Douglas Vickers, who died in a nursing home from septic pneumonia, took place yesterday at Brookwood.    The son of Mr. Douglas Vickers and the Hon. Mrs. Vickers, Captain Vickers left Eton at th
2 CONC e age of seventeen to learn flying at Hendon, took his "ticket" in ten days, and was soon afterwards fighting in France. He accounted for 11 enemy machines between June 6th and August 17th, 1917. He and his observer, Lieutenant Ho
2 CONC ne, fought single-handed an engagement 12 miles over the German line with 11 machines. They accounted for five, and just managed to reach our lines.  At the funeral yesterday there were present the late  Captain's father, Mr. Doug
2 CONC las Vickers, his mother, and brother, Mr. Angus Vickers. Sir Trevor Dawson and other directors of Vickers, Ltd., with representatives from the aviation department of the firm were also present.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p5049.htm#i50489 Captain Oliver Henry Douglas Vickers was born on 13 September 1898. He was the son of Douglas Vickers and Hon. Katharine Adelaide Chetwynd. He married Barbara Kathleen Wallace, daughter o
2 CONC f Falconer Lewis Wallace and Kathleen Annie Paget, on 29 April 1920.1 He died on 17 June 1928 at age 29.  He lived at Tulloch, Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Probate:  Vickers Oliver Henry Douglas of the Weybridge Club Weybridge Surrey and of Temple Dinsley Hitchin Hertfordshire died 17 June 1928 at Kiama Nursing Home Queen's Road Weybridge Surrey Administration London 29 November to D
2 CONC ouglas Vickers gentleman.  Effects 23049 pounds 4s. 8d.
