1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Links:==
2 CONT
2 CONT *[http://www.thepeerage.com/p10078.htm#i100771 The Peerage]
2 CONT *[http://www.geneall.net/W/per_page.php?id=6368 Geneall]
2 CONT *[http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GScid=1984365&GRid=9479278& Find a Grave]
2 CONT *'''Wikipedia:''' [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gustaf_VI_Adolf_of_Sweden English] [http://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gustaf_VI_Adolf Svenska]
2 CONT *[http://www.hansdenyngre.dk/hans_uk/wizg84.htm#711 Johannt he Younger #2829] + [http://www.hansdenyngre.dk/hans_uk/wizg63.htm#769 #2989]
2 CONT *'''King of Sweden:''' Reign 29 October 1950 – 15 September 1973
2 CONT >'''Predecessor:''' [http://www.geni.com/people/Kung-Gustaf-V-av-Sverige/4209146 Gustaf V] '''Successor:''' [http://www.geni.com/people/Carl-XVI-Gustaf-av-Sverige/4111915 Carl XVI Gustaf]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Gustaf VI Adolf was King of Sweden from 29 October 1950 until his death. He was the eldest son of King Gustaf V and his wife, Victoria of Baden, and had been Crown Prince of Sweden for the preceding 43 years in the reign of his fa
2 CONC ther.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gustaf VI Adolf was a lifelong amateur archeologist particularly interested in Ancient Italian cultures.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born at the Royal Palace in Stockholm and at birth created Duke of Scania. A patrilineal member of the Bernadotte family, he was also a descendant of the House of Vasa through maternal lines. Through his mother, Victoria, h
2 CONC e was a descendant of Gustav IV Adolf of Sweden of the deposed House of Holstein-Gottorp.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gustaf Adolf became Crown Prince of Sweden on 8 December 1907, on the death of his grandfather, King Oscar II.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1938 he was elected an honorary member of the Virginia Society of the Cincinnati.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 29 October 1950, Crown Prince Gustaf Adolf became king at age 67 upon the death of his father, King Gustaf V. He was at the time the world's oldest heir apparent to a monarchy (this in turn was broken by Charles, Prince of Wale
2 CONC s on 2 November 2016). His personal motto was Plikten framför allt, "Duty before all".
2 CONT
2 CONT During Gustaf VI Adolf's reign, work was underway on a new Instrument of Government to replace the 1809 constitution and produce reforms consistent with the times. Among the reforms sought by some Swedes was the replacement of the
2 CONC  monarchy or at least some moderation of the old constitution's provision that "The King alone shall govern the realm."
2 CONT
2 CONT Gustaf VI Adolf's personal qualities made him popular among the Swedish people and, in turn, this popularity led to strong public opinion in favour of the retention of the monarchy. Gustaf VI Adolf's expertise and interest in a wi
2 CONC de range of fields (architecture and botany being but two) made him respected, as did his informal and modest nature and his purposeful avoidance of pomp. While the monarchy had been de facto subordinate to the Riksdag and ministe
2 CONC rs since 1917, the king still nominally retained considerable reserve powers. Even these nominal powers were removed when Sweden's constitutional reform became complete in 1975, thus making Gustaf Adolf the last monarch to wield e
2 CONC ven nominal political power.
2 CONT
2 CONT The King died in 1973, ten weeks shy of his 91st birthday, at the old hospital in Helsingborg, Scania, close to his summer residence, Sofiero Castle, after a deterioration in his health that culminated in pneumonia. He was succeed
2 CONC ed on the throne by his 27-year-old grandson Carl XVI Gustaf, son of the late Prince Gustaf Adolf. He died the day before the election of 1973, which is suggested to have swayed it in support of the incumbent Social Democratic gov
2 CONC ernment. In a break with tradition, he was not buried in Riddarholmskyrkan in Stockholm, but in the Royal Cemetery in Haga alongside his two deceased wives. He was the last surviving son of Gustaf V.
2 CONT
2 CONT Crown Prince Gustaf Adolf meets some English footballers (c. 1910–1915).
2 CONT The King's reputation as a "professional amateur professor" was widely known; nationally and internationally, and among his relatives. Gustaf VI Adolf was a devoted archaeologist, and was admitted to the British Academy for his wo
2 CONC rk in botany in 1958. Gustaf VI Adolf participated in archaeological expeditions in China, Greece, Korea and Italy, and founded the Swedish Institute in Rome.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gustaf VI Adolf had an enormous private library consisting of 80 000 volumes and – nearly more impressively – he actually had read the main part of the books. He had an interest in specialist literature on Chinese art and East Asi
2 CONC an history. Throughout his life, King Gustaf VI Adolf was particularly interested in the history of civilization, and he participated in several archaeological expeditions. His other great area of interest was botany, concentratin
2 CONC g in flowers and gardening. He was considered an expert on the Rhododendron flower. At Sofiero Castle (the king's summer residence) he created one of the very finest Rhododendron collections.
2 CONT
2 CONT Like his sons, Prince Gustaf Adolf and Prince Bertil, Gustaf VI Adolf maintained wide, lifelong interests in sports. He enjoyed tennis and golf, and fly fishing for charity. He was president of the Swedish Olympic Committee and th
2 CONC e Swedish Sports Confederation from their foundations and until 1933, and these positions were then taken over by his sons in succession, Gustaf Adolf until 1947 and then Bertil until 1997.
2 CONT
2 CONT Then Crown Prince Gustaf Adolf with his first wife Crown Princess Margareta and children in 1912. Gustaf Adolf married Princess Margaret of Connaught on 15 June 1905 in St. George's Chapel, at Windsor Castle. Princess Margaret was
2 CONC  the daughter of Prince Arthur, Duke of Connaught, third son of Queen Victoria and Prince Albert of the United Kingdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Gustaf VI Adolf and Crown Princess Margaret of Sweden had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Name	Birth	Death	Notes Prince Gustaf Adolf, Duke of Västerbotten	22 April 1906	26 January 1947 (aged 40)	died in a plane crash at Copenhagen Airport, father of King Carl XVI Gustaf of Sweden Prince Sigvard, Duke of Uppland	7 June 
2 CONC 1907	4 February 2002 (aged 94)	later Sigvard Count of Wisborg Princess Ingrid	28 March 1910	7 November 2000 (aged 90)	later Queen of Denmark; wife of Frederick IX of Denmark and mother of the present Queen Margrethe II of Denmark 
2 CONC and Queen Anne-Marie of Greece Prince Bertil, Duke of Halland	28 February 1912	5 January 1997 (aged 84)	married Lillian Davies, no issue Prince Carl Johan, Duke of Dalarna	31 October 1916	5 May 2012 (aged 95)	later Carl Johan Coun
2 CONC t of Wisborg. Crown Princess Margaret died suddenly on 1 May 1920 of an infection following surgery. At the time, she was eight months pregnant and expecting her sixth child.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gustaf Adolf married Lady Louise Mountbatten, formerly Princess Louise of Battenberg, on 3 November 1923 at St. James's Palace. She was the sister of Lord Mountbatten and aunt of Prince Philip, Duke of Edinburgh. It was Lady Louis
2 CONC e who became Queen of Sweden. Both Queen Louise and her stepchildren were great-grandchildren of Queen Victoria of the United Kingdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT His second marriage produced only one stillborn daughter on 30 May 1925.
2 CONT
2 CONT While his first wife visited her native Britain in the early years of their marriage, it was widely rumored in Sweden that Gustaf Adolf had an affair there with operetta star Rosa Grünberg. Swedish vocalist Carl E. Olivebring (191
2 CONC 9–2002) in a press interview claimed to be an extramarital son of Gustaf VI Adolf, a claim taken seriously by the king's biographer Kjell Fridh (1944–1998).
2 CONT
2 CONT King Gustaf VI Adolf of Sweden was the grandfather of his direct successor King Carl XVI Gustaf of Sweden, of Queen Margrethe II of Denmark and also of former Queen Anne-Marie of Greece.
