1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT Ref https://hansentreefarm.com/history.html
2 CONT Ref https://hansentreefarm.com/hansen_family.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/145988207/henry-leo-hansen
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:MW15-SQB
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref https://www.startribune.com/obituaries/detail/5558903/
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Hansen Henry L. Hansen, 92, of St. Paul. Professor of forestry at the U of M from 1946-1982. Preceeded in death by Norwegian immigrant parents Hannah and Louis Hansen. Survived by wife Charlotte; three sons, Trygg of Jim Falls, Wi
2 CONC s, David (Karen Lilley), and Mark (Brenda), both of St. Paul; grandchildren Trygve, Britta, Per, Kip and Kell; and great- grandchild Chloe. Henry was an outstanding scholar; valedictorian of his St. Cloud Tech high school class at
2 CONC  age 16 and a straight "A" student at the U of M, receiving his BS in 1935 and Ph.D. in 1946, both in Forestry. A faculty member first at the U of M, he briefly taught at Michigan State University and Clemson College before coming
2 CONC  back to the "U". He taught thousands of students to understand, love and care for Minnesota's woods. Henry believed in physical, mental and spiritual health. He was a high school pole vaulter, U of M wrestler, cross country skier
2 CONC , sailor, and until age 86 an avid swimmer. He loved to read, everything from Horatio Hornblower to Kipling, and was also a crossword puzzle whiz. He was a skilled writer; as editor of his high school newspaper, for the Minnesota 
2 CONC Daily, and during his career interpreting his research for the general public. He was vice- president of his church, a Sunday school teacher and Boy Scout leader. Henry led a vigorous life. To finish his college education during t
2 CONC he Depression he helped map land and forests in the Boundary Waters for the US Forest Service. During WWII he was an inspector for the Army Air Corps glider program, and later an aerial gunnery instructor as a Navy Lieutenant. He 
2 CONC started Hansen Tree Farm on the sand plain outside Anoka, now a thriving forest and Christmas tree farm run by his children and grandchildren. He loved his summers with friends and family at Itasca State Park and Leech Lake, made 
2 CONC and over a dozen trips to Norway to visit relatives and learn Scandinavian models of wildland management. His research and teaching covered many areas of forest ecology, focusing on regeneration of Minnesota's pine forests. He bel
2 CONC ieved in the use of forests for recreation, timber and wildlife. He was a founding force for the Minnesota State Scientific and Natural Areas, the University's Quetico- Superior Wilderness Research Center, and forest ecology resea
2 CONC rch at Itasca State Park, Isle Royale National Park and many others. His impact is now visible, to this and future generations. Memorial service Saturday, May 14; visitation 10 a.m., service 11 a.m., St. Anthony Park Lutheran Chur
2 CONC ch, 2323 Como Ave., St. Paul. Memorials preferred to: Hansen Forest Ecology Fellowship - Fund #5613. U of M - CNR, 2003 Upper Buford Circle, St. Paul, MN 55108-6146.
2 CONT
2 CONT Published on May 8, 2005
2 CONT
