1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''From [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Crichton-Stuart,_4th_Marquess_of_Bute Wikipedia - John Crichton-Stuart, 4th Marquess of Bute]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT [Children of] John Crichton-Stuart, 4th Marquess of Bute, KT (20 June 1881 – 25 April 1947) and [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augusta_Crichton-Stuart,_Marchioness_of_Bute Augusta Mary Monica Crichton-Stuart, Marchioness of Bute, 
2 CONC DBE, DStJ (19 August 1880 – 16 May 1947)]
2 CONT
2 CONT [5th child] Lord David Crichton-Stuart (8 February 1911 – 3 March 1970); married Ursula Packe and had issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/information.pl?cite=1M4JACnD7e3ksT2ajvSCmw&scan=1 FreeBMD: Registration of birth of David Stuart in 1911]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT January to March 1911: Registration of birth of David Stuart; in Cardiff (Volume 11a, Page 418)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0001652/19110208/051/0008 British Newspaper Archive: Globe Wednesday, 8 February 1911 Page 8 Marchioness of Bute]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The Marchioness of Bute gave birth to a son at [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cardiff_Castle Cardiff Castle, Castle Street, Cardiff, Wales CF10 3RB 51.4823, -3.1812] today, Wednesday, 8 February 1911. Both are doing well. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Marchioness, who now has three sons and two daughters, is the second daughter of Alan Henry Bellingham, and was married to the Marquess in 1905.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://archive.org/details/scottishbirds5196869scot/page/206/mode/2up?q=%22Lord+David+Stuart%22&view=theater Scottish birds by Scottish Ornithologists' Club Volume 5 No 1 Published 1968 Pages 204-206]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT [Page 204] Birds of the Scar Rocks — the Wigtownshire gannetry by John G. Young
2 CONT
2 CONT Introduction: The Scar Rocks lie in Luce Bay, Wigtownshire (54.68333, -4.00019),...
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1883 Robert Service of Dumfries found two Gannets' nests on Big Scar, one containing a broken egg. Thereafter it was not until 1939 that the late Rev. John Morell McWilliam [Page 206] and Lord David Stuart discovered that Ganne
2 CONC ts were again breeding on this rock (British Birds [Volume?] 33 [Page?] 105).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0001851/19400403/023/0022 British Newspaper Archive: The Bystander Wednesday, 3 April 1940 Page 22 Mrs. Ursula Clifton]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Ursula Clifton is to marry Lord David Crichton-Stuart, third son of the Marquess and Marchioness of Bute. She is the younger daughter of Sir Edward and the Hon. Lady Packe, of Prestwold Hall, Loughborough. Her first marriage 
2 CONC took place in 1934, and was annulled on her petition two years later. Her grandfather was the first and last Lord Colebrooke.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.scotlandspeople.gov.uk/search-records/statutory-records/stat_marriages#form&surname=Crichton%20Stuart&surname_so=exact&forename=David&forename_so=exact&spsurname_so=exact&spforename=Ursula%20Sybil&spforename_s
2 CONC o=exact&from_year=1911&to_year=1970&record_type=stat_marriages Scotland's People: Marriages]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1940 marriage of David Crichton Stuart to Ursula Sybil Packe, in the district of St Andrew (Edinburgh)
2 CONT * 1940 marriage of David Crichton Stuart to Ursula Sybil Clifton, in the district of St Andrew (Edinburgh)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000564/19400525/083/0004 British Newspaper Archive: Dundee Courier Saturday, 25 May 1940 Page 4 Hush-hush wedding of Scots marquis's son]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord David Crichton-Stuart, son of the Marquis of Bute, was married yesterday, Friday, 24 May 1940, in an Edinburgh solicitor's office to Mrs Ursula Sybil Packe or Clifton, of Prestwold Hall, Loughborough [https://en.wikipedia.org
2 CONC /wiki/Prestwold_Hall Prestwold Hall, Prestwold, Leicestershire LE12 5SQ 52.7876981, -1.1426585]. Great care was taken to keep the wedding secret, and the couple arrived at the Sheriff Court shortly afterwards, almost unnoticed, fo
2 CONC r confirmation of the marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord David, who is 29, is described as a landed proprietor of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ascog Ascog, Isle of Bute, Argyll and Bute 55.825, -5.022]. His wife is a daughter of Sir Edward Packe, company director, and Lady Mary S
2 CONC ydney Packe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord David is the last of the Marquis of Bute's seven children to marry, and the first of his five sons to chose an English bride. Lord Bute's heir, the Earl of Dumfries, married the daughter of an Irish peer, and his three brothe
2 CONC rs' wives are respectively Scottish, Swedish, and Dutch.
2 CONT
2 CONT Yesterday's bride formerly married Mr Peter Clifton, of the Grenadier Guards, in 1934, and the marriage was dissolved two years later. Her mother, Lady Packe, is the daughter of the first Baron Colebrooke.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/information.pl?cite=4PRbhhkO12COl18IdPKytA&scan=1 FreeBMD - Registration of death of David (Lord) Stuart in 1970]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT January to March 1970: Registration of death of David (Lord) Stuart; born 8 February 1911 [aged 59]; in Chelsea (Volume 5a, Page 1809)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.ancestry.co.uk/imageviewer/collections/1904/images/48589_83024005506_3471-00599 Ancestry: 1970 probate Lord David Stuart]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 October 1970 probate of Lord David Stuart of Old Place of Mochrum, Wigtownshire, who died 3 March 1970, probate granted (confirmation) at London on 5 October 1970; Duplicate sealed 23 November 1970 and 4 and 7 December 1970.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://archive.org/details/connoisseurillus179lond The connoisseur by an illustrated magazine for collectors Volume 179 Published March 1972]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT For their section on Scottish woodware the authors have been able to incorporate the findings of twenty years' study of the subject by Lord David Stuart which he generously placed at their disposal. The result is the first detaile
2 CONC d account of Scottish pen-and-ink, tartan and transfer ware which affected a wide range of small articles, but not furniture, and achieved export sales as far apart as the United States and Australia. There is a particularly inter
2 CONC esting account of the Smiths of Mauchline, the most prominent firm in the industry, which comes from the author's own researches.
