1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rupert_Hart-Davis
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sir Rupert Charles Hart-Davis''' (28 August 1907 – 8 December 1999) was an English publisher, editor and man of letters. He founded the publishing company Rupert Hart-Davis Ltd. As a biographer, he is remembered for his Hugh Wa
2 CONC lpole (1952), as an editor, for his Collected Letters of Oscar Wilde (1962), and, as both editor and part-author, for the Lyttelton/Hart-Davis Letters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Working at a publishing firm before World War II, Hart-Davis began to forge literary relationships that would be important later in his career. Founding his publishing company in 1946, Hart-Davis was praised for the quality of the
2 CONC  firm's publications and production; but he refused to cater to public tastes, and the firm eventually lost money. After relinquishing control of the firm, Hart-Davis concentrated on writing and editing, producing collections of l
2 CONC etters and other works which brought him the sobriquet "the king of editors."
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Early years''':
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Rupert Hart-Davis''' was born in Kensington, London. He was legally the son of Richard Hart-Davis, a stockbroker, and his wife Sybil née Cooper, but by the time of his conception the couple were estranged, though still living t
2 CONC ogether, and Sybil Hart-Davis had many lovers at that period. Hart-Davis believed the most likely candidate for his natural father to be a Yorkshire banker called Gervase Beckett.[1] Hart-Davis was educated at Eton and Balliol Col
2 CONC lege, Oxford, though he found university life not to his taste and left after less than a year.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Hart-Davis decided to become an actor, and he studied at The Old Vic, where he came to realise that he was not a talented enough actor to succeed, and he turned instead to publishing in 1929, joining William Heinemann Ltd. as an o
2 CONC ffice boy and assistant to the managing director Charley Evans. He spent two years with Heinemann and a year as manager of the Book Society; during this period, he built up good relationships with a number of authors and was able 
2 CONC to negotiate a directorship for himself at Jonathan Cape Ltd.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT In his seven years with Cape, Hart-Davis recruited a successful group of authors ranging from the poets William Plomer, Cecil Day-Lewis, Edmund Blunden and Robert Frost, to the humorist Beachcomber. He was well placed to secure Du
2 CONC ff Cooper's life of Talleyrand, as Cooper was his uncle.[2] As the junior partner at Cape, he had to handle their difficult authors including Robert Graves, Wyndham Lewis and Arthur Ransome, the last being seen as difficult becaus
2 CONC e of his wife Genia, with her "distrustfulness, venom and guile". Hart-Davis was a close friend of Ransome, sharing an enthusiasm for cricket and rugby (but not fishing). After Cape's death he commented to George Lyttelton that Ca
2 CONC pe had been "one of the tightest-fisted old bastards I've ever encountered".[3] The second partner, Wren Howard, was "even tighter" than Cape,[3] and neither of them liked fraternising with authors, which they left to Hart-Davis.
2 CONT
2 CONT In World War II Hart-Davis volunteered for military service as a private soldier, but was soon commissioned into the Coldstream Guards. He did not see active service, never being stationed more than 25 miles from London.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Independent publisher===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Main article: Publications by Rupert Hart-Davis'''
2 CONT
2 CONT After the war, Hart-Davis was unable to obtain satisfactory terms from Jonathan Cape to return to the company, and in 1946 he struck out on his own, founding Rupert Hart-Davis Ltd, in partnership with David Garnett and Teddy Young
2 CONC  and with financial backing from Eric Linklater, Arthur Ransome, H. E. Bates, Geoffrey Keynes, and Celia and Peter Fleming. His own literary tastes dictated which books were accepted and which rejected. Frequently he turned down c
2 CONC ommercial successes because he thought little of the works' literary merit. He later said, "I usually found that the sales of the books I published were in inverse ratio to my opinion of them. That's why I established some sort of
2 CONC  reputation without making any money."[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1946 paper was still rationed; the firm used Garnett's ex-serviceman's ration, but as only one ex-serviceman's ration could be used per firm it could not use that of Hart-Davis. However, the firm was given the allocation at cos
2 CONC t of a Glasgow bookseller and occasional prewar publisher, Alan Jackson. The partners decided to start initially with reprints of dead authors, as if a new book became a best-seller the firm would not have paper for a reprint and 
2 CONC the author might leave. They made an exception for Stephen Potter's Gamesmanship which was a short book, collected every ream of paper they could buy and printed 25,000 copies. Likewise 25,000 copies of Eric Linklater’s Sealskin T
2 CONC rousers (five short stories) were printed.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT The firm had best-sellers such as Gamesmanship and Heinrich Harrer's Seven Years in Tibet, which sold more than 200,000 copies.[7] Also in the early years Hart-Davis secured Ray Bradbury for his firm, recognising the quality of a 
2 CONC science fiction author who also wrote poetry.[8] Other good sellers were Peter Fleming, Eric Linklater and Gerald Durrell; but best-sellers were too few, and though the output of Rupert-Hart-Davis Ltd was regularly praised for the
2 CONC  high quality of its printing and binding, that too was an expense that weighed the company down.[9] A further expense was added when G. M. Young's biography of Stanley Baldwin was published in 1952; both Winston Churchill and Lor
2 CONC d Beaverbrook threatened to sue if certain passages were not removed or amended. With the help of the lawyer Arnold Goodman an agreement was reached to replace the offending sentences, but the firm had the "hideously expensive" jo
2 CONC b of removing and replacing seven leaves from 7,580 copies.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT By the mid-fifties, Rupert Hart-Davis Ltd could no longer sustain an independent existence and in 1956 it was absorbed into the Heinemann group.[11] Heinemann sold the imprint to the American firm Harcourt Brace in 1961, who sold 
2 CONC it to the Granada Group in 1963, when Hart-Davis retired from publishing, though remaining as non-executive chairman until 1968.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Rupert Hart-Davis Ltd logo was a woodcut of a fox, with a background of oak leaves. The company was based at No. 36 Soho Square, London W1. Reprint series published over the years were the Reynard Library of great English writ
2 CONC ers and the Mariners Library of nautical books.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Author:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT As Hugh Walpole's literary executor, and being unable to find a potential biographer who would tackle the job to his satisfaction, Hart-Davis proposed to Walpole's publishers, Macmillan, that he should write the biography himself,
2 CONC  to which Harold Macmillan replied that he couldn't think of a better person to do it.[12] When Hugh Walpole was published in 1952, it was praised as "among the half dozen best biographies of the century".[13] It has been reissued
2 CONC  several times.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hart-Davis wrote no more books until after his retirement from publishing, but between 1955 and 1962, he wrote about a quarter of a million words to his old schoolmaster George Lyttelton, which, together with Lyttelton's similar c
2 CONC ontribution, made up the six volumes of the Lyttelton/Hart-Davis Letters, published between 1978 and 1984 after Lyttelton's death. Although he spent much of his life researching old letters, Hart-Davis destroyed the originals of t
2 CONC he letters after his edited versions of them had been printed.[14] He was equally unscholarly about his uncle Duff Cooper's diaries, whose frankness shocked him so much that he wanted to destroy them.[15]
2 CONT
2 CONT In retirement, Hart-Davis wrote three volumes of autobiography entitled The Arms of Time (1979), The Power of Chance (1991) and Halfway to Heaven (1998). The first, a particularly cherished project, was a memoir of his beloved mot
2 CONC her Sybil, who died young, to her son's desolation.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Editor:
2 CONT
2 CONT Hart-Davis was described by The Times as "the king of editors".[7] He edited volumes of the letters of the playwright Oscar Wilde, the writer and caricaturist Max Beerbohm, and the writer George Moore, as well as the diaries of th
2 CONC e poet Siegfried Sassoon and the autobiography of Arthur Ransome. A Beggar in Purple, his commonplace book, was published in 1983. Praise from the Past, a collection of tributes to writers, was published in 1996.
2 CONT
2 CONT His Complete Letters of Oscar Wilde, compiled over the same period as Hart-Davis's correspondence with George Lyttelton, was described in a review of the latter as "a mammoth undertaking whose difficulties and challenges are docum
2 CONC ented in great detail in the letters, giving a satisfying portrayal of what dedication in literary scholarship looks like from the inside".[16] Wilde's grandson, Merlin Holland, wrote, "It was his decision fifty years ago to publi
2 CONC sh the first edition of Oscar Wilde's letters which helped to put my grandfather back into the position which he lost in 1895 as one of the most charismatic and fascinating figures in English literary history."[17]
2 CONT
2 CONT In his last memoir, Hart-Davis listed the books he had edited as: 
2 CONT
2 CONT * The Second Omnibus Book (Heinemann) 1930; 
2 CONT * Then and Now (Cape) 1935; 
2 CONT * The Essential Neville Cardus (Cape) 1949; 
2 CONT * Cricket All His Life by E.V. Lucas (RHD Ltd) 1950; 
2 CONT * All in Due Time by Humphry House (RHD Ltd) 1955; 
2 CONT * George Moore: Letters to Lady Cunard 1895-1933 (RHD Ltd) 1957; 
2 CONT * The Letters of Oscar Wilde (RHD Ltd) 1962; 
2 CONT * Max Beerbohm: Letters to Reggie Turner (RHD Ltd) 1964; 
2 CONT * More Theatres by Max Beerbohm (RHD Ltd) 1969;
2 CONT *  Last Theatres by Max Beerbohm (RHD Ltd) 1970; 
2 CONT * A Peep into the Past by Max Beerbohm (Heinemann) 1972; 
2 CONT * A Catalogue of the Caricatures of Max Beerbohm (Macmillan) 1972 ; 
2 CONT * The Autobiography of Arthur Ransome (Cape) 1976; 
2 CONT * Electric Delights by William Plomer (Cape) 1978; S
2 CONT * elected Letters of Oscar Wilde (Oxford) 1979; 
2 CONT * Two Men of Letters (Michael Joseph) 1979; 
2 CONT * Siegfried Sassoon: Diaries 1920-1922 3 vols. (Faber) 1981-85; 
2 CONT * War Poems of Siegfried Sassoon (Faber) 1983; 
2 CONT * More Letters of Oscar Wilde (Murray) 1985; 
2 CONT * Siegfried Sassoon: Letters to Max Beerbohm (Faber) 1986; 
2 CONT * Letters of Max Beerbohm (Murray) 1986.[18]
2 CONT
2 CONT *
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Ancestry and personal life:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Hart-Davis was a great-great-great-grandson of William IV. William had several illegitimate children with his mistress, Mrs. Jordan. Their youngest daughter, Lady Elizabeth Fitzclarence, later Countess of Erroll, had daughters inc
2 CONC luding Lady Agnes Hay. Lady Agnes married James Duff, 5th Earl Fife and among their children was Lady Agnes Duff, who married Sir Alfred Cooper. Their children included Sybil Cooper, mother of Rupert Hart-Davis.[19]
2 CONT
2 CONT While still an actor, Hart-Davis met the young Peggy Ashcroft whom he married in 1929. The marriage was short-lived, ending in divorce in 1933, though the two remained warm friends until Ashcroft's death more than sixty years late
2 CONC r. In November 1933 he married Catherine Comfort Borden-Turner (1910–1970), with whom he had a daughter, Bridget, in 1935, and two sons, Duff in 1936, and Adam in 1943. The second marriage became dysfunctional, although husband an
2 CONC d wife remained on good terms and stayed together until their children were grown up, when Hart-Davis and Comfort divorced. In 1964 he married Ruth Simon, née Ware, with whom he had had a long-term relationship. After her death in
2 CONC  1967 he married June Williams in 1968, who outlived him.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the war, until his retirement, Hart-Davis lived during the week in a flat above his publishing business in Soho Square, returning to his main home at Bromsden Farm, Oxfordshire at weekends. He retired to Marske in North York
2 CONC shire, where he died at the age of 92.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Public service and honours:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1957 to 1969, Hart-Davis was chairman of the London Library. During this period, a financial crisis arose when Westminster City Council decided that the library should no longer qualify for charitable exemption from local pro
2 CONC perty tax. Hart-Davis organised fund-raising on a grand scale, including an auction, with E. M. Forster offering the manuscript of A Passage to India, and T. S. Eliot, a duplicate manuscript of The Waste Land.[2] Hart-Davis was al
2 CONC so secretary of The Literary Society and a member of A. P. Herbert's committee on censorship.[20]
2 CONT
2 CONT Public honours included honorary doctorates from the universities of Durham and Reading and a knighthood in 1967 for services to literature. Twenty-two books were dedicated to him between 1936 and 1998, including works by H. E. Ba
2 CONC tes, Edmund Blunden, C. Day Lewis, Ray Bradbury, Diana Cooper, Eric Linklater, Compton Mackenzie, Anthony Powell and Leon Edel.[21] Merlin Holland's Oscar Wilde: A Life in Letters (2003) was dedicated "To the memory of Rupert Hart
2 CONC -Davis, with love and gratitude."
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Notes:'''
