0 @N6000000005598973192@ NOTE Princess Anne, Princess Royal and Princess of Orange From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Anne, Princess Royal of Great Britain, Ireland, and Hanover and Princess of Orange-Nassau, (2 November 1709-12 January 1759) was the second child and eldest daughter of King George II of Great Britain and his consort, Queen Caroli
1 CONC ne. She was the wife of Prince Willem IV of Orange-Nassau, the first hereditary stadholder of the Netherlands (11 September 1711-22 October 1751). Princess Anne was the second daughter of a British Sovereign to hold the title Prin
1 CONC cess Royal.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her Serene Highness Princess Anne of Hanover, Duchess of Brunswick and Lüneburg was born at Schloss Herrenhausen, Hanover, Germany, five years before her paternal grandfather, the Elector Georg Ludwig, succeeeded to the British th
1 CONC rone as King George I. Upon her grandfather's ascension in 1714, it became established practice that the legitimate children and the male-line grandchildren of a British Sovereign would be titled prince or princess of Great Britai
1 CONC n and Ireland and styled Royal Highness; great grandchildren in the male line would be prince or princess of Great Britain and Ireland and styled Highness. Her father became king on 11 June 1727. On 30 August of that year, George 
1 CONC II created his eldest daughter Princess Royal. King Charles I first bostowed this title on his daughter, Mary Stuart (the mother of William III of England), in 1642. However, the title fell from use until the reign of George II. (
1 CONC Princess Anne became Princess Royal during the lifetime of her aunt, Queen Sophea Dorothea of Prussia, who had been eligible for this honor but did not receive it.) On 25 March 1734, the Princess Royal married Prince Willem IV of 
1 CONC Orange-Nassau at the Chapel Royal, St. James's Palace, London. The groom was the son of Prince Johan Willem Friso of Orange-Nassau, the stadholder of Friesland and the Friesian claimant to the leadership of the United Provinces, a
1 CONC nd of Langravine Maria of Hesse-Cassel (1688-1765). He became stadholder of Groningen in 1718; Drenthe and Guelders in 1722; and Zealand, Holland, Utrecht and Overijssel in 1741. In an effort to quell internal strife amongst the v
1 CONC arious factions, in 1748, the States-General selected Willem IV as their stadholder, making it a hereditary position.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Prince and Princess of Orange-Nassau had the following children: 1. a stillborn son in 1735 2. a stillborn daughter in 1736 3. a stillborn daughter 1739 4. Wilhlemina Carolina (1743-1787); married 1760, Duke Karl Christian of 
1 CONC Nassau-Weilburg (1735-1788), and had issue. 4. Anna (b. and d. 1746) 6. Willem V (Batavus) (1748-1806), Prince of Orange-Nassau, stadholder of the Netherlands (1751-1795, deposed); married 1767, Princess Wilhelmina of Prussia (175
1 CONC 1-1820), and had issue.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Princess Royal Anne died at the Hague, Holland.
