0 @N6000000005598992259@ NOTE Marie Sophie Frederikke From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Marie Sophie Frederikke, Queen Consort of Denmark and Norway, was born 1767 and died 1852. She was born as 'Marie Sophie Friederike von Hessen-Kassel', the eldest child of Prince Charles of Hesse-Cassel and Princess Louise of Denm
1 CONC ark.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her paternal grandparents were Landgrave Frederick II of Hesse-Cassel and Princess Mary of Great Britain. Mary was a daughter of George II of Great Britain and Caroline of Ansbach. Her maternal grandparents were Frederick V of Den
1 CONC mark and Louise of Great Britain and Ireland, another daughter of George II of Great Britain and Caroline of Ansbach. Her father was the second son of the Landgrave (= sovereign prince of Kassel), and as such, had no own principal
1 CONC ity. Thus he acted in several such positions that were offered to cadet members of royal houses by their reigning relatives. Denmark was offering more and better positions than the small Cassel.
1 CONT
1 CONT She grew up largely in Denmark, where her father held remarkable positions, such as governorate of provinces. Her mother was third and youngest daughter of king Frederick V of Denmark and his consort, Louise of Great Britain. As s
1 CONC uch, she was niece of King Christian VII and of Prince Regent Frederik, as well as first cousin of Regent, Crown Prince Frederik, the Danish rulers of that period.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1790 she married her first cousin, Crown Prince Frederik of Denmark (1768-1839), then Regent of the Realm, the future king Frederick VI of Denmark. Her husband had been since 1784 (16 years old) the Regent on behalf of his insane 
1 CONC father Christian VII of Denmark, who died 1808. The royal couple assumed the throne officially only upon king Christian's death, having acted as de facto monarchs already some couple of decades. In the aftermath of the defeat of D
1 CONC enmark's ally, Emperor Napoleon of the French, Denmark lost its dominion Norway, and the royal couple ceased to be Norway's king and queen in 1814.
1 CONT
1 CONT They had eight children. However, none of Frederick VI's sons survived infancy and when he died in 1839, he was succeeded by his cousin, Christian VIII of Denmark. The surviving children of King Frederick VI and Queen Marie Sophie
1 CONC  Frederikke were their two daughters: 1. Caroline of Denmark (1793-1881), married with her father's first cousin, Hereditary Prince Frederick Ferdinand of Denmark, d 1863 some months before his nephew Frederick VII of Denmark, Her
1 CONC editary Prince of Denmark, youngest son of Hereditary Prince and sometime Regent Frederik of Denmark and as such brother of Christian VIII of Denmark. Childless. 2. Wilhelmine of Denmark (1808-1891), firstly married with her secon
1 CONC d cousin Prince Frederik of Denmark, the future Frederick VII of Denmark, but they divorced, and she married secondly with Duke Charles of Lyksborg (Duke of Glucksburg), who was eldest brother of the future Christian IX of Denmark
1 CONC . Both her marriages were childless.
1 CONT
1 CONT Queen Marie Sophie Frederikke lamented the total lack of sons and of grandchildren of her own. When her youngest sister, Duchess Louise Caroline of Lyksborg became widow when most of her large brood of children yet very young, Que
1 CONC en Marie accepted some of the younger ones into her tutelage in the Royal Household. They were much younger than queen's own two surviving daughters, which implies that the queen felt like a grandmother. One such foster child of h
1 CONC ers was prince Christian of Lyksborg, born 1818, the future Christian IX of Denmark.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her titles were: 1767-90 Marie, Landgravine and/or Princess of Hesse, or of Hesse-Cassel 1790-1808 Marie, Crown Princess of Denmark 1808-14 Marie, Queen (Consort) of Denmark (and Norway) 1814-39 Marie, Queen (Consort) of Denmark 1
1 CONC 839-52 Marie, Queen Dowager of Denmark
0 @N6000000006127657222@ NOTE Maria Sofia Fredrika av Hessen-Kassel (1744-1836) föddes i Hanau i den tyska delstaten Hessen i Tyskland, den 28 oktober 1767 som dotter till lantgreven Karl av Hessen-Kassel (1744-1836) och Louise av Oldenburg (1750-1831). Hennes
1 CONC  morfar var dendanske kungen Fredrik V.Vid 22 års ålder, den 31 juli 1790, gifte hon sig med sin kusin, den danske kronprinsen Fredrik (VI) och 25 år senare, samma dag de firade sin silverbröllopsdag, den 31 juli 1815, kröntes de 
1 CONC till kung och drottning i Danmark. De fick med tidenåtta barn varav endast två blev vuxna: Caroline 1793 och Vilhelmina 1808 (hon blev senare gift med Fredrik VII) Mellan åren 1814-1815 deltog hennes man i kongressen i Wien, och h
1 CONC on var då regent i landet, men annars deltog hon inte i riksstyrelsen. Däremot ägnade hon sig ivrigt åt genealogiska studier och hon gav ut en bok som innehöll en stamtavla med deeuropeiska furstehusen. Hennes man hade under hela 
1 CONC 30 år en älskarinna som hette Bente Rafsted, som Marie mycket väl kände till. Han fick fyra barn med henne. Fredrik dog i december 1839, och på hans begravning var bägge kvinnorna där och Marie skall då ha sagt till hansälskarinna
1 CONC : "Vi har bägge förlorat en god man".Maria dog den 21 mars 1852, 84 år gammal. Gravplats: Danmark - Roskilde katedral  
