0 @N6000000002188445618@ NOTE
1 CONT
1 CONT      Karl III was the first-born son of Duke Friedrich Wilhelm (1711-1815) of Braunschweig - Wolfenbüttel - Bevern.  When Duke Friedrich Wilhelm was unexpectedly killed in 1815 in the war against Napoleon in the battle at Waterloo
1 CONC , he left behind two as yet minor-aged sons, Karl III and Wilhelm.  King George IV of Great Britain assumed the guardianship of the two young boys.  Until Karl III came of age, a regency council ruled in the reestablished Duchy of
1 CONC  Braunschweig.  At the Vienna Congress in 1814 the principality of Braunschweig - Wolfenbüttel was elevated to the duchy of Braunschweig. Beginning in 1826 the young Duke Karl III personally assumed the rule  in Braunschweig.  In 
1 CONC 1830 the generally inept governing style of duke Karl III and his neo-absolutist regime led to a revolution in the duchy and his removal from office. Thereupon, in September 1830, duke Karl III left his land in full flight and rem
1 CONC oved to London.  In Berlin the younger brother duke Wilhelm did his service as a cavalry captain in the Prussian army.  In the same month, after the removal of Karl III in Braunschweig, duke Wilhelm took over the regency in the du
1 CONC chy.  Although immediately after his flight duke Karl III transferred the rule to his brother Wilhelm for a limited period of time, Wilhelm never gave it back to his brother Karl III.  The relationship between the two brothers rem
1 CONC ained disturbed until the death of Karl III in 1873.  The deposed duke Karl III sought repeatedly to recover the throne in Braunschweig.  In 1831 extraordinary proceedings in Paris and the dissolute life of the powerless duke led 
1 CONC the Germanic states which were joined in the Frankfurt parliament to make a declaration about the duke's fitness for rule.  This judgment was never again raised in the parliament.  Powerless and embittered, the unmarried duke Karl
1 CONC  III died in Geneva in 1873.  He bequeathed to this city his not inconsiderable fortune.  Even today an imposing memorial in front of  the hotel "Beaurivage" in Geneva reminds one of this inheritance.
0 @N6000000002188445596@ NOTE
1 CONT
1 CONT      Duke Wilhelm was the last ruling prince from the line of Braunschweig - Wolfenbüttel - Bevern.  With his death in 1884, the small duchy of Braunschweig fell to the Lüneburg line (New House of Lüneburg), the House of Hanover, 
1 CONC which had already been deprived of its power in 1866 by Prussia.  Wilhelm was the youngest son of duke Friedrich Wilhelm (1771-1815).  However, before Wilhelm took up the rule in Braunschweig, his older brother Karl III ruled.  At
1 CONC  the Vienna Congress in 1814, while it was ruled by a regency council in lieu of the still minor-aged Karl III, the principality of Braunschweig - Wolfenbüttel was elevated to the duchy of Braunschweig.  The governing style of Kar
1 CONC l III angered his subjects through numerous inexplicable decisions, so that in 1830 an insurrection took place in the duchy of Braunschweig.  Karl III fled thereupon, in September 1830, to London.  Still in September of the that y
1 CONC ear, Wilhelm took up the rule of the duchy and never returned it to his brother.  The relationship of the two brothers remained unsettled until the death of Karl III in 1873.  Duke Wilhelm gave his ministers a largely free hand in
1 CONC  the affairs of state.  The constitutional monarch was much esteemed by the people.  The Duke preferred not to marry and to give up the traditional courtly diversions, like hunting and theater.  His banished brother died in 1873 i
1 CONC n Geneva and bequeathed his fortune to this city.  Duke Wilhelm died  in 1884 in Sibyllenort.  With the death of duke Wilhelm, the line of Braunschweig - Bevern also died out and therewith, too, the New House of Braunschweig.  The
1 CONC  hereditary succession fell to the Hanoverian line, which, due to political difficulties, was unable to take up the rule in Brunswick until 1913.
