0 @N6000000005599098169@ NOTE Christian IX of Denmark (April 8, 1818 - January 29, 1906) was King of Denmark from November 15, 1863 to January 29, 1906.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was born in Gottorp the fourth son of Friedrich Wilhelm, Duke of Schleswig-Holstein-Sonderburg-Glücksburg and Luise Caroline, Princess of Hesse-Kassel. Through his mother, Christian was a great-grandson of Frederik V of Denmark
1 CONC , great-great-grandson of George II of Great Britain and descendant of several other monarchs, but had no strong claim to any European throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT He grew up in Denmark and was educated in the Military Academy of Copenhagen .
1 CONT
1 CONT As a young man, he unsuccessfully sought the hand of Queen Victoria in marriage. In 1842 he married Luise of Hesse-Kassel, a niece of Christian VIII and had a family of six children by her.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1847, he was willed the Danish throne by Christian VIII, as Christian VIII did not expect his only surviving son, the future Frederik VII to have any sons.
1 CONT
1 CONT He succeeded Frederik VII to the throne in November 15, 1863. He was immediately plunged into a crisis over the possession and status of Schleswig and Holstein, two territories to Denmark's south when, under pressure, he signed th
1 CONC e November Constitution, a treaty that made Schleswig part of Denmark. This resulted in a brief war between Denmark and a Prussian/Austrian alliance in 1864. The war ended with the incorporation of Schleswig into Prussia in 1865. 
1 CONC Holstein was likewise incorporated into Prussia in 1865, following further battle between Austria and Prussia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Christian's 43-year reign was otherwise peaceful.
1 CONT
1 CONT He did seek, unsuccessfully, to prevent the spread of democracy throughout Denmark. However, he signed a treaty in 1874 which allowed Iceland, then a Danish possession, to have its own constitution, albeit one that still had Denma
1 CONC rk ruling Iceland. In 1900, he approved the establishment of a Danish parliament which would have power over absolutism.
1 CONT
1 CONT Another reform occurred in 1866, when the Danish constitution was revised so that Denmark's upper chamber would have more power than the lower.
1 CONT
1 CONT Social security also took a few steps forward during his reign. Old age pensions were introduced in 1891 and unemployment and family benefits were introduced in 1892.
1 CONT
1 CONT Christian died of old age at 88 at Copenhagen and was buried at Roskilde, Denmark.
1 CONT
1 CONT He remains noteworthy for the number of immediate descendants that have become monarchs in their own right. His sons included Frederik VIII of Denmark and George I of Greece. His grandsons include Nicholas II of Russia, Constantin
1 CONC e I of Greece , George V of the United Kingdom, Christian X of Denmark and Haakon VII of Norway. He was named father-in-law of Europe. Details of his children are as follows:
1 CONT
1 CONT King Frederik VIII of Denmark (June 3, 1843 - May 14, 1912), married to Princess Lovisa of Sweden Princess Alexandra of Denmark, later Queen consort of Edward VII of the United Kingdom (December 1, 1844 - November 20 , 1925).
1 CONT
1 CONT King George I of Greece (December 24, 1845 - March 18, 1913). Princess Dagmar of Denmark, later consort of Tsar Alexander III of Russia (November 26, 1847 - October 13, 1928).
1 CONT
1 CONT Princess Thyra of Denmark, later consort of Ernst August of Hanover, 3rd Duke of Cumberland (September 29, 1853 - February 26, 1933).
1 CONT
1 CONT Prince Valdemar of Denmark, (October 27, 1858 - January 14, 1939), married to princess Marie de Orleáns-Bourbon (1865-1909).
1 CONT
1 CONT Christian IX of Denmark From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Christian IX of Denmark (April 8, 1818 - January 29, 1906) was King of Denmark from November 15, 1863 to January 29, 1906.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was born in Gottorp the fourth son of Friedrich Wilhelm, Duke of Schleswig-Holstein-Sonderburg-Glücksburg and Luise Caroline, Princess of Hesse-Kassel. Through his mother, Christian was a great-grandson of Frederik V of Denmark
1 CONC , great-great-grandson of George II of Great Britain and descendant of several other monarchs, but had no strong claim to any European throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT He grew up in Denmark and was educated in the Military Academy of Copenhagen.
1 CONT
1 CONT As a young man, he unsuccessfully sought the hand of Queen Victoria in marriage. In 1842 he married Luise of Hesse-Kassel, a niece of Christian VIII and had a family of six children by her.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1847, he was willed the Danish throne by Christian VIII, as Christian VIII did not expect his only surviving son, the future Frederik VII to have any sons.
1 CONT
1 CONT He succeeded Frederik VII to the throne in November 15, 1863. He was immediately plunged into a crisis over the possession and status of Schleswig and Holstein, two territories to Denmark's south when, under pressure, he signed th
1 CONC e November Constitution, a treaty that made Schleswig part of Denmark. This resulted in a brief war between Denmark and a Prussian/Austrian alliance in 1864. The war ended with the incorporation of Schleswig into Prussia in 1865. 
1 CONC Holstein was likewise incorporated into Prussia in 1865, following further battle between Austria </wiki/Austria> and Prussia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Christian's 43-year reign was otherwise peaceful.
1 CONT
1 CONT He did seek, unsuccessfully, to prevent the spread of democracy throughout Denmark. However, he signed a treaty in 1874 which allowed Iceland, then a Danish possession, to have its own constitution, albeit one that still had Denma
1 CONC rk ruling Iceland. In 1900, he approved the establishment of a Danish parliament which would have power over absolutism.
1 CONT
1 CONT Another reform occurred in 1866, when the Danish constitution was revised so that Denmark's upper chamber would have more power than the lower. Social security also took a few steps forward during his reign. Old age pensions were 
1 CONC introduced in 1891 and unemployment and family benefits were introduced in 1892. Christian died of old age at 88 at Copenhagen and was buried at Roskilde, Denmark. He remains noteworthy for the number of immediate descendants that
1 CONC  have become monarchs in their own right. His sons included Frederik VIII of Denmark and George I of Greece. His grandsons include Nicholas II of Russia, Constantine I of Greece, George V of the United Kingdom, Christian X of Denm
1 CONC ark and Haakon VII of Norway.
1 CONT
1 CONT Details of his children are as follows: 1. King Frederik VIII of Denmark (June 3, 1843 - May 14, 1912) 2. Princess Alexandra of Denmark , later Queen consort of Edward VII of the United Kingdom (December 1, 1844 - November 20, 192
1 CONC 5). 3. King George I of Greece (December 24, 1845 - March 18, 1913). 4. Princess Dagmar of Denmark, later consort of Tsar Alexander III of Russia (November 26, 1847 - October 13, 1928). 5. Princess Thyra of Denmark, later consort 
1 CONC of Ernst August of Hanover, 3rd Duke of Cumberland (September 29, 1853 - February 26, 1933). 6. Prince Valdemar of Denmark, (October 27, 1858 - January 14, 1939), married to princess Marie de Orleáns-Bourbon (1865-1909).
1 CONT
1 CONT External links A detailed listing of his descendants <http://www.angelfire.com/dc/fjohansen/old/royalgen/christian9.html> (http://www.angelfire.com/dc/fjohansen/old/royalgen/christian9.html)
0 @N6000000006127645569@ NOTE Christian IX föddes den 8 april 1818 på Gottorps slott (se bild), som ligger i den nordtyska delstaten Schleswig-Holstein, som fjärde son till hertig Fredrik Vilhelm av Schleswig-Holstein-Sönderburg-Glücksburg (1785-1831) och Lovi
1 CONC sa Karolina avHessen-Kassel (1789-1831) som var dotterdotter till Fredrik V och yngre syster till Fredrik VI hustru Maria Sofia Fredrika av Hessen-Kassel.Han släkt tillhörde en sidolinje av det danska kungahuset Oldenburg, som här
1 CONC stammade från Christian III:s son Hans d.y. och kallades den "Beckska", innan den fick rätt att bära namnet "den Glücksburgska".Fredrik VI och hans hustru Maria höll mycket av honom och de uttalade öppet att de gärna velat ha Chri
1 CONC stian till son. Christian växte upp i Köpenhamn förutom åren 1839-1841 då han studerade vid universitetet i Bonn.När han var 24 år gifte han sig, den 26 maj 1842, med sin syssling Louise av Hessen-Kassel, som var dotter till lantg
1 CONC reven & generalen Vilhelm av Hessen-Kassel (1787-1867) och Christian VIII:s syster Louise Charlotta av Oldenburg. De fick medtiden sex barn:1) Fredrik VIII 1843-1912 (gift med Louise Bernadotte) 2) Alexandra 1844-1925 (gift med St
1 CONC orbritanniens kung Edward VII)3) Wilhelm 1845-1913 (blev kung i Grekland med namn "Georg I")4) Dagmar (Maria Feodorovna) 1847-1928 (g.m. Alexander III av Ryssland)5) Thyra 1853-19336) Waldemar 1858-1939Då det Schleswigs-Holsteinsk
1 CONC a upproret bröt ut 1848 tjänstgjorde han personligen men var inte med på någon drabbning. 1851 utsågs han till tronföljare, eftersom Fredrik VII inte hade några barn. Genom Londontraktatet den 8 maj 1852 erkände allastormakterna C
1 CONC hristian som arvinge till hela danska monarkin. Danska riksdagen antog, efter långa överläggningar, den nya tronföljdsordningen. Den 31 juli 1853 kungjordes så lagen och Christian blev då prins av Danmark. 1856 fick han inträde ir
1 CONC ådet och 1859 blev han chef för kavalleriet.När Fredrik VII dog den 15 november 1863 blev Christian, vid 45 års ålder, kung.Samma år fastställde han, under press från den sittande regeringen, den så kallade "novemberförfattningen"
1 CONC , men försökte sedan återta sin stadfästelse, vilket gjorde att han blev impopulär både hos regeringen och det vanliga folket. Genom sinredbara och ärliga personlighet vann han med tiden i alla fall allmän aktning, också även ute 
1 CONC i Europa.Kristian kallades för Europas svärfar eftersom flera av hans barn blev ingifta i Europeiska kungahus. Han dog den 29 januari 1906, 87 år gammal, efter 43 år på tronen. Nästa att bli kung i Danmark var hans son Fredrik VII
1 CONC I. Gravplats: Danmark - Roskilde katedral  
