0 @N6000000005599137253@ NOTE At the Saturday evening of September 24, 1803, in Ludwigslust Palace, Mecklenburg-Schwerin, Her Imperial Highness Grand Duchess Helena Pavlovna died from tuberculosis at age 18.
1 CONT
1 CONT She was granddaughter and goddaughter of Catherine II, daughter of Emperor Paul I and sister of Alexander I.
1 CONT
1 CONT In her 14 Helena was married to His Serene Highness Hereditary Prince Frederick Louis of Mecklenburg whom she bore son Paul Frederick, future Grand Duke of Mecklenburg(-Schwerin), and daughter Marie.
1 CONT
1 CONT When in 1874 Paul Frederick's granddaughter Marie married a son of Russian Emperor, she received the patronymics 'Pavlovna' in memory of her ancestor Emperor Paul.
1 CONT
1 CONT Helena Pavlovna was buried in Schwerin.
0 @N6000000002188420560@ NOTE In 1816 the then crown prince Wilhelm von Württemberg in Petersburg married his cousin, Katharina Pawlowna, Grand Duchess of Russia. However, after only three years of marriage the beloved wife died at just 30 years of age.  Just 
1 CONC how great the esteem for and importance of the deceased Queen was, is shown by the willingness in 1819 to pull down the family seat of the Württembergs to make room for her sepulchral chapel (Grabkapelle).  Queen Katharina had bro
1 CONC ught an unusually modern social structure to the state. She founded the first Württemberg savings bank, created schools with modern curricula, hospitals, children's homes and institutions for feeding the poor, which she paid for p
1 CONC artially from her immense Russian dowry. As the Russian Grand Duchess she was a member of the Russian-Orthodox Church and remained so (an indispensable condition for the marriage of a Russian princess to a member of a Western dyna
1 CONC sty of princes) when she wed the Lutheran Crown Prince Wilhelm. Every Whit Monday a church service is held in the Sepulchral Chapel in her memory.
0 @N6000000002322332607@ NOTE On death of his older brother, Nicholas came to the throne after suppressing
1 CONT the Decembrist revolt, staged by reform-minded army officers who favored the
1 CONT accession of his brother Constantine. His domestic policy was autocratic and
1 CONT his foreign policy agressive. His designs upon Constantinople provoked war
1 CONT with Turkey (1853) into which other European powers were drawn (the Crimean
1 CONT War, 1854-1856). He succeeded brother Alexander I in 1825.
0 @N6000000002176677307@ NOTE On death of his older brother, Nicholas came to the throne after suppressing
1 CONT the Decembrist revolt, staged by reform-minded army officers who favored the
1 CONT accession of his brother Constantine. His domestic policy was autocratic and
1 CONT his foreign policy agressive. His designs upon Constantinople provoked war
1 CONT with Turkey (1853) into which other European powers were drawn (the Crimean
1 CONT War, 1854-1856). He succeeded brother Alexander I in 1825.
