0 @N6000000002188440892@ NOTE
1 CONT
1 CONT      King Friedrich Wilhelm IV, son and successor of King Friedrich Wilhelm III. A romanticist and a mystic, he conceived vague schemes of reform based on a revival of the medieval structure, with the rule of estates and a patriar
1 CONC chal monarchy. During the revolution of 1848 in Prussia, which broke out in March, Friedrich Wilhelm was forced at first to accede to revolutionary demands. Later, however, he crushed the opposition, dissolved in December of 1848 
1 CONC the constituent assembly, and promulgated a conservative constitution, which, as modified in 1850, remained in force until 1918. King Friedrich Wilhelm refused the crown of a united Germany offered him in 1849 by the Frankfurt Par
1 CONC liament on the grounds that a monarch by divine right could not receive authority from an elected assembly. Although unwilling to accept the crown from an elected assembly, King Friedrich Wilhelm desired German unity under Prussia
1 CONC n leadership and presented the Prussian Union plan for a confederation of Prussia and the smaller German states. Austrian opposition to the plan forced King Friedrich Wilhelm to abandon it in the Treaty of Olmütz in 1850. In 1848,
1 CONC  King Friedrich Wilhelm briefly supported the revolt in Schleswig-Holstein against Denmark but yielded to British pressure for an armistice. In 1857 his mental condition necessitated a temporary (later permanent) regency of his br
1 CONC other, who succeeded him as King William I.
