0 @N6000000002188420106@ NOTE Wilhelm I (1797-1888) was the seventh King of Prussia and the first German Emperor or Kaiser. He asceeded to the Prussian throne in 1861 after his older brother Frederick William IV died. He was instrumental with the assistance of
1 CONC  of the Iron Chancelor, Count Otto von Bismark, and other advisors in founding the German state in the modern sence--uniting a great diversity of prinipalities under the martial leadership of Prussia. William was crowned Emperor i
1 CONC n 1871 after defeating Emperor Napoleon III in the Framco Prussian War. He and his descendents retained the crown of Prussia. Wilhelm followed the Hohenzollern tradition of a military career. His career began when he was only 17 y
1 CONC ears of age with the 1814 campaign against Napoleon who had returned from exile on Elba. It closed with the 1870-71 campaign against Napoleon III. Wilhelm fell in love with a Polish Princess--Princess Elise Radziwill. The Prussian
1 CONC  court, however, did not believe that a mere Polish aristocrat was an acceptable match for a Prussian Hohenzollern and he was not allowed to marry her.  Wilhelm pledged that he would never give his affection to anyone else. Wilhel
1 CONC m was ordered to popose to Princess Marie Louise Augusta of Sachsen-Weimar and dutifully did so. She accepted, but with an equal lack of enthusism. The two mairred in 1829, but the mairrage was no love match. As Kaiser and Kaiseri
1 CONC na the two avoided each other. They had a completely opposite outlooks on life. The two could hardly spend a few minutes together in a room without arguing. The two quarled from the day of their mairrage and tried to live apart as
1 CONC  much as possible. Prince Albert when he met them determined that they were both equally responsible. Their personalities clashed.
