1 CONT family.
0 @N6000000002188395595@ NOTE Her baptismal name was Louise Caroline Alberta Victoria.
0 @N6000000005599217060@ NOTE [Peer18.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT RIN: 483
1 CONT PREFIX: Princess
1 CONT FULL TITLE: Duchess of Argyll
1 CONT Titles: Princess Louise, Duchess of Argyll. Cremated at Golders Green Crematorium. Ashes buried in the Royal Burial Ground at Frogmore
1 CONT
1 CONT Princess Louise, Duchess of Argyll From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her Royal Highness Princess Louise, later Her Royal Highness the Princess Louise, Duchess of Argyll (18 March 1848 - 3 December 1939) was given the names Louise Caroline Alberta. She was the sixth child of Queen Victoria and Princ
1 CONC e Albert.
1 CONT
1 CONT Princess Louise attended the Kensington National Art Training School when she was 20.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her mother, the Queen, wished for her daughter to marry and a hunt was put in place for a suitable husband. The Princess of Wales (later Queen Alexandra) proposed her own brother, Crown Prince Frederick of Denmark as a possible ca
1 CONC ndidate, but the Queen firmly opposed to another Danish marriage that could annoy Prussia. Louise's eldest sister the Crown Princess of Prussia, proposed her own candidate, the tall and rich Prince Albrecht of Prussia, her husband
1 CONC 's cousin. However, he was reluctant to come and live in England. Victoria then proposed the idea that Louise marry a member of the British nobility. Edward, Prince of Wales (later King Edward VII), disapproved of this idea.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eventually a suitable candidate was chosen, the Marquess of Lorne, heir to the title of Duke of Argyll. The Marquess' parents were known to the Queen, and they approved of the proposed union. The Marquess was also a Member of Parl
1 CONC iament at this time.
1 CONT
1 CONT Princess Louise married the Marquess of Lorne on March 21, 1871 in St. George's Chapel at Windsor Castle.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1878, the Marquess was offered the position of Governor General of Canada. On November 14, 1878, Lorne and Louise departed from Liverpool Harbour on the long journey to Canada. Princess Louise was popular in Canada, and extensi
1 CONC vely toured the country, as well as visiting the neighbouring United States.
1 CONT
1 CONT Princess Louise was an accomplished writer, sculptor and artist - she painted well in both oils and water colours. A door she painted with sprigs of apple blossoms can still be seen in the Monck wing corridor at Rideau Hall. She g
1 CONC ave the name Regina (which is Latin for Queen) to the capital of Saskatchewan, and both the district of Alberta in the Northwest Territories (later the province of Alberta) and Lake Louise in that district were named after her. Al
1 CONC though she was often unwell, she was a compassionate woman who, during an epidemic of scarlet fever, personally nursed the sick.
1 CONT
1 CONT On February 14, 1880, she was seriously injured when the viceregal sleigh overturned on the streets of Ottawa, Ontario, but she eventually made a full recovery. The couple left Canada in 1883 to return to England, although Louise 
1 CONC had already returned home for 2 years previously.
1 CONT
1 CONT Three Canadian regiments still honour her in their titles: 8th Canadian Hussars (Princess Louise’s) The Princess Louise Fusiliers The Argyll and Sutherland Highlanders of Canada (Princess Louise’s)
1 CONT
1 CONT On 4 April 1900, the 8th Duke of Argyll died, and the Marquess of Lorne, became the 9th Duke of Argyll, and Louise, the Duchess of Argyll.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Duke died in 1914 from pneumonia. Princess Louise, now a widow, spent World War I visiting Canadian Army units coming to fight in France. She survived until the beginning of World War II, dying on December 3, 1939 at Kensingto
1 CONC n Palace, when she was ninety one years old. At her own request her body was cremated at Golders Green  in North London and her ashes were buried at Royal Cemetery at Frogmore.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Duke and Duchess of Argyll did not have any children.
1 CONT
1 CONT Princess Louise's titles from birth to death: Her Royal Highness The Princess Louise Her Royal Highness Princess Louise, Marchioness of Lorne Her Royal Highness Princess Louise, The Duchess of Argyll
0 @N6000000007329641440@ NOTE [Kopi av ROYALS.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Titles:  Princess Louise, Duchess of Argyll.  Cremated at Golders Green
1 CONT Crematorium.  Ashes buried in the Royal Burial Ground at Frogmore.
