0 @N6000000002244428576@ NOTE She would become Princess Henry of Battenberg
0 @N6000000005599214965@ NOTE Princess Beatrice, Princess Henry of Battenberg From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Princess Beatrice, Princess Henry of Battenberg (14 April 1857 - 26 October 1944), was the fifth daugther and the youngest child of Queen Victoria and Prince Albert of Saxe-Coburg-Gotha.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her Royal Highness The Princess Beatrice Mary Victoria Feodore of Great Britain and Ireland, V.A., C.I., G.C.V.O., G.B.E., R.R.C. was born at Buckingham Palace. Her father, the Prince Consort, died when she was only four years old
1 CONC  and the griefstriken Queen clung to her youngest child. Nicknamed "Baby" by the Queen, the princess became a good pianist; some her compositions were later published. Her chief occupation, however, was to act as her mother's secr
1 CONC etary and companion, a role she fulfilled until Queen Victoria's death on 22 January 1901. She was linked romantically with Napoleon Eugene, Prince Imperial, who was killed in the Anglo-Zulu War in an ambush.
1 CONT
1 CONT On 23 July 1885, Princess Beatrice married His Serene Highness Prince Henry of Battenberg (5 October 1858 - 20 January 1896) at St. Mildred's Church, Whippingham  on the Isle of Wight. Queen Victoria gave her blessing to the marri
1 CONC age on the condition that the couple made their home with her. Prince Henry was the third son of His Grand Ducal Highness Prince Alexander of Hesse and by Rhine (1823-1888) by his morganatic marriage to Julie Therese, Countess von
1 CONC  Hauke (1825-1895), the daughter of a Polish politician. His elder brother, Prince Louis of Battenberg, had married Princess Victoria of Hesse and by Rhine, a granddaughter of Queen Victoria and a niece of Princess Beatrice, a yea
1 CONC r earlier. On the day of his wedding, the Queen granted Prince Henry the style Royal Highness, a style that was only in effect in Great Britain, not Hesse and by Rhine, where the prince was a Serene Highness.
1 CONT
1 CONT Their Royal Highnesses Prince and Princess Henry of Battenberg had four children. By Royal Warrant of 13 December 1886, the Queen granted the children the style Highness. This style was was only in effect in Great Britain and not 
1 CONC Hesse and by Rhine, where as children of Prince Henry they were only entitled to the style Serene Highness:
1 CONT
1 CONT Prince Alexander of Battenberg (3 November 1896 - 23 February 1960), created 1st Marquess of Carisbrooke 18 July 1917; m. 19 July 1917 Irene Denison (4 July 1890 - 16 July 1956 ) daughter of William Denison, 3rd Earl of Londesbrou
1 CONC gh, and had issue.
1 CONT
1 CONT Princess Victoria Eugénie of Battenberg (24 October 1887 - 15 April 1969); m. 31 May  1906 King Alfonso XIII of Spain (17 May 1886 - 28 February 1941), and had issue.
1 CONT
1 CONT Prince Leopold of Battenberg (21 May 1889 - 23 April 1922).
1 CONT
1 CONT Prince Maurice of Battenberg (3 October 1891 - 27 October 1914).
1 CONT
1 CONT Prince Henry of Battenberg died of a fever he contracted while on active military duty in the second Ashanti War, leaving Princess Henry a widow at the age of 38.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Queen Victoria died, the Princess went to live in Osborne Cottage and carried out her duties as governor of the Isle of Wright, a position she inherited from her husband. In 1914, she moved to Carisbrooke Castle, but maintain
1 CONC ed an apartment in Kensington Palace as her London home. She spent thirty years edited her mother's journals. In doing so, she followed Queen Victoria's instruction to remove anything that might cause pain to persons mentioned or 
1 CONC to her relatives. The Princess transcribed the text in her own handwriting and burned the originals. She finished the task in 1931 and the 111 volumes of Queen Victoria's edited journals are at the Royal Archives at Windsor Castle
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT In July 1917, when King George V changed the name of the British Royal House from the House of Saxe-Coburg-Gotha to House of Windsor, he relinquished, on behalf of his various relatives who were British subjects, the use of all Ge
1 CONC rman titles and styles. Princess Henry resumed the style of "HRH The Princess Beatrice." Her two surviving sons assumed by Royal Warrant the surname Mountbatten and discontinued the use of the title Prince of Battenberg and the st
1 CONC yle Highness. Her elder son, Alexander ("Drino") was created Marquess of Clarisbrooke, while the younger son, Leopold, assumed the courtesy title Lord Leopold Mountbatten.
1 CONT
1 CONT In January 1919, George V created Princess Beatrice a Dame Grand Cross of the Most Excellent Order of the British Empire (G.B.E.), in recognition for her role as president of the Isle of Wight branch of the British Red Cross Socie
1 CONC ty. She was created a Dame Grand Cross of the Order of St. John of Jerusalem in 1927.
1 CONT
1 CONT Princess Beatrice died at her wartime home, Brantridge Park, Balcombe, Sussex, in October 1944. Her funeral took place at St. George's Chapel, Windsor on 3 November, followed by internment at the royal tomb house. Her remains were
1 CONC  transferred later to Battenberg Chapel at St Mildred's Church on the Isle of Wight on 28 August 1945. She was the last surviving child of Queen Victoria and Prince Albert.
