0 @N6000000002188420696@ NOTE The marriage of the Russian Grand Duchess to the successor to the throne in Württemberg in 1846 had begun with diverse dynastic relations between the two houses and Olga was also accepted in Württemberg due to her "truly royal att
1 CONC itude". On a social level she emulated Queen Katharina, however without being able to make use of as large a personal fortune. The fact that the royal couple was childless also proved to be a serious problem for their marriage. In
1 CONC  1870 Olga's niece Wera was adopted. Queen Olga lies buried in the Palace Church (Schloßkirche) in Stuttgart.
0 @N6000000002322332561@ NOTE He was King of Prussia and Emperor of Germany from March 9 - June 15, 1888
1 CONT after succeeding his father, for he died after reigning 3 months, with his
1 CONT son, William II succeeding him.  99 days' reign, during which time he was a
1 CONT voiceless invalid, dying of throat cancer. Although influenced by liberal,
1 CONT constitutional, and middle-class ideas, he retained a strong sense of the
1 CONT Hohenzollern royal and imperial dignity. His only significant official act
1 CONT was to dismiss von Puttkamer, minister of interior, an extreme conservative.
0 @N6000000002188420119@ NOTE Frederick was born in 1831, the son of King Wilhelm I of Prussia at Potsdam. When his father succeeded to the throne of Prussia in 1861, Frederick became Crown Prince Frederick William. Frederick was liberal in his political views
1 CONC , uncharacteristic for the Hohenzollerens. He opposed Count von Bismark throught his long term as Chancellor. His liberal views were strngthened by mairring the British Princess Royal Victoria, but he died only a few months after 
1 CONC becoming Emperor. For that reason 1888 in Germany is called the year of the three emperors. The crown passed to his son who had a very different tempernent and political leanings. Friederich reportedly had a very unhappy childhood
1 CONC , in part because his parents had disliked each other from the day of their mairrage and quarled constantly when together. Friderich had a conventional upbringing. He was supervised in the nursery by nurses and governesses. As he 
1 CONC had only one sister, several years younger, he had little company. At age 7 he was entrusted to a military governor and tutor. Friderich was much closer to his mother than his more distant father. He took after her in may ways. Fr
1 CONC iederich especially liked to read. Perhaps as a result, he adopted a liberal outlook.
1 CONT
