0 @N6000000002188389937@ NOTE
1 CONT
1 CONT      His Majesty George V succeeded his father, Edward VII, to the British throne in 1910.  Born on June 3, 1865, he pursued a naval career until the death (1892) of his older brother made him second in line to the throne of his g
1 CONC randmother, Victoria. In 1894 he married Princess Mary of Teck, and was named the Duke of York.  At the beginning of his reign George was involved in two crises--one over reform of the House of Lords (1910-11) and the other over I
1 CONC rish Home Rule(1912-14). His conduct in these and, later, his choices of Stanley Baldwin as prime minister in 1923 and Ramsay MacDonald as head of thenational government in 1931 generated some controversy. Nonetheless, he won popu
1 CONC lar affection, despite his gruff personality, by his visits to the troops during World War I and later by his Christmas radiobroadcasts. During the war he changed the name of the royal family to Windsor. George's silver jubilee in
1 CONC  1935 was celebrated with elaborate festivities. He died on Jan. 20, 1936, and was succeeded by his oldest son, Edward VIII.
0 @N6000000000769948015@ NOTE [v37t1235.ftw]
1 CONT
1 CONT Facts about this person:
1 CONT
1 CONT Fact 1July 07, 1865
1 CONT Baptized: St. George's Chapel. Windsor Castle, London
1 CONT
1 CONT Fact 2June 22, 1911
1 CONT Acceded: Westminster Abbey, London, England
1 CONT
1 CONT Fact 3January 28, 1935/36
1 CONT Interred: St. George's Chapel, Windsor Castle, England
0 @N6000000005598912750@ NOTE He was the 19th Prince of Wales. He reigned from 1910 to 1936. He was Duke of York. He had a Japanese practitioner to tattoo a large blue and red dragon on his right arm. He was known as the Sailor Prince. He entered in the Britis
1 CONC h Royal Navy in 1877 and reached the rank of Vice Admiral in 1903. Following England's declaration of war on Germany, he renounced all German titles belonging to him and his family.
1 CONT
1 CONT (r. 1910-36)
1 CONT
1 CONT George V's reign began amid the continuing constitutional crisis over the House of Lords, which refused to pass a Parliament Bill limiting its powers (which would remove its power to veto a Bill from the Commons). After the Libera
1 CONC l government obtained the King's promise to create sufficient peers to overcome Conservative opposition in the Lords (and won a second election in 1910), the Parliament Bill was passed by the Lords in 1911 without a mass creation 
1 CONC of peers.
1 CONT
1 CONT George visited India in 1911, the only King-Emperor to do so. In 1914 the First World War broke out. The King made over 450 visits to troops and over 300 visits to hospitals visiting wounded servicemen, he pressed for proper treat
1 CONC ment of German prisoners-of-war and he pressed also for more humane treatment of conscientious objectors. In 1917 anti-German feeling led him to adopt the family name of Windsor (after the Castle of the same name). Support for hom
1 CONC e rule for Ireland had grown in the late 19th century. This was resisted by the Unionists in the north and by the Conservative Party. The 1916 Easter Rising in Dublin, and subsequent civil war, resulted in the setting up of the Ir
1 CONC ish Free State (later to become the Irish Republic) in 1922, while the six northern counties remained part of the United Kingdom. George played a conciliatory role on this, and on other occasions, such as the General Strike of 192
1 CONC 6.
1 CONT
1 CONT George readily accepted the first Labour government in 1924. Following the world slump of 1929, the King persuaded the Labour leader to head a National Government composed of all parties, which won the election of 1931. The Statut
1 CONC e of Westminster of 1931 meant Dominion Parliaments could now pass laws without reference to United Kingdom laws, and abolished various reserve powers still possessed by the Crown and Parliament. This paradoxically increased the m
1 CONC onarchy's importance, since the Dominions (no longer subordinated to one supreme Parliament at Westminster) were now linked through common allegiance to the Crown.
1 CONT
1 CONT George started the annual Christmas Broadcast by the sovereign to the Empire (more recently to the Commonwealth), the first being transmitted in 1932. In 1935, the King celebrated his Silver Jubilee. He died in 1936 and his son Ed
1 CONC ward succeeded to the throne
1 CONT
1 CONT King George was styled as "By the Grace of God, of Great Britain and Ireland, and of the British Dominions Beyond the Seas, King, Defender of the Faith, Emperor of India."
1 CONT As a sub-lieutenant, George participated in the battle of Jutland, in May, 1916.
1 CONT When George came to the throne, he worked with a speech coach to help him overcome his stammer.When Indian independence was granted in 1947, George relinquished the title "Emperor of India," making him the last British monarch to 
1 CONC be styled thus.
1 CONT Died of cancer.
1 CONT A card was attached to the wreath the British government sent to George's funeral, on which Winston Churchill wrote the same words as those inscribed on the Victoria Cross, "For Valour." ________________________
1 CONT
1 CONT George V was born June 3, 1865, the second son of Edward VII and Alexandra. His early education was somewhat insignificant as compared to that of the heir apparent, his older brother Albert. George chose the career of professional
1 CONC  naval officer and served competently until Albert died in 1892, upon which George assumed the role of the heir apparent. He married Mary of Teck (affectionately called May) in 1893, who bore him four sons and one daughter. He die
1 CONC d the year after his silver jubilee after a series of debilitating attacks of bronchitis, on January 20, 1936.
1 CONT
