0 @N6000000001562994438@ NOTE Rumors concerning Edward's attachment to Mrs. Simpson circulated before the death of George V.  The situation came to the brink of constitutional crisis with Edward's accession: The Church of England (of which he was the head) cen
1 CONC sured divorce, Parliament refused to grant Wallis any title, the populace was opposed to having a twice-divorced woman as the King's consort and English law had no precedent for a wife of the king with no title or official capacit
1 CONC y.  Had Edward pressed the issue, the constitutional monarchy would be irreparably damaged; he chose to abdicate rather than mar the image of the monarchy.  He was created Duke of Windsor, married Wallis and relished in social lif
1 CONC e. There is speculation and circumstantial evidence that he was a liaison to Hitler's Germany, but hard proof of the link has yet to surface. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward's situation had similarities with several different monarchs.  He had the second shortest reign in English history, the shortest being that of his namesake, Edward V. The only other adult bachelor to succeed the throne was 
1 CONC William II. The precedent of marrying a divorced woman was set by Henry II's marriage to Eleanor of Aquitaine.  He left the throne in the same manner as James II, abdication in face of popular opposition. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward was immensely popular when he came to throne; his widow, the Duchess of Windsor, lamented his abdication after his death: "He might have been a great King; the people loved him."
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward VIII, King of England later titled as: Duke of Windsor when he abdicated the throne to marry Mrs. Wallis Warfield Simpson. 
1 CONT Christened: Edward Albert Christian George Andrew Patrick David. 
1 CONT Reign: 20 Jan 1936 to 11 Dec 1936. His reign was only 326 days long. 
1 CONT Title: Edward VIII, King of Great Britain and Northern Ireland and Emperor of India. He abdicated in favor of his brother, the Duke of York, who became King George VI. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward married Mrs. Simpson in June of 1937.  Because his wife was not accorded the privileges of a royal Duchess in England, the Duke of Windsor resided abroad.  In 1937 he observed social and housing conditions in Germany and vi
1 CONC sited Adolf Hitler.  
1 CONT
1 CONT During World War II he served as a Major General in the British Expeditionary Froce, and he was a governor of the Bahama Islands from 1940 to 1945. After the war he lived as a private British citizen, chiefly in the United States 
1 CONC and France.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the funeral of George VI in Feb. 1952, he took part in a British royal ceremony for the first time since his abdication.  The Duke wrote, "A King's Story" (1951) which was made into a film in 1967, and "Windsor Revisited" (1960
1 CONC ).  Edward was a Freemason.  Tsarevich Nicholas II and his finance, Alix were the godparents at Edward's baptism.  
1 CONT
1 CONT Seven different names of the Duke of Windsor: 1. Prince Edward of York 2. Prince Edward of Cornwall and York 3. Duke of Cornwall 4. Edward, Prince of Wales 5. King Edward VIII 6. Prince Edward 7. Duke of Windsor. He is famous for 
1 CONC popularizing a fat symmetrical knot for a necktie called the Windsor knot.
0 @N6000000002176683790@ NOTE Edward VIII was proclaimed King in 1936 when his father died. He loved a Mrs.
1 CONT Wallis Warfield Simpson, a twice divorcee, but was forced to abdicate his
1 CONT crown in the name of love to marry Mrs. Simpson (1896-1986) in June of 1937.
1 CONT The date of the abdication was December 11, 1936, in favor of his brother,
1 CONT the Duke of York, and George VI became King, and Edward became the Duke of
1 CONT Windsor, living the life of a British citizen in the U.S. and France after WW
1 CONT II, and died in Paris, 1972. His wife, Wallis,  died in 1986.
