0 @N6000000006127643894@ NOTE Gustaf Adolf 1906-47, svensk prins, äldste son till Gustav VI Adolf, hertig av Västerbotten. Gift 1932t med prinsessan Sibylla av Sachsen- Coburg-Gotha. Barn: Margaretha, Birgitta, Désirée, Christina och Carl (XVI) Gustaf. Gustaf 
1 CONC Adolf var militär och idrottsman. Han var en mycket begåvad idrottsman, särskilt i ridning och fäktning; 1936, 1937 och 1940 blev han svensk mästare i sabelfäktning. Han deltog bl a i det svenska laget i prishoppning vid OS i Berl
1 CONC in 1936.Gustaf Adolf var dessutom ordförande i Riksidrottsförbundet och Sveriges olympiska kommitté från 1933. Gustaf Adolf var även organisatoriskt verksam inom idrotts-, scout- och fältsportväsendet. Från 1932 var han också ordf
1 CONC örande i Svenska scoutrådet och från 1938 även i Internationella scoutunionen. Under andra världskrigets beredskapsårtjänstgjorde han som stabsofficer med rangen överstelöjtnant.Gustaf Adolf omkom endast 40 år gammal i en flygolyc
1 CONC ka den 26 januari 1947. Det KLM-plan han färdades i på väg hem från en jaktresa i Holland störtade på Kastrup i Danmark strax efter starten och alla 15 ombordvarande omkom. Hans ende son,arvprins Carl Gustaf, var då endast 9 månad
1 CONC er gammal.I Per Svenssons biografi 2006 framstår Gustav Adolf som en lätt tragisk figur utan de personliga egenskaper som förknippas med kungligheter, förmågan att ta människor, charm och folklighet. Vittnesmål handlar alltid om h
1 CONC ur kantig ochsvårtillgänglig han var.Däremot fanns det mycket kring honom som var intressant: tidsandan och de grundvärderingar, som den svenska överklassen hade, den världsbild, som Gustaf Adolf säkerligen delade. Per Svensson se
1 CONC r honom som prototypen för den tidens adelsman,utbildad på Lundsberg och på Karlberg till officer, med jakter i Skåne och umgänge med släkten. I de grupperna fanns både tyskvänlighet och en misstänksamhet mot demokratin. Så nära f
1 CONC örknippades prinsen med dessa åsikter att socialdemokraternabehöll republikkravet i en omtalad diskussion på partikongressen 1944.
0 @N6000000007329696726@ NOTE Gustaf Adolf
1 CONT 1906-1947 
1 CONT
1 CONT Prins av Sverige, Hertug av Västerbotten - gift 1932 med Prinsesse Sibylla av Sachsen-Coburg-Gotha (1908-1972). Fem barn 
1 CONT
1 CONT Prince Gustav Adolph, Duke of Vasterbotten, eld. son of King Gustav VI Adolph,
1 CONT and Sibylla of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha.
0 @N6000000002188604199@ NOTE The Count renounced his rights of sucession to the Swedish throne on March 7, 1934.  The following day he married.
0 @N6000000006127643904@ NOTE Bernadotte, Sigvard, född 1907, greve av Visborg (titeln förlänad 1951 av storhertiginnan av Luxemburg), son till Gustav VI Adolf, formgivare av silverarbeten m.m. och industridesigner. Sigvard Bernadotte har främst verkat som sil
1 CONC versmed hos Georg Jensen men också som keramiker, textilkonstnär och industriell formgivare. Han drev firman Bernadotte Design AB. Sigvard förlorade arvsrätten till kronan 1934 genom sitt äktenskap med den tyskfödda Erica Patzek (
1 CONC äktenskapet senare upplöst). Han har förgäves försökt få tillbaka titeln. 1983 återtog han utan tillstånd sin prinstitel. I ett försök att återfåprinstiteln anmälde Sigvard Bernadotte 2001 Sverige till Europadomstolen. Han hävdade
1 CONC  att Sverige brutit mot de mänskliga rättigheterna.Sigvard Bernadotte gifte sig 1961 med skådespelerskan Marianne Lindberg.Om Visborg:Visborgs slott låg i sydvästra hörnet av Visby och var sammanbyggt med stadsmuren. 1676 erövrade
1 CONC s slottet av danskarna, som sprängde slottet i luften innan de lämnade Gotland. Oscar Bernadotte, som först fick titeln "greve av Visborg" hade varit hertig av Gotland och namnet Visborg har sannolikt kommit till användning som en
1 CONC  erinran om detta. Sigvard Oscar Fredrik Bernadotte 1907-2002  
0 @N6000000002188579914@ NOTE
1 CONT
1 CONT Queen Ingrid was the most royal of all royals. Not for one second did she forget her role from the moment she came to Denmark in 1935 as the new Crown Princess until she died at the age of 90, 65 years later. For her, her duty to 
1 CONC Denmark and the Danish Crown preceded everything else. With a will of steel and assiduous application, with an outstanding sense of situation, an innate dignity and a natural authority, she had a firm grip on the monarchy, her fam
1 CONC ily - and the entire family that is Denmark.
1 CONT She was always the most distinguished guardian of the Danish monarchy at the same time as being a model for younger generations of royals beyond the borders of Denmark. "The keeper of the royal flame" is the somewhat solemn title 
1 CONC she has sometimes been accorded.
1 CONT With her eminent, sharp eye for the demands of changing times, Queen Ingrid knew how to open, modernise and humanise the monarchy. But she knew how vulnerable the monarchy is and never went too far. And she constantly maintained t
1 CONC he knife-edge balance between on the one hand always being Her Majesty and on the other making genuine contact with thousands and thousands of people on all levels and from all strata of society. Her ability to make people relax w
1 CONC hen they were together with her was legendary.
1 CONT When her eldest daughter succeeded King Frederik on the throne in 1972, Queen Ingrid understood how to step back with natural dignity. Her role was now a different one. But at the same time, Queen Margrethe and the then government
1 CONC  agreed that Queen Ingrid should be able to act as Regent during the Queen's absence. Although for a hundred years there had been statutory authority for such an appointment, this was the first time a Regent had not been in the li
1 CONC ne of succession.
1 CONT It was a decision that was completely in keeping with the enormous respect and affection in which Denmark and the Danes held her. And she was raised above criticism to her dying day.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ingrid Victoria Sofia Louise Margareta did not have a particularly lovely childhood. Having grown up in the rigid Swedish Court, she became independent and mature long before she reached adulthood.
1 CONT She was only ten years old when her mother died, and she undertook to be a kind of replacement in relation to both her father and her four brothers. As an adult, she never showed her feelings behind her perfect exterior - she had 
1 CONC wept all the tears she needed to weep when her mother had died, she said. On the other hand, she was endowed with a quick temper, which she tried to keep in check throughout her life. It was a temper that could burst out if she re
1 CONC ally felt herself provoked. And it is said to have happened about once every five years!
1 CONT She never imagined that she could come to live what might be called a normal life. "I must marry; that is the only thing I am suited to," she once commented, words that must be said to be a vast understatement when one thinks that
1 CONC  she was a born leader and a thorough-going perfectionist. "She can lead her troops like a general," one of her brothers once observed, or, as Queen Margrethe once put it:
1 CONT "Mother is a splendid strategist and tactician. She could have been a brilliant lawyer."
1 CONT To this can be added the fact that Queen Ingrid was passionately fond of gardening, a quality that found expression in the most beautiful palace gardens at Fredensborg and Graasten.
1 CONT However, the Swedish Princess married the Danish Crown Prince, and this was a meeting of opposites: he was warm, impulsive, spontaneous, she was cool, controlled and reflective. He was a man of the people. She was royalty. It was 
1 CONC to turn out to be the perfect match, and they came to form an ideal partnership. Queen Ingrid made her husband into a good king; Frederik IX loved and admired her, and the family at Amalienborg became Denmark's nuclear family no. 
1 CONC 1, consisting of father, mother and three delightful little princesses.
1 CONT It is never easy to come to a new country and a new language, not even if Sweden and Denmark are closely related, brethren. The first time was difficult; she was exposed to many prejudices. Was she a little too cool? A little too 
1 CONC Swedish?
1 CONT But she quickly learned Danish, and once she came to Southern Jutland and the Graasten Castle that was fitted out as the summer residence for the Crown Prince and herself, a place she loved throughout her life, she made her breakt
1 CONC hrough as a Dane via her social work. This was the region that always lay closest to her heart. If there was a tiny remnant of a foreigner in her, that disappeared on 9 April 1940, the day Denmark was occupied. She never hid the f
1 CONC act that: "That morning, I realised I was Danish". In Denmark during the occupation, the Crown Prince and Princess were often seen walking with a pram in Copenhagen or cycling about the city. That was something the Danes could und
1 CONC erstand; the symbolical value was irreplaceable and helped to gather the population around the royal family.
1 CONT In 1953, the new Danish Constitution and Act of Succession came into force. Partial female succession was introduced, and Denmark's new heir to the throne was called Princess Margrethe. Even today people still discuss whether behi
1 CONC nd the scenes Queen Ingrid had any influence on this change, though she herself always denied it.
1 CONT
1 CONT Not only did Queen Ingrid keep Denmark and the Danes together, but she also kept her ever-increasing family together, a family that was scattered around Europe and the USA. Her attitude was that the family should be kept united. S
1 CONC he understood how important continuity is for a monarchy, and she made a great effort to gather the younger generation around her, especially during the summers at Graasten Castle.
1 CONT For her ten grandchildren, she was the beloved and perfect grandmother, and it was to her they went to open their hearts. They could talk to her at any time about anything at all. She was wise, understanding, and they used her as 
1 CONC a library, indeed, she was a "store of knowledge and wisdom", said Crown Prince Frederik.
1 CONT In 1997, Queen Ingrid, who suffered from osteoporosis, fell and broke her hip. She more or less recovered after the fracture and was very determined in exercising so as to regain her mobility. But her health slowly deteriorated, a
1 CONC nd during her last years she only rarely appeared in public. Queen Ingrid died of old age, surrounded by her large family, daughters, sons-in-law, grandchildren and great-grandchildren. With her 90 years she beat all records for D
1 CONC anish queens: none had previously reached such a high old age. The sense of loss after her will be great, in the family - and in Denmark. She was the incarnation of everything royal. She was the Swedish Princess who became Queen o
1 CONC f Denmark. But she was also the Queen who became one with Denmark.
