1 CONT [edit] Titles and styles
1 CONT
1 CONT     * His Royal Highness Prince Philip of Greece and Denmark (10 June 1921–18 March 1947)
1 CONT     * Lieutenant Philip Mountbatten, RN (18 March–19 November 1947)
1 CONT     * Lieutenant Sir Philip Mountbatten, KG, RN (19 November–19 November 1947)
1 CONT     * His Royal Highness Sir Philip Mountbatten, KG (19 November–20 November 1947)
1 CONT     * His Royal Highness The Duke of Edinburgh, KG (20 November 1947–03 November 1951)
1 CONT     * His Royal Highness The Duke of Edinburgh, KG, PC (04 November 1951–20 April 1952)
1 CONT     * His Royal Highness The Duke of Edinburgh, KG, KT, PC (21 April 1952–21 May 1953)
1 CONT     * His Royal Highness The Duke of Edinburgh, KG, KT, GBE, PC (22 May 1953–9 June 1968)
1 CONT     * His Royal Highness The Duke of Edinburgh, KG, KT, OM, GBE, PC (10 June 1968–14 November 1981)
1 CONT     * His Royal Highness The Duke of Edinburgh, KG, KT, OM, GBE, QSO, PC (15 November 1981–12 June 1988)
1 CONT     * His Royal Highness The Duke of Edinburgh, KG, KT, OM, GBE, AC, QSO, PC (from 13 June 1988)
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Arms
1 CONT Shield of arms of Prince Philip as the Duke of Edinburgh
1 CONT Shield of arms of Prince Philip as the Duke of Edinburgh
1 CONT Royal Standard of Prince Philip as the Duke of Edinburgh
1 CONT Royal Standard of Prince Philip as the Duke of Edinburgh
1 CONT
1 CONT The family coat of arms of Prince Philip is that of the House of Schleswig-Holstein-Sonderburg-Glücksburg.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Duke has his own personal coat of arms, created on 19 November 1947. Unlike the arms used by other members of the Royal Family, the Duke's arms do not feature the Royal coat of arms of the United Kingdom, as men are not entitl
1 CONC ed to bear the arms of their wives. However they do feature elements representing Greece and Denmark, from which he is descended in the male line; the Mountbatten family arms, from which he is descended in the female line; and the
1 CONC  City of Edinburgh, representing his dukedom.
1 CONT
1 CONT The shield is quartered. The first quarter depicting the arms of Denmark consists of three blue lions passant and nine red hearts on a yellow field. The second quadrant depicts the arms of Greece, a white cross on a blue field. Th
1 CONC e third quarter depicts the arms of the Mountbatten family, two vertical black stripes on a white field. The fourth quarter depicts the arms of the City of Edinburgh, a black and red castle. The dexter supporter is a savage from t
1 CONC he Danish Royal Coat of Arms; the sinister a golden lion (a traditional British symbol) wearing a ducal cornet and gorged (collared) with a naval crown, alluding to the Duke's naval career.
1 CONT
1 CONT The coat features both the motto God is my help and the motto of the Order of the Garter, Honi soit qui mal y pense (Shamed be he who thinks ill of it) on a representation of the Garter behind the shield.
1 CONT
1 CONT A banner of the Duke's arms is used as his personal standard.[16]
1 CONT
