1 CONT [edit] Controversial remarks
1 CONT Wikiquote has a collection of quotations related to:
1 CONT Prince Philip, Duke of Edinburgh
1 CONT
1 CONT Prince Philip is well-known for making remarks during public visits which are sometimes regarded as insensitive.[17]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Speaking to a driving instructor in Scotland, he asked: "How do you keep the natives off the booze long enough to get them through the test?"[18]
1 CONT     * When visiting China in 1986, he told a group of British students, "If you stay here much longer, you'll all be slitty-eyed".[18]
1 CONT     * After accepting a gift from a Kenyan citizen he replied, "You are a woman, aren't you?"[18]
1 CONT     * "If it has four legs and is not a chair, has wings and is not an aeroplane, or swims and is not a submarine, the Cantonese will eat it." (1986)[18]
1 CONT     * In 1966 he remarked that "British women can't cook."[18]
1 CONT     * To a British student in Papua New Guinea: "You managed not to get eaten then?"[18]
1 CONT     * Angering local residents in Lockerbie when on a visit to the town in 1993, the Prince said to a man who lived in a road where eleven people had been killed by wreckage from the Pan Am jumbo jet: "People usually say that afte
1 CONC r a fire it is water damage that is the worst. We are still trying to dry out Windsor Castle."[19]
1 CONT     * On a visit to the new National Assembly for Wales in Cardiff, he told a group of deaf children standing next to a Jamaican steel drum band, "Deaf? If you are near there, no wonder you are deaf."[20][18]
1 CONT     * In 2002, he asked an Indigenous Australian businessman, "Do you still throw spears at each other?"[21][18]
1 CONT     * Said to a Briton in Budapest, Hungary, "You can't have been here that long – you haven't got a pot belly." (1993)[18]
1 CONT     * Seeing a shoddily installed fuse box in a high-tech Edinburgh factory, HRH remarked that it looked "like it was put in by an Indian".[22][23]
1 CONT     * "Aren't most of you descended from pirates?" (in 1994, to an islander in the Cayman Islands)[18]
1 CONT     * At the height of the recession in 1981 he said: "Everybody was saying we must have more leisure. Now they are complaining they are unemployed."[18]
1 CONT     * Upon presenting a Duke of Edinburgh Award to a student, when informed that the young man was going to help out in Romania for six months, he asked if the student was going to help the Romanian orphans; upon being informed he
1 CONC  was not, it was claimed the 85-year-old duke added: "Ah good, there's so many over there you feel they breed them just to put in orphanages."[24]
1 CONT     * At the University of Salford, he told a 13-year-old aspiring astronaut: "You could do with losing a bit of weight."[25]
1 CONT     * In 1997, the Duke of Edinburgh, participating in an already controversial British visit to the Jallianwala Bagh massacre (Amritsar Massacre) Monument, provoked outrage in India and in the UK with an offhand comment. Having o
1 CONC bserved a plaque claiming "This place is saturated with the blood of about two thousand Hindus, Sikhs and Muslims who were martyred in a non-violent struggle.", Prince Philip observed, "That's a bit exaggerated, it must include th
1 CONC e wounded". When asked how he had come to this conclusion Philip said "I was told about the killings by General Dyer's son. I'd met him while I was in the Navy." [26]
1 CONT     * During a Royal visit to a Tamil Hindu temple in London, he asked a Hindu priest if he was related to terrorist organization the Tamil Tigers.[18]
1 CONT     * In 1996, he drew sharp criticism when he said "a gun is no more dangerous than a cricket bat in the hands of a madman". The comment came in the wake of the massacre of 16 children and their teacher in Dunblane, Scotland.[20]
1 CONT     * In 1987, he wrote in his foreword to If I Were an Animal that "In the event that I am reincarnated, I would like to return as a deadly virus, in order to contribute something to solve overpopulation."[27]
1 CONT     * In 2002, speaking to a blind, wheelchair bound woman who was accompanied by her guide dog, he remarked : "Do you know they're now producing eating dogs for the anorexics?"[28]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] In popular culture
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Actor James Cromwell portrays Prince Philip in the 2006 Oscar-winning film, The Queen.
1 CONT
1 CONT     * A fictionalised Philip (in his capacity as a World War II naval officer) is a minor character in John Birmingham's Axis of Time series of alternate history novels.
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Prince Philip appears as a fictional character in Nevil Shute's 1952 novel, In the Wet.
