1 CONT
1 CONT House of Schleswig-Holstein-Sonderburg-Glucksburg (Danish and Greek Royal Houses)
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Christian IX, King of Denmark, 1818 - 1906
1 CONT    2. George I, King of Greece, 1845 - 1913
1 CONT    3. Prince Andrew of Greece and Denmark, 1882 - 1944
1 CONT    4. Prince Philip, Duke of Edinburgh, 1921 -
1 CONT    5. Charles, Prince of Wales, 1948 -
1 CONT
1 CONT Legacy
1 CONT
1 CONT Popular culture
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles wrote a children's book, The Old Man of Lochnagar, and even read it on the BBC's Jackanory programme.
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles has a keen interest in illusionism, and is a member of The Magic Circle. He passed his audition by performing the cups and balls effect.[76]
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1977, Charles was interviewed on the Australian Broadcasting Commission rock and roll music program, Countdown by Ian (Molly) Meldrum.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 2000, he made an appearance in the UK soap, Coronation Street, to celebrate the show's 40th anniversary on ITV1.[77]
1 CONT
1 CONT In 2005, Prince Charles appeared as himself in the New Zealand adult cartoon series Bro'Town. The episode aired on TV3 on Wednesday 26 October and was the final episode in the second series of the popular show. Prince Charles agre
1 CONC ed to record some impromptu audio for Series Two while attending a performance from the show's creators during a visit to New Zealand. After some enthusiastic encouragement from Prime Minister Helen Clark (who also appears in the 
1 CONC episode), the Prince gave a royal rendition of the Bro'Town catch-cry "Morningside 4 Life!"
1 CONT
1 CONT In 2006, a court case was filed by Prince Charles against the Mail on Sunday after publication of extracts from his personal journals. Lawyers for the Prince argued that he was as entitled to keep private documents as any other pe
1 CONC rson. Various revelations were made, including his opinions on the takeover of Hong Kong by the People's Republic of China in 1997, in which he described Chinese government officials as "appalling old waxworks". His ex-private sec
1 CONC retary also alleged that the Prince considers himself a dissident, working against majority political opinions.[78]
1 CONT
1 CONT On Saturday 20 May 2006, ITV presented the 30th birthday of The Prince's Trust. It included songs from Embrace and their song 'World at our Feet' and Annie Lennox and also an interview with Prince Charles, Prince Harry and Prince 
1 CONC William by Ant and Dec.
1 CONT
1 CONT Prince Charles is sometimes referred to in the popular press as "Chazza" (along the lines of "Gazza", "Hezza" and similar coinages).
1 CONT
1 CONT Prince Charles has been criticised for publishing a memo on ambition and opportunity.[79] This memo was widely understood to criticise meritocracy for creating a competitive society. In humorist Lynne Truss's critique of British m
1 CONC anners entitled Talk to the Hand,[80] Charles's memo is evaluated with respect to the putative impact of meritocracy on British boorishness. Truss came to the conclusion that the prince might have a point, that the positive motiva
1 CONC tional impact of meritocracy might be balanced against the negative impact of a competitive society.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Prince of Wales Hospital in Hong Kong was named in his honour in 1984.
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles is often parodied by Scottish-American comedian Craig Ferguson on The Late Late Show.
1 CONT
