1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early life
1 CONT
1 CONT Andrew was born on 19 February 1960 in the Belgian Suite of Buckingham Palace, London. His mother is the reigning British monarch, Queen Elizabeth II, the elder daughter of King George VI and Queen Elizabeth. His father is Prince 
1 CONC Philip, Duke of Edinburgh, the only son of Prince Andrew of Greece and Denmark and Princess Alice of Battenberg. He was named after his paternal grandfather.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Prince was baptised in the Music Room of Buckingham Palace on 8 April 1960 by the Archbishop of Canterbury, Dr. Geoffrey Fisher, and his godparents were the Duke of Gloucester; Princess Alexandra; Lord Elphinstone; the Earl of
1 CONC  Euston and Georgina, Lady Kennard.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the first child born to a reigning British monarch since Queen Victoria's youngest child, The Princess Beatrice, in 1857. As a child of the reigning monarch, he was styled His Royal Highness The Prince Andrew from birth.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Education
1 CONT
1 CONT The Prince Andrew received his early education at home, before attending Heatherdown Preparatory School, near Ascot, Berkshire. In September 1973, he followed his father, The Duke of Edinburgh, and his elder brother, HRH Prince of
1 CONC  Wales, to Gordonstoun, in Morayshire, Scotland. From January to June 1977, Prince Andrew went on an exchange programme to Lakefield College School, Ontario, Canada. He left Gordonstoun in July 1979 with A-levels in English, histo
1 CONC ry and economics, and political science. Unlike his brothers, Prince Andrew did not attend university, choosing to enter the Britannia Royal Naval College at Dartmouth instead.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Military Service
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Royal Navy
1 CONT
1 CONT In November 1978 it was announced that Prince Andrew intended joining the Royal Navy in the following year. In December he underwent various sporting tests and examinations at the RAF Officers' Selection Centre, RAF Biggin Hill. F
1 CONC urther tests and interviews were conducted at HMS Daedalus, Lee-on-Solent, and interviews at the Admiralty Interview Board, HMS Sultan. In March and April 1979 he was with the Royal Naval College Flight, and underwent pilot gradin
1 CONC g. He was accepted as a trainee helicopter pilot and signed on for 12 years from 11 May 1979.
1 CONT British Royal Family
1 CONT
1 CONT HM The Queen
1 CONT HRH The Duke of Edinburgh
1 CONT
1 CONT     * HRH The Prince of Wales
1 CONT       HRH The Duchess of Cornwall
1 CONT           o HRH Prince William of Wales
1 CONT           o HRH Prince Henry of Wales
1 CONT     * HRH The Duke of York
1 CONT           o HRH Princess Beatrice of York
1 CONT           o HRH Princess Eugenie of York
1 CONT     * HRH The Earl of Wessex
1 CONT       HRH The Countess of Wessex
1 CONT           o Viscount Severn
1 CONT           o Lady Louise Windsor
1 CONT     * HRH The Princess Royal
1 CONT
1 CONT     * HRH The Duke of Gloucester
1 CONT       HRH The Duchess of Gloucester
1 CONT     * HRH The Duke of Kent
1 CONT       HRH The Duchess of Kent
1 CONT     * HRH Prince Michael of Kent
1 CONT       HRH Princess Michael of Kent
1 CONT     * HRH Princess Alexandra
1 CONT
1 CONT v • d • e
1 CONT
1 CONT Prince Andrew was appointed a Midshipman on 1 September 1979 and entered Dartmouth on 12 September. He also undertook the Royal Marine 'Green Beret' course during 1980.
1 CONT
1 CONT After passing out of Dartmouth, Prince Andrew went on to elementary flying training with the Royal Air Force at RAF Leeming, Yorkshire, and later basic flying training with the Royal Navy at Culdrose, Cornwall, where he learned to
1 CONC  fly the Gazelle helicopter.
1 CONT
1 CONT Prince Andrew later converted onto the Sea King helicopter and conducting operational flying training. In 1982, he joined his first front-line unit 820 Naval Air Squadron, serving aboard the aircraft carrier, HMS Invincible.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Falklands War (1982)
1 CONT
1 CONT On 2 April 1982 the British colony of the Falkland Islands was invaded by Argentina, an event that led to the Falklands War. Prince Andrew's ship, HMS Invincible was one of two operational aircraft carriers available to the Royal 
1 CONC Navy, and as such was to play a major role in the Royal Navy taskforce being assembled to sail south to retake the islands.
1 CONT
1 CONT At first the British government were apprehensive of allowing Prince Andrew to remain on Invincible, and wished to move him to a desk job. The prospect of the son of the Queen being killed in action was a possibility, and the gove
1 CONC rnment wished to avoid such a circumstance. However, the Queen insisted that Prince Andrew be allowed to remain with his ship, and so he joined the Invincible as it sailed south, as a Sea King helicopter co-pilot.
1 CONT
1 CONT Throughout the conflict Prince Andrew flew on various missions, including Anti-Submarine Warfare (ASW) and Anti-Surface Warfare search (ASuW), Exocet missile decoy, as well as other missions. He also helped in casualty evacuation,
1 CONC  transport and was involved in some highly dangerous Search and Air Rescue (SAR) missions. When the conflict ended, and Invincible returned to Portsmouth, the Queen and Prince Philip joined other families of the crew to welcome th
1 CONC e vessel home.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Prince remained with HMS Invincible, with brief assignments to the carrier HMS Illustrious, Culdrose, and the Joint Services School of Intelligence, Ashford, Kent, until 1983.
1 CONT
1 CONT In Commander 'Sharkey' Ward's book Sea Harrier Over the Falklands, the Prince is described as "an excellent pilot and a very promising officer", by the fighter ace.
1 CONT
1 CONT Since the war, Prince Andrew has laid a wreath at the Cenotaph each year on Remembrance Sunday to commemorate the South Atlantic campaign.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Career Naval Officer (1979-2001)
1 CONT
1 CONT In late 1983, Prince Andrew transferred to RNAS Portland and learned to fly the Lynx helicopter. On promotion to Lieutenant on 1 February 1984, The Queen appointed him a Personal Aide-de-camp.
1 CONT
1 CONT Later service saw the Prince aboard HMS Brazen as Flight Pilot, 1984-1986 (including deployment to the Mediterranean as part of NAVOCFORMED).
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1986, he undertook the Lieutenants' Greenwich Staff Course at the Royal Naval College, Greenwich. On 23 October 1986 he transferred to the General List. He then did a four month helicopter warfare instructors' course at RNAS Ye
1 CONC ovilton (HMS Heron), and served from February 1987 to April 1988 as a helicopter warfare officer in 702 Naval Air Squadron, RNAS Portland (HMS Osprey).
1 CONT
1 CONT He served on HMS Edinburgh as an Officer of the Watch and Assistant Navigating Officer 1988-1989, including a six month deployment as part of Exercise Outback 88 to the Far East.
1 CONT
1 CONT Prince Andrew, now the Duke of York, served as flight commander and pilot of the Lynx HAS3 of HMS Campbeltown 1989 to 1991. During this assignment he served as Force Aviation Officer to STANAVFORLANT when HMS Campbeltown was flags
1 CONC hip of the NATO force in the North Atlantic 1990-1991.
1 CONT
1 CONT Prince Andrew passed the squadron command examination on 16 July 1991. In 1992, he attended the Staff College, Camberley and completed the army staff course.
1 CONT
1 CONT He became a Lieutenant-Commander on 1 February 1992, and passed the ship command examination on 12 March 1992. Between 1993 and 1994, he commanded the Hunt Class Minehunter HMS Cottesmore, based at Portsmouth. Curiously, one of th
1 CONC e officers of the ship was Sub-Lieutenant JW Gold, a Special Duties (commissioned from the ranks) electronic warfare officer. It is not clear whether this had any specific implications, however such highly specialised officers are
1 CONC  usually deployed to major warships which contain a strategic intelligence suite, and his presence on a mine countermeasures vessel was unusual.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1995 and 1996, the Duke of York was Senior Pilot of 815 Naval Air Squadron, a unit with over 80 aircrew, 420 ground staff and 41 helicopters - the largest flying unit in the Fleet Air Arm. The main role of the Senior Pilot was 
1 CONC to supervise flying standards to guarantee an effective operational capability.
1 CONT
1 CONT Prince Andrew was made a Commander on 27 April 1999. He finished his naval career at the Ministry of Defence in London 1999 to 2001 as an officer of the Diplomatic Directorate of the Naval Staff.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was released from the Active List of the Royal Navy in July 2001. In July 2005, he was promoted to Captain, Royal Navy. Unusually, although a former career officer, His Royal Highness was made an Honorary Captain, rather than g
1 CONC iven the substantive rank of Captain, as was traditional and might have been expected.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Canadian military
1 CONT
1 CONT Andrew is the Colonel-in-Chief of a number of Canadian Forces regiments, travelling to Canada to frequently undertake duties related to these roles. Rick Peters, commanding officer of the Royal Highland Fusiliers of Canada stated:
1 CONC  "[Andrew]'s very well informed on Canadian military methods."[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriage
1 CONT
1 CONT On 23 July 1986, Prince Andrew married Sarah Ferguson, the second daughter of the late Major Ronald Ferguson and his former wife, the late Susan (Mrs. Hector) Barrantes, at Westminster Abbey. On the day of his wedding, the Queen c
1 CONC reated him Duke of York, Earl of Inverness and Baron Killyleagh - titles previously held by both his maternal great-grandfather and grandfather, King George V and King George VI. Prince Andrew and Sarah Ferguson are close in age b
1 CONC eing only a few months apart. They had known each other since childhood and would meet occasionally at Polo matches, a sport in which both families took a keen interest. They became re-acquainted with one another during the Ascot 
1 CONC racing season of 1985.
1 CONT
1 CONT Together the Duke and Duchess of York have two children:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Princess Beatrice of York (born 8 August 1988)
1 CONT     * Princess Eugenie of York (born 23 March 1990).
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Divorce
1 CONT This article needs additional citations for verification.
1 CONT Please help improve this article by adding reliable references. Unsourced material may be challenged and removed. (December 2006)
1 CONT
1 CONT The Duke and Duchess of York appeared to have a happy marriage and presented a united front during the late 1980s. The Duchess was seen as a breath of fresh air in the context of the stuffy royal protocol surrounding the Royal Fam
1 CONC ily. However, underneath there were problems in the marriage, largely involving the fact that the Duke of York was frequently away on Navy business and the relentless, often critical, media attention focussed on the Duchess.
1 CONT
1 CONT On 19 March 1992, the Duke and Duchess announced their plans to separate. It was an amicable parting, and the decision was made by the Duke and Duchess alone. Some months after their separation in 1992, pictures of the Duchess app
1 CONC eared in the British tabloid press, showing her in compromising positions with her financial advisor, the American John Bryan. This effectively ended any hopes of a reconciliation and did nothing to diminish the extent to which th
1 CONC e Duchess was being targeted by the tabloid media.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Duke and Duchess of York divorced on 30 May 1996.[3] Their divorce was friendly, and it was stipulated that custody of the two princesses would be shared between them. The Duchess of York was no longer Her Royal Highness, but 
1 CONC instead became styled, as the ex-wife of a peer, as Sarah, Duchess of York. The close, now-platonic, friendship between the Duke and Duchess has continued, and the Duchess lived on and off in the Duke's home at Sunninghill Park un
1 CONC til 2004 when the Duke moved to Royal Lodge, the former home of the Queen Mother. In 2007 the Duchess purchased Dolphin House, a mansion which lies directly beside Royal Lodge, effectively rendering the Duke and Duchess of York as
1 CONC  next door neighbours.It is said, and speculated, by many that she bought this house so she could be closer to her daughters, who reside with the Duke.
1 CONT
1 CONT The prince speaks warmly of his ex-wife. "We have managed to work together to bring our children up in a way that few others have been able to do and I am extremely grateful to be able to do that."[4]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Current role
1 CONT This article needs additional citations for verification.
1 CONT Please help improve this article by adding reliable references. Unsourced material may be challenged and removed. (December 2006)
1 CONT
1 CONT Currently the Duke of York works for the Department of Trade and Industry as the United Kingdom's Special Representative for International Trade and Investment. He took over from Prince Edward, Duke of Kent in this role. The role 
1 CONC involves the Duke representing the UK at various trade fairs and conferences all over the world.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Duke is also a keen golfer and is Captain of the Royal and Ancient Golf Club of St. Andrews and also a member of the Royal Burgess Golfing Society. This is not without controversy and the Duke has been criticised for using the
1 CONC  Queen's Flight for transport to various golfing functions.
1 CONT
1 CONT Since 2004, Prince Andrew has lived at Royal Lodge, Windsor Great Park, the former country home of his grandmother, Queen Elizabeth the Queen Mother. Prior to that he lived at Sunninghill Park. The Duke of York maintains an office
1 CONC  at Buckingham Palace.
1 CONT
1 CONT For the General Assembly of the Church of Scotland in May 2007, the Duke served as Lord High Commissioner. The role granted him, in Scotland, whilst the assembly was in session, precedence immediately after his parents, and the st
1 CONC yle His Grace The Lord High Commissioner.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Titles, styles, honours and arms
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Titles and styles
1 CONT Styles of
1 CONT HRH The Duke of York
1 CONT Reference style His Royal Highness
1 CONT Spoken style Your Royal Highness
1 CONT Alternative style Sir
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 19 February 1960 – 23 July 1986: His Royal Highness The Prince Andrew
1 CONT     * 23 July 1986 – present: His Royal Highness The Duke of York
1 CONT           o in Scotland: May 2007: His Grace The Lord High Commissioner
1 CONT
1 CONT Prince Andrew's current full style is His Royal Highness The Prince Andrew Albert Christian Edward, Duke of York, Earl of Inverness, Baron Killyleagh, Knight Companion of the Most Noble Order of the Garter, Knight Commander of the
1 CONC  Royal Victorian Order, Personal Aide-de-Camp to The Queen, South Atlantic Medal, Queen Elizabeth II Silver Jubilee Medal, Queen Elizabeth II Golden Jubilee Medal, Canadian Forces Decoration, New Zealand 1990 Commemoration Medal
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Honours
1 CONT
1 CONT British Honours
1 CONT
1 CONT     * KG: Knight of the Garter, 23 April 2006
1 CONT     * KCVO: Knight Commander of the Royal Victorian Order, 3 June 2003
1 CONT           o CVO: Commander of the Royal Victorian Order, 19 December 1979
