1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early life
1 CONT
1 CONT Princess Anne Elizabeth Alice Louise was born on 15 August 1950 at Clarence House, London. Her father is Prince Philip, Duke of Edinburgh, the son of Prince Andrew of Greece and Denmark and the former Princess Alice of Battenberg.
1 CONC  Her mother is Queen Elizabeth II, Queen of the United Kingdom, the eldest daughter of King George VI and Queen Elizabeth, The Queen Mother.
1 CONT
1 CONT She was baptised in the Music Room of Buckingham Palace on 21 October 1950 by Cyril Garbett, Archbishop of York. Her godparents were: The Earl Mountbatten of Burma, Andrew Elphinstone, Queen Elizabeth, The Queen Mother, Princess A
1 CONC ndrew of Greece and Denmark and Princess Gottfried of Hohenlohe-Langenburg.
1 CONT
1 CONT Shortly before the birth of her elder brother, Prince Charles, in 1948, their grandfather King George VI issued Letters Patent granting the titular dignity of Prince or Princess of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern 
1 CONC Ireland and the style Royal Highness to any children born to the Duke and Duchess of Edinburgh. Thus, from birth, Princess Anne was styled Her Royal Highness Princess Anne of Edinburgh. Had it not been for these letters patent she
1 CONC  would have been known as 'Lady Anne Mountbatten' until her mother became Queen in 1952.
1 CONT
1 CONT Princess Anne was educated in Buckingham Palace and then at Benenden School, a public boarding school in Kent.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] The Princess Anne
1 CONT Medal record
1 CONT Equestrian
1 CONT European Championships
1 CONT Gold 1971 Burghley Individual eventing
1 CONT Silver 1975 Luhmuhlen Team eventing
1 CONT Silver 1975 Luhmuhlen Individual eventing
1 CONT
1 CONT On 6 February 1952, when Anne's grandfather, George VI, died, her mother ascended the throne as Elizabeth II. Anne was now styled Her Royal Highness The Princess Anne. Given her young age, she did not attend her mother's coronatio
1 CONC n. Princess Anne began to undertake royal and official duties as a teenager in the late 1960s.
1 CONT
1 CONT Anne has always shown a keen interest in horses, and equine pursuits have been an important part of her life. At the age of 21, she won the individual title at the European Eventing Championship held at Burghley and was voted BBC'
1 CONC s BBC Sports Personality of the Year in 1971. Her daughter Zara Phillips would win the same award 35 years later, on 10 December 2006. For over five years she competed with the British eventing team, winning a silver medal in both
1 CONC  individual and team disciplines in the 1975 European Eventing Championships held in Germany riding the home-bred Doublet. The following year she participated in the 1976 Montreal Olympic Games as a member of the British team, rid
1 CONC ing the Queen's horse Goodwill. On 5 February 1987, she appeared as a panellist on the 200th edition of the BBC TV panel game A Question of Sport, becoming the first titled British royal to appear as a contestant on a TV quiz show
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT British Royal Family
1 CONT
1 CONT HM The Queen
